{"level":30,"time":1776951732524,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"3.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012sroDCTwZuWcYP1LDijY3L","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Austin Energy Service and ROW Utility Coordination\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers all coordination requirements with Austin Energy (for electrical service, street lighting, and moonlight tower compliance) and with other ROW utility stakeholders (AULCC, Gattan, telecommunications, dry utilities, and CIP) before a site plan can be approved. It also captures the administrative closeout requirements — ROW utility coordination case closure, the Additional Review Acknowledgement table, street name reservations, and addressing approvals — that gate final ROW Review approval.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Austin Energy Service Coordination.** Austin Energy (AE) is the City of Austin's municipal electric utility serving both the city and portions of the surrounding area. All new development or service modification requires submission of an Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) to the appropriate AE Design business unit along with required documents to obtain an electrical design (AEDC 1.4.2.1). For service ≤350 amps single-phase or ≤225 amps three-phase (non-network), the Service-Only process applies; for larger loads or any service within the downtown Network Area, the Design Services process applies and full AE Design involvement is required (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4). Normally, the ESPA is submitted after DSD approves the site plan and must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A). A Building Service Planning Application (BSPA) — distinct from the ESPA — is required for building permits when the exterior of a structure is modified. For locations where it is uncertain which utility serves the site, an Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirms whether the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area (AEDC 1.4.1). AE Design has sole authority over available service characteristics and voltage at any given location (AEDC 1.3.2). AE owns electric facilities in the public ROW; customers own facilities from the point of service toward the building, except for the meter (AEDC 1.3.5). Before AE begins installation, the customer must grant AE an electrical easement using standard City forms signed by the property owner (AEDC 1.3.6).\n    10→\n    11→**Land Development Review Requirements (AEDC 1.16.0).** All land development applications submitted for Austin Energy review must include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) showing: all public and private easements; all existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, and transformers); permanent structures with footprint and height; final topology (grades, floodplains, detention ponds); and property lines with building setbacks. Plans must demonstrate sufficient clearances for existing and proposed electric facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(2)), dedicate additional easements if insufficient (at owner's expense, with metes and bounds descriptions), demonstrate that the development will not limit AE personnel access to current and proposed facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(4)), identify one point of service and one service voltage (exceptions require AE approval), and show approximate location and space for electric facilities with projected load. When a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, AEDC 1.16.0(8) requires specific plat notes: (i) AE tree pruning rights; (ii) provision for additional easements and access; (iii) owner erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection responsibilities; (iv) clearance maintenance obligation; (v) restriction on permanent structures that would violate NESC clearances within the transmission easement; (vi) 24-hour AE access across the property to transmission easements; (vii) all roads and driveways in the transmission easement built to support ≥48,000 lb tandem axle loads; (viii) 48-hour notice to AE before construction or grading within a transmission easement. Land within transmission easements may not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication (AEDC 1.16.0(9)).\n    12→\n    13→**Required Austin Energy Plan Notes.** All consolidated site plan applications must include four Standard Austin Energy Notes verbatim — changing the required text results in rejection: (1) Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery, and other obstructions to keep easements clear, and AE will comply with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B; (2) the owner/developer must provide AE with any easement and/or access required (beyond those indicated) for overhead and underground electric facilities, and such facilities shall not cause the site to be out of compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8; (3) the owner is responsible for temporary erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and initial tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of proposed overhead electrical facilities, and AE's work area must be included within the limits of construction; (4) the property owner is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines; AE will not render service unless required clearances are maintained; failure costs are charged to the owner. In addition to these four standard notes, AE's land development review process under AEDC 1.16.0(7) requires site-specific plan notes: (a) a note identifying the site as lying within the Austin Energy service area, and (b) a note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current AE Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts.\n    14→\n    15→**Street Lighting.** AE develops a complete lighting installation plan for new residential and commercial developments. Developer conduit and infrastructure responsibilities vary by location type: for new residential subdivisions within city limits, the developer provides conduit, pull string, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs standard poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.4); for commercial areas within city limits, the developer pays all installation costs and provides conduit, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.6); outside city limits in the AE service area, the developer bears all costs including monthly energy charges (AEDC 1.6.5, 1.6.7). All street lighting must meet IESNA spacing and illumination standards. Non-standard poles require developer/AE collaboration and the developer pays the cost difference. For projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary — bounded by Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez St (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W) — Great Streets Chapter 6 lighting standards apply: 8 poles per block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures). All pole placement and spacing requires prior AE Design coordination and, where applicable, Urban Design coordination.\n    16→\n    17→**AULCC and ROW Utility Coordination.** The Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee (AULCC) — referred to as \"ULCC\" in the UCM — administers the ROW utility coordination process. Per UCM 3.2.0, all projects that impact infrastructure located in the public right-of-way must be processed through the AULCC before proceeding to the permitting and construction phase. AULCC review is triggered when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) **300 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW anywhere in the city; (2) **25 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within the **Downtown Austin Project Coordination Zone (DAPCZ)** — roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east (IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street), Oltorf Street/Barton Skyway to the south, Loop 1 (MoPac) to the west, and Enfield Road/Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd to the north; the formal metes-and-bounds boundary is in Code § 14-11-161(7) and includes the boundary streets; (3) **any utility pole relocation**, regardless of linear footage, anywhere in the city; or (4) **water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets** (UCM 2.9.1.B.1). Projects that do not meet any of these thresholds are not subject to AULCC review. Successful AULCC coordination results in an AULCC Completeness Letter (clearance letter) that must be on file before the ROW Review can be marked Approved. The ROW utility coordination case must be closed in the site plan case management system before approval can be issued. Additional external utility clearances required through the ROW coordination process include: Gattan (a specific utility provider — its service territory is not described in Bureau code files; applicability is determined by the presence of Gattan-labeled facilities on utility plan sheets or from AULCC correspondence; requires human verification if unknown), telecommunications providers, dry utility providers, and any active CIP project manager for projects in areas with active capital improvement work.\n    18→\n    19→**Moonlight Towers.** Moonlight towers are specific, fixed-location, City-owned steel lattice towers with supporting guy wires, historically used for citywide arc lighting and concentrated in downtown and central Austin. Two separate regulatory thresholds apply: **(1) 100-foot permit threshold (COA § 14-11-201):** no construction activity may occur within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower without a permit under COA Division 4, Chapter 14-11; construction on private property requires application to the building official; construction in the ROW requires application to the director; the guy wire buffer applies independently from the tower base, so a project can be more than 100 feet from the base but still require a permit if within 100 feet of a guy wire. **(2) 25-foot ROW prohibition (COA § 14-11-205):** no facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; a permittee must also obtain a temporary use of ROW permit to occupy the ROW near a tower. A 24-hour advance notification to Austin Energy before construction near a moonlight tower is an AE operational practice but is not codified in COA §§ 14-11-201 through 14-11-205. Moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files — the review agent cannot determine proximity from plans alone. For projects in downtown or central Austin, moonlight tower proximity items should be flagged for human verification unless plans affirmatively note the absence of nearby towers.\n    20→\n    21→**Administrative Closeout.** Final site plan approval requires: the AULCC Completeness Letter on file; the Additional Review Acknowledgement table completed on the cover sheet or transmittal listing all applicable external reviews and permits (TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, and Planning and Development Review Department approvals) per UCM 2.5.1.F; all proposed street names, including names for private drives, reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before approval; and an addressing plan approved by Address Management Services. **Receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan** — applicants frequently confuse these two documents and reviewers must confirm the addressing plan is approved, not just that an AVL was issued.\n    22→\n    23→**Key Thresholds:**\n    24→- **ESPA service type:** ≤350 A single-phase or ≤225 A three-phase (non-network) → Service-Only process; >350 A single-phase or >225 A three-phase, or any Network Area service → Design Services process with full AE Design involvement (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4)\n    25→- **Continuous load:** >320 A must upgrade to instrument-rated CT service (AEDC 1.4.0)\n    26→- **Moonlight tower permit:** Construction within 100 feet of a tower or its guy wires requires permit under COA § 14-11-201\n    27→- **Moonlight tower ROW prohibition:** No facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower (COA § 14-11-205)\n    28→- **AULCC trigger (citywide):** ≥300 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation (UCM 3.2.0)\n    29→- **AULCC trigger (DAPCZ):** ≥25 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation within DAPCZ (UCM 3.2.0)\n    30→- **AULCC trigger (pole):** Any utility pole relocation, anywhere in the city (UCM 3.2.0)\n    31→- **Great Streets lighting boundary:** MLK Blvd (N) / Lady Bird Lake–Cesar Chavez (S) / IH-35 (E) / Lamar Blvd (W) — 8 poles/block, 24-ft street / 12-ft pedestrian mounting heights (Great Streets Ch. 6)\n    32→\n    33→**Exceptions:**\n    34→- ESPA submission normally occurs after DSD site plan approval, not before; ESPA must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A)\n    35→- Electric Service Availability Letter is required only where there is a question as to which utility serves the site; not required for all projects in the established AE service area (AEDC 1.4.1)\n    36→- Non-standard street light poles are permitted with developer/AE collaboration and developer payment of cost difference\n    37→- Projects below all AULCC thresholds (no pole relocations, <300 LF citywide, <25 LF in DAPCZ, no main assignments) are exempt from AULCC review\n    38→\n    39→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    40→- Citing LDC § 25-9-173 for the electric service capacity/availability letter requirement — this section does not exist in the current LDC (chapter numbering jumps from 164 to 191 in that division); the correct authority is AEDC 1.4.1 and AEDC 1.3.2\n    41→- Citing COA § 14-11-205 for the 100-foot moonlight tower permit requirement — § 14-11-205 governs only the 25-foot ROW installation prohibition; the 100-foot permit requirement is in COA § 14-11-201\n    42→- Treating AULCC clearance as an \"all sites\" requirement — AULCC review is only triggered when one of the four specific thresholds (linear footage, DAPCZ linear footage, pole relocation, or main assignment) is met; universal application generates false deficiencies for small infill projects\n    43→- Accepting an Address Validation Letter (AVL) as an approved addressing plan — these are distinct documents; the addressing plan must be separately approved\n    44→\n    45→## Code References\n    46→\n    47→- AEDC 1.3.0 — Basic requirements of electric distribution service (customer vs. AE facility ownership, easement grants, access requirements, truck access dimensions)\n    48→- AEDC 1.4.0 — Requesting and obtaining electric distribution service; ESPA submission process and service type thresholds\n    49→- AEDC 1.4.1 — Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirming service area\n    50→- AEDC 1.6.0 — Street lighting standards, developer responsibilities, and IESNA requirements\n    51→- AEDC 1.16.0 — Land development review application requirements for Austin Energy (AutoCAD drawing, clearance demonstration, easement sufficiency, access, point of service, plat notes for transmission easements)\n    52→- Addressing Standards, Introduction — Address Management Services (AMS) is the addressing authority for the City of Austin and Travis County; addressing review occurs during subdivision and site plan review processes; approval required for permitting\n    53→- Addressing Standards, Streets — Private drive, access easement, and street naming criteria; all proposed street names must be reviewed by AMS and reserved before use; named driveways and easements must adhere to Austin and Travis County Street Name Standards\n    54→- COA § 14-11-201 — Permit required for construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or its guy wires\n    55→- COA § 14-11-205 — No facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; temporary use of ROW permit required\n    56→- Great Streets Chapter 6 — Street lighting design standards for projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), Lamar Blvd (W)): 8 poles/block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures)\n    57→- UCM 2.5.1.F — Additional authorizations required for final plan approval (TxDOT, railroad, gas company, TCEQ, water districts, etc.)\n    58→- UCM 3.2.0 — AULCC: designation as ROW utility coordination body; requirement that all projects impacting ROW infrastructure be processed through the AULCC\n    59→- UCM 3.4.0 — Utility coordination process phases (Pre-Design, Design, Final Design, Utility Adjustment)\n    60→- UCM 3.8.0 — Pre-construction and construction meeting requirements\n    61→- General Site Plan Notes — Standard Austin Energy Notes — Four mandatory notes required verbatim on all consolidated site plan applications\n    62→\n    63→## Documents to Review\n    64→\n    65→- **Site/Utility Plan Sheets** — Verify Austin Energy service area note, developer responsibility note for conduit/infrastructure, street light placement and dimensions, signal cabinet representations, utility conflicts in ROW (street lights vs. gas lines, storm inlets, etc.)\n    66→- **Plan Notes Block / General Notes Sheet** — Check for all four Standard Austin Energy Notes (verbatim), moonlight tower permit and notification language, preconstruction meeting requirement note, developer responsibility note for AE conduit standards\n    67→- **Cover Sheet or Transmittal** — Confirm Additional Review Acknowledgement table is complete with all external reviews/approvals (TxDOT, railroad, gas, TCEQ, water districts, etc.) per UCM 2.5.1.F\n    68→- **Approval/Clearance Letters on File** — ESPA approval from Austin Energy, AEU clearance letter, Electric Service Availability Letter (where service provider was uncertain), AULCC Completeness Letter, Gattan clearance, telecommunications provider clearances, dry utility clearances, CIP clearance\n    69→- **ROW Review Case Status** — Confirm utility coordination case is closed before ROW Review can be marked Approved in the Site Plan case\n    70→- **Addressing Records** — Confirm addressing plan has been approved and all street names (including private drives) are reserved with 911 Addressing before site plan approval\n    71→- **Easement Documents** — Verify electrical easements are dedicated using standard City forms where AE facilities are proposed on private property\n    72→\n    73→## Checklist Items\n    74→\n    75→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    76→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    77→| EL-3.1 | Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) not submitted to Austin Energy for review and approval, or ESPA approval not documented in the plan set | AEDC 1.4.2.1 | Only when new or modified electrical service is required. Check: project scope for new construction or electrical service upgrades |\n    78→| EL-3.2 | Austin Energy Utility (AEU) clearance or approval not obtained or not documented | Reviewer Convention | All sites served by Austin Energy. Check: submittal package for AEU clearance letter |\n    79→| EL-3.3 | Electric Service Availability Letter from Austin Energy not provided confirming that the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area; or for locations where the serving utility is uncertain, AE Public Involvement has not been contacted to obtain this letter | AEDC 1.4.1; AEDC 1.3.2 | Only when new electrical service is being obtained from Austin Energy and there is a question as to which electric utility serves the location. Check: project location relative to established AE service territory and ESPA documentation |\n    80→| EL-3.4 | Site plan does not include a note stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area | AEDC 1.16.0(7) | All sites within the Austin Energy service area. Check: plan notes sheet for AE service area note |\n    81→| EL-3.5 | Plans do not include the required developer responsibility note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts | AEDC 1.16.0(7); AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when new Austin Energy electrical infrastructure (conduit, conductors, pull-boxes, foundations) is required. Check: ESPA or electrical plans for new service |\n    82→| EL-3.6 | One or more of the four required Standard Austin Energy Notes is missing or altered from the required verbatim text: (1) AE tree pruning rights in easements; (2) owner/developer obligation to provide additional easements or access for AE facilities; (3) owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines; (4) owner responsibility for maintaining NESC/OSHA/City/state clearances near overhead power lines | General Site Plan Notes — Standard AE Notes | All consolidated site plan applications. Check: general notes sheet for all four required notes verbatim; changing the required text results in rejection |\n    83→| EL-3.7 | Austin Energy approval not obtained for electrical transformer and pull box design prior to construction | AEDC 1.4.2.4 | Only when new or relocated transformers or pull boxes are proposed. Check: utility plan sheets for proposed AE equipment |\n    84→| EL-3.8 | New driveway or site access improvement conflicts with existing Austin Energy power pole guy anchor support and coordination with Austin Energy has not been completed | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when new driveways or access improvements are proposed near existing power poles with guy anchors. Check: utility plan sheets for pole anchor locations adjacent to proposed driveways |\n    85→| EL-3.9 | Plans do not include a note that a preconstruction meeting with Austin Energy is required prior to starting demolition | UCM 3.8.0 | Only when demolition is proposed. Check: demolition plan or general notes for preconstruction meeting requirement |\n    86→| EL-3.10 | Final layout solution and electrical easement recording have not been coordinated with Austin Energy before approval sign-off; easement documents using standard City forms have not been returned to AE before AE begins installation | AEDC 1.16.0(3); AEDC 1.3.6 | Only when new electrical easements are required for proposed AE facilities on private property. Check: easement exhibits and AE design correspondence |\n    87→| EL-3.11 | Land development application submitted to Austin Energy does not include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) with all required content: public/private easements, existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, transformers), permanent structures with footprint and height, final topology, and property lines with building setbacks | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when submitting a land development application to Austin Energy for review. Check: AE submittal package for electronic drawing format compliance and required content |\n    88→| EL-3.12 | Plans do not demonstrate that the proposed development will not limit Austin Energy personnel access to current and proposed electric facilities (e.g., no access path shown to proposed transformers, pull boxes, or overhead line equipment; no truck access dimensions provided for AE equipment areas) | AEDC 1.16.0(4) | Only when development is adjacent to existing or proposed AE electric facilities. Check: site plan and grading plan for AE equipment access routes; verify minimum 12-ft width and 16-ft vertical clearance for truck access per AEDC 1.3.16 |\n    89→| EL-3.13 | Plan notes do not state that a permit from Austin Energy is required for any construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower, per COA § 14-11-201 | COA § 14-11-201 | Only when a moonlight tower or its guy wires is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: utility base map and Austin Energy moonlight tower records; if project is in downtown or central Austin, flag for human verification of proximity — moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files |\n    90→| EL-3.14 | Plan notes do not document Austin Energy's advance notification requirement before construction commences within 100 feet of a moonlight tower; this is an Austin Energy operational practice (not codified in COA § 14-11-205) and plans near moonlight towers should include coordination notes and current AE contact information for pre-construction notification | Austin Energy operational requirement | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: same proximity verification as EL-3.13; note that any phone number shown on plans should be confirmed current with Austin Energy |\n    91→| EL-3.15 | Plans propose installing a facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower, which is prohibited; additionally, no temporary use of ROW permit has been obtained or referenced for ROW occupancy in the vicinity of the tower | COA § 14-11-205 | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area and ROW facility installations are proposed. Check: proposed ROW facility locations vs. any annotated or known moonlight tower locations; flag for human verification if project is in downtown or central Austin and plans show ROW work within potential tower proximity |\n    92→| EL-3.16 | Proposed street light placement in the ROW conflicts with an existing utility (e.g., gas line, storm sewer) and the conflict has not been resolved on the plans | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in or adjacent to the ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for horizontal conflicts between proposed light poles and mapped utilities |\n    93→| EL-3.17 | Traffic signal cabinets shown on plans not updated to reflect current field conditions at affected intersections | Reviewer Convention | Only when traffic signal cabinets are shown on plans. Check: utility or streetscape plan sheets for signal cabinet locations vs. current field layout |\n    94→| EL-3.18 | Street light pole spacing and placement not coordinated with Austin Energy; pole locations not confirmed with AE Design (and with Urban Design where applicable) | AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for evidence of AE coordination on pole spacing |\n    95→| EL-3.19 | Electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans do not extend to all required utility infrastructure locations within the project area; plan coverage ends before reaching the full extent of proposed utility work along the project frontage | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans are part of the plan set and utility infrastructure extends across the project frontage. Check: plan coverage limits vs. full extent of proposed utility work |\n    96→| EL-3.20 | Street lighting labels on plan sheets are duplicated or incorrectly applied (e.g., extra Great Streets light label assigned to a tree well or non-light feature) | Great Streets Chapter 6 | Only when the site is within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (bounded by MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W)) and Great Streets lighting standards apply. Check: confirm project is within Great Streets boundary, then review streetscape plan sheets for label accuracy and duplication |\n    97→| EL-3.21 | Dimensions between street light poles not provided on plan sheets | AEDC 1.16.0(1); AEDC 1.6.0 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for dimensional callouts between pole locations |\n    98→| EL-3.22 | Street light pole placement conflicts with a storm drain inlet or other subsurface stormwater infrastructure, requiring pole relocation | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in close proximity to stormwater inlets. Check: utility plan sheets overlaying proposed pole locations with storm sewer infrastructure |\n    99→| EL-3.23 | AULCC (Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee) clearance not obtained before site plan approval; AULCC Completeness Letter not on file | UCM 3.2.0 | Only when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) 300 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW (citywide); (2) 25 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within DAPCZ (roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east — note: IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary only in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street — Oltorf/Barton Skyway (S), Loop 1/MoPac (W), Enfield Road/MLK Blvd (N); formal metes-and-bounds boundary in Code § 14-11-161(7); boundary streets included); (3) any utility pole relocation, regardless of linear footage; (4) water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets. Check: measure total linear footage of proposed ROW utility work on utility plan sheets; check for any pole relocations; determine if project is within DAPCZ |\n   100→| EL-3.24 | Gattan clearance or approval not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when Gattan infrastructure is present or affected. Check: utility plan sheets for Gattan-labeled facilities in the project area; if unknown, flag for human verification via AULCC correspondence |\n   101→| EL-3.25 | Telecommunications provider clearances not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when telecommunications infrastructure is affected by or located within the project area or ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for telecom facilities |\n   102→| EL-3.26 | Dry utility clearances not obtained from all applicable providers | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when dry utilities (electric, gas, telecom) are present in the ROW or affected by site work. Check: utility plan sheets for dry utility facilities |\n   103→| EL-3.27 | CIP (Capital Improvement Projects) clearance not obtained; a CIP project manager has not been identified or confirmed | Reviewer Convention | Only when a CIP project is active in or adjacent to the project area. Check: CIP project lookup for the project vicinity — requires human verification via City of Austin CIP project management records |\n   104→| EL-3.28 | ROW utility coordination case not closed; ROW Review cannot be marked Approved in the Site Plan case until the utility coordination case is complete | UCM 3.2.0 | All sites with proposed ROW work. Check: ROW Review case status in site plan case management system |\n   105→| EL-3.29 | Additional Review Acknowledgement table not completed on plans with all applicable external reviews, approvals, or permits required for the project (e.g., TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, Planning and Development Review Department approvals) | UCM 2.5.1.F | All sites. Check: cover sheet or transmittal for completed Additional Review Acknowledgement table |\n   106→| EL-3.30 | Proposed street names, including names for private drives, not approved and reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before site plan approval | Addressing Standards, Streets | Only when new streets or private drives are proposed. Check: plan set for any new street names; confirm 911 Addressing reservation status |\n   107→| EL-3.31 | Addressing plan not approved; or consultation meeting with 911 Addressing not scheduled before submittal when no approved addressing plan exists; note that receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan | Addressing Standards, Introduction | Only when new addresses are required for the development. Check: addressing records for an approved addressing plan separate from any AVL issued |\n   108→| EL-3.32 | Transmission easement plat notes missing or incomplete; when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, plans must include all required AEDC 1.16.0(8) plat notes (AE tree pruning rights; additional easement/access provision; erosion control and tree protection responsibilities; clearance maintenance; NESC compliance restrictions on structures; 24-hour AE access to easement; ≥48,000 lb tandem axle road/driveway capacity within easement; 48-hour notice before construction or grading within easement); additionally, land within the transmission easement must not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication per AEDC 1.16.0(9) | AEDC 1.16.0(8); AEDC 1.16.0(9) | Only when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property. Check: easement documents, plat sheets, and plan notes for all eight required AEDC 1.16.0(8) notes; verify that no portion of the transmission easement is labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eedca44c-cd40-46be-bd59-597db07f0864","uuid":"07b52f00-dcae-4ab3-afb6-c0ca920f85e6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/3.md","content":"# Austin Energy Service and ROW Utility Coordination\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers all coordination requirements with Austin Energy (for electrical service, street lighting, and moonlight tower compliance) and with other ROW utility stakeholders (AULCC, Gattan, telecommunications, dry utilities, and CIP) before a site plan can be approved. It also captures the administrative closeout requirements — ROW utility coordination case closure, the Additional Review Acknowledgement table, street name reservations, and addressing approvals — that gate final ROW Review approval.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Austin Energy Service Coordination.** Austin Energy (AE) is the City of Austin's municipal electric utility serving both the city and portions of the surrounding area. All new development or service modification requires submission of an Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) to the appropriate AE Design business unit along with required documents to obtain an electrical design (AEDC 1.4.2.1). For service ≤350 amps single-phase or ≤225 amps three-phase (non-network), the Service-Only process applies; for larger loads or any service within the downtown Network Area, the Design Services process applies and full AE Design involvement is required (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4). Normally, the ESPA is submitted after DSD approves the site plan and must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A). A Building Service Planning Application (BSPA) — distinct from the ESPA — is required for building permits when the exterior of a structure is modified. For locations where it is uncertain which utility serves the site, an Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirms whether the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area (AEDC 1.4.1). AE Design has sole authority over available service characteristics and voltage at any given location (AEDC 1.3.2). AE owns electric facilities in the public ROW; customers own facilities from the point of service toward the building, except for the meter (AEDC 1.3.5). Before AE begins installation, the customer must grant AE an electrical easement using standard City forms signed by the property owner (AEDC 1.3.6).\n\n**Land Development Review Requirements (AEDC 1.16.0).** All land development applications submitted for Austin Energy review must include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) showing: all public and private easements; all existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, and transformers); permanent structures with footprint and height; final topology (grades, floodplains, detention ponds); and property lines with building setbacks. Plans must demonstrate sufficient clearances for existing and proposed electric facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(2)), dedicate additional easements if insufficient (at owner's expense, with metes and bounds descriptions), demonstrate that the development will not limit AE personnel access to current and proposed facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(4)), identify one point of service and one service voltage (exceptions require AE approval), and show approximate location and space for electric facilities with projected load. When a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, AEDC 1.16.0(8) requires specific plat notes: (i) AE tree pruning rights; (ii) provision for additional easements and access; (iii) owner erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection responsibilities; (iv) clearance maintenance obligation; (v) restriction on permanent structures that would violate NESC clearances within the transmission easement; (vi) 24-hour AE access across the property to transmission easements; (vii) all roads and driveways in the transmission easement built to support ≥48,000 lb tandem axle loads; (viii) 48-hour notice to AE before construction or grading within a transmission easement. Land within transmission easements may not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication (AEDC 1.16.0(9)).\n\n**Required Austin Energy Plan Notes.** All consolidated site plan applications must include four Standard Austin Energy Notes verbatim — changing the required text results in rejection: (1) Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery, and other obstructions to keep easements clear, and AE will comply with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B; (2) the owner/developer must provide AE with any easement and/or access required (beyond those indicated) for overhead and underground electric facilities, and such facilities shall not cause the site to be out of compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8; (3) the owner is responsible for temporary erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and initial tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of proposed overhead electrical facilities, and AE's work area must be included within the limits of construction; (4) the property owner is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines; AE will not render service unless required clearances are maintained; failure costs are charged to the owner. In addition to these four standard notes, AE's land development review process under AEDC 1.16.0(7) requires site-specific plan notes: (a) a note identifying the site as lying within the Austin Energy service area, and (b) a note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current AE Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts.\n\n**Street Lighting.** AE develops a complete lighting installation plan for new residential and commercial developments. Developer conduit and infrastructure responsibilities vary by location type: for new residential subdivisions within city limits, the developer provides conduit, pull string, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs standard poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.4); for commercial areas within city limits, the developer pays all installation costs and provides conduit, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.6); outside city limits in the AE service area, the developer bears all costs including monthly energy charges (AEDC 1.6.5, 1.6.7). All street lighting must meet IESNA spacing and illumination standards. Non-standard poles require developer/AE collaboration and the developer pays the cost difference. For projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary — bounded by Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez St (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W) — Great Streets Chapter 6 lighting standards apply: 8 poles per block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures). All pole placement and spacing requires prior AE Design coordination and, where applicable, Urban Design coordination.\n\n**AULCC and ROW Utility Coordination.** The Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee (AULCC) — referred to as \"ULCC\" in the UCM — administers the ROW utility coordination process. Per UCM 3.2.0, all projects that impact infrastructure located in the public right-of-way must be processed through the AULCC before proceeding to the permitting and construction phase. AULCC review is triggered when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) **300 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW anywhere in the city; (2) **25 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within the **Downtown Austin Project Coordination Zone (DAPCZ)** — roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east (IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street), Oltorf Street/Barton Skyway to the south, Loop 1 (MoPac) to the west, and Enfield Road/Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd to the north; the formal metes-and-bounds boundary is in Code § 14-11-161(7) and includes the boundary streets; (3) **any utility pole relocation**, regardless of linear footage, anywhere in the city; or (4) **water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets** (UCM 2.9.1.B.1). Projects that do not meet any of these thresholds are not subject to AULCC review. Successful AULCC coordination results in an AULCC Completeness Letter (clearance letter) that must be on file before the ROW Review can be marked Approved. The ROW utility coordination case must be closed in the site plan case management system before approval can be issued. Additional external utility clearances required through the ROW coordination process include: Gattan (a specific utility provider — its service territory is not described in Bureau code files; applicability is determined by the presence of Gattan-labeled facilities on utility plan sheets or from AULCC correspondence; requires human verification if unknown), telecommunications providers, dry utility providers, and any active CIP project manager for projects in areas with active capital improvement work.\n\n**Moonlight Towers.** Moonlight towers are specific, fixed-location, City-owned steel lattice towers with supporting guy wires, historically used for citywide arc lighting and concentrated in downtown and central Austin. Two separate regulatory thresholds apply: **(1) 100-foot permit threshold (COA § 14-11-201):** no construction activity may occur within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower without a permit under COA Division 4, Chapter 14-11; construction on private property requires application to the building official; construction in the ROW requires application to the director; the guy wire buffer applies independently from the tower base, so a project can be more than 100 feet from the base but still require a permit if within 100 feet of a guy wire. **(2) 25-foot ROW prohibition (COA § 14-11-205):** no facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; a permittee must also obtain a temporary use of ROW permit to occupy the ROW near a tower. A 24-hour advance notification to Austin Energy before construction near a moonlight tower is an AE operational practice but is not codified in COA §§ 14-11-201 through 14-11-205. Moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files — the review agent cannot determine proximity from plans alone. For projects in downtown or central Austin, moonlight tower proximity items should be flagged for human verification unless plans affirmatively note the absence of nearby towers.\n\n**Administrative Closeout.** Final site plan approval requires: the AULCC Completeness Letter on file; the Additional Review Acknowledgement table completed on the cover sheet or transmittal listing all applicable external reviews and permits (TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, and Planning and Development Review Department approvals) per UCM 2.5.1.F; all proposed street names, including names for private drives, reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before approval; and an addressing plan approved by Address Management Services. **Receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan** — applicants frequently confuse these two documents and reviewers must confirm the addressing plan is approved, not just that an AVL was issued.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **ESPA service type:** ≤350 A single-phase or ≤225 A three-phase (non-network) → Service-Only process; >350 A single-phase or >225 A three-phase, or any Network Area service → Design Services process with full AE Design involvement (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4)\n- **Continuous load:** >320 A must upgrade to instrument-rated CT service (AEDC 1.4.0)\n- **Moonlight tower permit:** Construction within 100 feet of a tower or its guy wires requires permit under COA § 14-11-201\n- **Moonlight tower ROW prohibition:** No facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower (COA § 14-11-205)\n- **AULCC trigger (citywide):** ≥300 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **AULCC trigger (DAPCZ):** ≥25 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation within DAPCZ (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **AULCC trigger (pole):** Any utility pole relocation, anywhere in the city (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **Great Streets lighting boundary:** MLK Blvd (N) / Lady Bird Lake–Cesar Chavez (S) / IH-35 (E) / Lamar Blvd (W) — 8 poles/block, 24-ft street / 12-ft pedestrian mounting heights (Great Streets Ch. 6)\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- ESPA submission normally occurs after DSD site plan approval, not before; ESPA must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A)\n- Electric Service Availability Letter is required only where there is a question as to which utility serves the site; not required for all projects in the established AE service area (AEDC 1.4.1)\n- Non-standard street light poles are permitted with developer/AE collaboration and developer payment of cost difference\n- Projects below all AULCC thresholds (no pole relocations, <300 LF citywide, <25 LF in DAPCZ, no main assignments) are exempt from AULCC review\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Citing LDC § 25-9-173 for the electric service capacity/availability letter requirement — this section does not exist in the current LDC (chapter numbering jumps from 164 to 191 in that division); the correct authority is AEDC 1.4.1 and AEDC 1.3.2\n- Citing COA § 14-11-205 for the 100-foot moonlight tower permit requirement — § 14-11-205 governs only the 25-foot ROW installation prohibition; the 100-foot permit requirement is in COA § 14-11-201\n- Treating AULCC clearance as an \"all sites\" requirement — AULCC review is only triggered when one of the four specific thresholds (linear footage, DAPCZ linear footage, pole relocation, or main assignment) is met; universal application generates false deficiencies for small infill projects\n- Accepting an Address Validation Letter (AVL) as an approved addressing plan — these are distinct documents; the addressing plan must be separately approved\n\n## Code References\n\n- AEDC 1.3.0 — Basic requirements of electric distribution service (customer vs. AE facility ownership, easement grants, access requirements, truck access dimensions)\n- AEDC 1.4.0 — Requesting and obtaining electric distribution service; ESPA submission process and service type thresholds\n- AEDC 1.4.1 — Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirming service area\n- AEDC 1.6.0 — Street lighting standards, developer responsibilities, and IESNA requirements\n- AEDC 1.16.0 — Land development review application requirements for Austin Energy (AutoCAD drawing, clearance demonstration, easement sufficiency, access, point of service, plat notes for transmission easements)\n- Addressing Standards, Introduction — Address Management Services (AMS) is the addressing authority for the City of Austin and Travis County; addressing review occurs during subdivision and site plan review processes; approval required for permitting\n- Addressing Standards, Streets — Private drive, access easement, and street naming criteria; all proposed street names must be reviewed by AMS and reserved before use; named driveways and easements must adhere to Austin and Travis County Street Name Standards\n- COA § 14-11-201 — Permit required for construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or its guy wires\n- COA § 14-11-205 — No facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; temporary use of ROW permit required\n- Great Streets Chapter 6 — Street lighting design standards for projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), Lamar Blvd (W)): 8 poles/block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures)\n- UCM 2.5.1.F — Additional authorizations required for final plan approval (TxDOT, railroad, gas company, TCEQ, water districts, etc.)\n- UCM 3.2.0 — AULCC: designation as ROW utility coordination body; requirement that all projects impacting ROW infrastructure be processed through the AULCC\n- UCM 3.4.0 — Utility coordination process phases (Pre-Design, Design, Final Design, Utility Adjustment)\n- UCM 3.8.0 — Pre-construction and construction meeting requirements\n- General Site Plan Notes — Standard Austin Energy Notes — Four mandatory notes required verbatim on all consolidated site plan applications\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site/Utility Plan Sheets** — Verify Austin Energy service area note, developer responsibility note for conduit/infrastructure, street light placement and dimensions, signal cabinet representations, utility conflicts in ROW (street lights vs. gas lines, storm inlets, etc.)\n- **Plan Notes Block / General Notes Sheet** — Check for all four Standard Austin Energy Notes (verbatim), moonlight tower permit and notification language, preconstruction meeting requirement note, developer responsibility note for AE conduit standards\n- **Cover Sheet or Transmittal** — Confirm Additional Review Acknowledgement table is complete with all external reviews/approvals (TxDOT, railroad, gas, TCEQ, water districts, etc.) per UCM 2.5.1.F\n- **Approval/Clearance Letters on File** — ESPA approval from Austin Energy, AEU clearance letter, Electric Service Availability Letter (where service provider was uncertain), AULCC Completeness Letter, Gattan clearance, telecommunications provider clearances, dry utility clearances, CIP clearance\n- **ROW Review Case Status** — Confirm utility coordination case is closed before ROW Review can be marked Approved in the Site Plan case\n- **Addressing Records** — Confirm addressing plan has been approved and all street names (including private drives) are reserved with 911 Addressing before site plan approval\n- **Easement Documents** — Verify electrical easements are dedicated using standard City forms where AE facilities are proposed on private property\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-3.1 | Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) not submitted to Austin Energy for review and approval, or ESPA approval not documented in the plan set | AEDC 1.4.2.1 | Only when new or modified electrical service is required. Check: project scope for new construction or electrical service upgrades |\n| EL-3.2 | Austin Energy Utility (AEU) clearance or approval not obtained or not documented | Reviewer Convention | All sites served by Austin Energy. Check: submittal package for AEU clearance letter |\n| EL-3.3 | Electric Service Availability Letter from Austin Energy not provided confirming that the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area; or for locations where the serving utility is uncertain, AE Public Involvement has not been contacted to obtain this letter | AEDC 1.4.1; AEDC 1.3.2 | Only when new electrical service is being obtained from Austin Energy and there is a question as to which electric utility serves the location. Check: project location relative to established AE service territory and ESPA documentation |\n| EL-3.4 | Site plan does not include a note stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area | AEDC 1.16.0(7) | All sites within the Austin Energy service area. Check: plan notes sheet for AE service area note |\n| EL-3.5 | Plans do not include the required developer responsibility note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts | AEDC 1.16.0(7); AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when new Austin Energy electrical infrastructure (conduit, conductors, pull-boxes, foundations) is required. Check: ESPA or electrical plans for new service |\n| EL-3.6 | One or more of the four required Standard Austin Energy Notes is missing or altered from the required verbatim text: (1) AE tree pruning rights in easements; (2) owner/developer obligation to provide additional easements or access for AE facilities; (3) owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines; (4) owner responsibility for maintaining NESC/OSHA/City/state clearances near overhead power lines | General Site Plan Notes — Standard AE Notes | All consolidated site plan applications. Check: general notes sheet for all four required notes verbatim; changing the required text results in rejection |\n| EL-3.7 | Austin Energy approval not obtained for electrical transformer and pull box design prior to construction | AEDC 1.4.2.4 | Only when new or relocated transformers or pull boxes are proposed. Check: utility plan sheets for proposed AE equipment |\n| EL-3.8 | New driveway or site access improvement conflicts with existing Austin Energy power pole guy anchor support and coordination with Austin Energy has not been completed | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when new driveways or access improvements are proposed near existing power poles with guy anchors. Check: utility plan sheets for pole anchor locations adjacent to proposed driveways |\n| EL-3.9 | Plans do not include a note that a preconstruction meeting with Austin Energy is required prior to starting demolition | UCM 3.8.0 | Only when demolition is proposed. Check: demolition plan or general notes for preconstruction meeting requirement |\n| EL-3.10 | Final layout solution and electrical easement recording have not been coordinated with Austin Energy before approval sign-off; easement documents using standard City forms have not been returned to AE before AE begins installation | AEDC 1.16.0(3); AEDC 1.3.6 | Only when new electrical easements are required for proposed AE facilities on private property. Check: easement exhibits and AE design correspondence |\n| EL-3.11 | Land development application submitted to Austin Energy does not include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) with all required content: public/private easements, existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, transformers), permanent structures with footprint and height, final topology, and property lines with building setbacks | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when submitting a land development application to Austin Energy for review. Check: AE submittal package for electronic drawing format compliance and required content |\n| EL-3.12 | Plans do not demonstrate that the proposed development will not limit Austin Energy personnel access to current and proposed electric facilities (e.g., no access path shown to proposed transformers, pull boxes, or overhead line equipment; no truck access dimensions provided for AE equipment areas) | AEDC 1.16.0(4) | Only when development is adjacent to existing or proposed AE electric facilities. Check: site plan and grading plan for AE equipment access routes; verify minimum 12-ft width and 16-ft vertical clearance for truck access per AEDC 1.3.16 |\n| EL-3.13 | Plan notes do not state that a permit from Austin Energy is required for any construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower, per COA § 14-11-201 | COA § 14-11-201 | Only when a moonlight tower or its guy wires is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: utility base map and Austin Energy moonlight tower records; if project is in downtown or central Austin, flag for human verification of proximity — moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files |\n| EL-3.14 | Plan notes do not document Austin Energy's advance notification requirement before construction commences within 100 feet of a moonlight tower; this is an Austin Energy operational practice (not codified in COA § 14-11-205) and plans near moonlight towers should include coordination notes and current AE contact information for pre-construction notification | Austin Energy operational requirement | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: same proximity verification as EL-3.13; note that any phone number shown on plans should be confirmed current with Austin Energy |\n| EL-3.15 | Plans propose installing a facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower, which is prohibited; additionally, no temporary use of ROW permit has been obtained or referenced for ROW occupancy in the vicinity of the tower | COA § 14-11-205 | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area and ROW facility installations are proposed. Check: proposed ROW facility locations vs. any annotated or known moonlight tower locations; flag for human verification if project is in downtown or central Austin and plans show ROW work within potential tower proximity |\n| EL-3.16 | Proposed street light placement in the ROW conflicts with an existing utility (e.g., gas line, storm sewer) and the conflict has not been resolved on the plans | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in or adjacent to the ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for horizontal conflicts between proposed light poles and mapped utilities |\n| EL-3.17 | Traffic signal cabinets shown on plans not updated to reflect current field conditions at affected intersections | Reviewer Convention | Only when traffic signal cabinets are shown on plans. Check: utility or streetscape plan sheets for signal cabinet locations vs. current field layout |\n| EL-3.18 | Street light pole spacing and placement not coordinated with Austin Energy; pole locations not confirmed with AE Design (and with Urban Design where applicable) | AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for evidence of AE coordination on pole spacing |\n| EL-3.19 | Electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans do not extend to all required utility infrastructure locations within the project area; plan coverage ends before reaching the full extent of proposed utility work along the project frontage | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans are part of the plan set and utility infrastructure extends across the project frontage. Check: plan coverage limits vs. full extent of proposed utility work |\n| EL-3.20 | Street lighting labels on plan sheets are duplicated or incorrectly applied (e.g., extra Great Streets light label assigned to a tree well or non-light feature) | Great Streets Chapter 6 | Only when the site is within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (bounded by MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W)) and Great Streets lighting standards apply. Check: confirm project is within Great Streets boundary, then review streetscape plan sheets for label accuracy and duplication |\n| EL-3.21 | Dimensions between street light poles not provided on plan sheets | AEDC 1.16.0(1); AEDC 1.6.0 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for dimensional callouts between pole locations |\n| EL-3.22 | Street light pole placement conflicts with a storm drain inlet or other subsurface stormwater infrastructure, requiring pole relocation | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in close proximity to stormwater inlets. Check: utility plan sheets overlaying proposed pole locations with storm sewer infrastructure |\n| EL-3.23 | AULCC (Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee) clearance not obtained before site plan approval; AULCC Completeness Letter not on file | UCM 3.2.0 | Only when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) 300 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW (citywide); (2) 25 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within DAPCZ (roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east — note: IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary only in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street — Oltorf/Barton Skyway (S), Loop 1/MoPac (W), Enfield Road/MLK Blvd (N); formal metes-and-bounds boundary in Code § 14-11-161(7); boundary streets included); (3) any utility pole relocation, regardless of linear footage; (4) water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets. Check: measure total linear footage of proposed ROW utility work on utility plan sheets; check for any pole relocations; determine if project is within DAPCZ |\n| EL-3.24 | Gattan clearance or approval not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when Gattan infrastructure is present or affected. Check: utility plan sheets for Gattan-labeled facilities in the project area; if unknown, flag for human verification via AULCC correspondence |\n| EL-3.25 | Telecommunications provider clearances not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when telecommunications infrastructure is affected by or located within the project area or ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for telecom facilities |\n| EL-3.26 | Dry utility clearances not obtained from all applicable providers | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when dry utilities (electric, gas, telecom) are present in the ROW or affected by site work. Check: utility plan sheets for dry utility facilities |\n| EL-3.27 | CIP (Capital Improvement Projects) clearance not obtained; a CIP project manager has not been identified or confirmed | Reviewer Convention | Only when a CIP project is active in or adjacent to the project area. Check: CIP project lookup for the project vicinity — requires human verification via City of Austin CIP project management records |\n| EL-3.28 | ROW utility coordination case not closed; ROW Review cannot be marked Approved in the Site Plan case until the utility coordination case is complete | UCM 3.2.0 | All sites with proposed ROW work. Check: ROW Review case status in site plan case management system |\n| EL-3.29 | Additional Review Acknowledgement table not completed on plans with all applicable external reviews, approvals, or permits required for the project (e.g., TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, Planning and Development Review Department approvals) | UCM 2.5.1.F | All sites. Check: cover sheet or transmittal for completed Additional Review Acknowledgement table |\n| EL-3.30 | Proposed street names, including names for private drives, not approved and reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before site plan approval | Addressing Standards, Streets | Only when new streets or private drives are proposed. Check: plan set for any new street names; confirm 911 Addressing reservation status |\n| EL-3.31 | Addressing plan not approved; or consultation meeting with 911 Addressing not scheduled before submittal when no approved addressing plan exists; note that receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan | Addressing Standards, Introduction | Only when new addresses are required for the development. Check: addressing records for an approved addressing plan separate from any AVL issued |\n| EL-3.32 | Transmission easement plat notes missing or incomplete; when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, plans must include all required AEDC 1.16.0(8) plat notes (AE tree pruning rights; additional easement/access provision; erosion control and tree protection responsibilities; clearance maintenance; NESC compliance restrictions on structures; 24-hour AE access to easement; ≥48,000 lb tandem axle road/driveway capacity within easement; 48-hour notice before construction or grading within easement); additionally, land within the transmission easement must not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication per AEDC 1.16.0(9) | AEDC 1.16.0(8); AEDC 1.16.0(9) | Only when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property. Check: easement documents, plat sheets, and plan notes for all eight required AEDC 1.16.0(8) notes; verify that no portion of the transmission easement is labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication |","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732552,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017do5qavcF6zw8Yw6LBVPia","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n    10→\n    11→Customer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n    12→\n    13→The Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n    14→\n    15→**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n    16→\n    17→**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n    18→\n    19→Service-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n    20→\n    21→**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n    22→\n    23→AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n    24→\n    25→**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n    26→\n    27→Transmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n    28→\n    29→**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n    30→\n    31→Permanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n    32→\n    33→**Key Thresholds:**\n    34→- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n    35→- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n    36→- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n    37→- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n    38→- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n    39→- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n    40→- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n    41→- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n    42→- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n    43→- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n    44→- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n    45→\n    46→**Exceptions:**\n    47→- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n    48→- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n    49→- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n    50→\n    51→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    52→- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n    53→- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n    54→- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n    55→- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n    56→- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n    57→- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n    58→\n    59→## Code References\n    60→\n    61→- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n    62→- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n    63→- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n    64→- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n    65→- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n    66→- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n    67→- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n    68→- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n    69→- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n    70→- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n    71→- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n    72→- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n    73→- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n    74→- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n    75→- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n    76→- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n    77→- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n    78→- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n    79→- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n    80→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n    81→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n    82→- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n    83→\n    84→*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n    85→- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    86→- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    87→\n    88→## Documents to Review\n    89→\n    90→- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n    91→- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n    92→- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n    93→- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n    94→- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n    95→- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n    96→- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n    97→- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n    98→\n    99→## Checklist Items\n   100→\n   101→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   102→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   103→| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n   104→| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n   105→| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n   106→| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n   107→| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n   108→| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n   109→| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n   110→| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n   111→| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n   112→| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n   113→| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n   114→| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n   115→| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n   116→| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n   117→| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n   118→| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n   119→| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n   120→| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n   121→| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n   122→| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n   123→| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n   124→| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n   125→| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n   126→| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n   127→| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n   128→| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n   129→| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n   130→| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24aa3565-6e12-4421-aab9-b461d3b546f4","uuid":"a1bcce8b-b501-4f7b-b4b6-0203b607f70e","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/1.md","content":"# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n\nCustomer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n\nThe Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n\n**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n\n**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n\nService-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n\n**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n\nAE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n\n**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n\nTransmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n\n**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n\nPermanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n\n*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":130}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732576,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SxoKvU422XrpDpT8z9ojU1","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n    10→\n    11→Customer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n    12→\n    13→The Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n    14→\n    15→**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n    16→\n    17→**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n    18→\n    19→Service-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n    20→\n    21→**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n    22→\n    23→AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n    24→\n    25→**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n    26→\n    27→Transmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n    28→\n    29→**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n    30→\n    31→Permanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n    32→\n    33→**Key Thresholds:**\n    34→- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n    35→- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n    36→- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n    37→- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n    38→- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n    39→- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n    40→- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n    41→- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n    42→- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n    43→- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n    44→- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n    45→\n    46→**Exceptions:**\n    47→- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n    48→- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n    49→- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n    50→\n    51→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    52→- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n    53→- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n    54→- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n    55→- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n    56→- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n    57→- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n    58→\n    59→## Code References\n    60→\n    61→- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n    62→- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n    63→- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n    64→- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n    65→- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n    66→- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n    67→- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n    68→- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n    69→- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n    70→- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n    71→- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n    72→- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n    73→- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n    74→- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n    75→- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n    76→- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n    77→- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n    78→- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n    79→- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n    80→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n    81→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n    82→- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n    83→\n    84→*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n    85→- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    86→- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    87→\n    88→## Documents to Review\n    89→\n    90→- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n    91→- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n    92→- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n    93→- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n    94→- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n    95→- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n    96→- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n    97→- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n    98→\n    99→## Checklist Items\n   100→\n   101→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   102→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   103→| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n   104→| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n   105→| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n   106→| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n   107→| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n   108→| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n   109→| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n   110→| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n   111→| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n   112→| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n   113→| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n   114→| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n   115→| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n   116→| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n   117→| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n   118→| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n   119→| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n   120→| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n   121→| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n   122→| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n   123→| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n   124→| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n   125→| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n   126→| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n   127→| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n   128→| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n   129→| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n   130→| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4a1fc80-d233-40ef-93cf-fcf0c529f6de","uuid":"4d4c335d-84a7-4370-accd-fe01f68378dd","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/1.md","content":"# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n\nCustomer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n\nThe Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n\n**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n\n**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n\nService-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n\n**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n\nAE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n\n**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n\nTransmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n\n**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n\nPermanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n\n*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":130}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732577,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"3.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CfbG4igg6jMvuKRra5kcrZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Austin Energy Service and ROW Utility Coordination\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers all coordination requirements with Austin Energy (for electrical service, street lighting, and moonlight tower compliance) and with other ROW utility stakeholders (AULCC, Gattan, telecommunications, dry utilities, and CIP) before a site plan can be approved. It also captures the administrative closeout requirements — ROW utility coordination case closure, the Additional Review Acknowledgement table, street name reservations, and addressing approvals — that gate final ROW Review approval.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Austin Energy Service Coordination.** Austin Energy (AE) is the City of Austin's municipal electric utility serving both the city and portions of the surrounding area. All new development or service modification requires submission of an Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) to the appropriate AE Design business unit along with required documents to obtain an electrical design (AEDC 1.4.2.1). For service ≤350 amps single-phase or ≤225 amps three-phase (non-network), the Service-Only process applies; for larger loads or any service within the downtown Network Area, the Design Services process applies and full AE Design involvement is required (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4). Normally, the ESPA is submitted after DSD approves the site plan and must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A). A Building Service Planning Application (BSPA) — distinct from the ESPA — is required for building permits when the exterior of a structure is modified. For locations where it is uncertain which utility serves the site, an Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirms whether the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area (AEDC 1.4.1). AE Design has sole authority over available service characteristics and voltage at any given location (AEDC 1.3.2). AE owns electric facilities in the public ROW; customers own facilities from the point of service toward the building, except for the meter (AEDC 1.3.5). Before AE begins installation, the customer must grant AE an electrical easement using standard City forms signed by the property owner (AEDC 1.3.6).\n    10→\n    11→**Land Development Review Requirements (AEDC 1.16.0).** All land development applications submitted for Austin Energy review must include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) showing: all public and private easements; all existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, and transformers); permanent structures with footprint and height; final topology (grades, floodplains, detention ponds); and property lines with building setbacks. Plans must demonstrate sufficient clearances for existing and proposed electric facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(2)), dedicate additional easements if insufficient (at owner's expense, with metes and bounds descriptions), demonstrate that the development will not limit AE personnel access to current and proposed facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(4)), identify one point of service and one service voltage (exceptions require AE approval), and show approximate location and space for electric facilities with projected load. When a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, AEDC 1.16.0(8) requires specific plat notes: (i) AE tree pruning rights; (ii) provision for additional easements and access; (iii) owner erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection responsibilities; (iv) clearance maintenance obligation; (v) restriction on permanent structures that would violate NESC clearances within the transmission easement; (vi) 24-hour AE access across the property to transmission easements; (vii) all roads and driveways in the transmission easement built to support ≥48,000 lb tandem axle loads; (viii) 48-hour notice to AE before construction or grading within a transmission easement. Land within transmission easements may not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication (AEDC 1.16.0(9)).\n    12→\n    13→**Required Austin Energy Plan Notes.** All consolidated site plan applications must include four Standard Austin Energy Notes verbatim — changing the required text results in rejection: (1) Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery, and other obstructions to keep easements clear, and AE will comply with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B; (2) the owner/developer must provide AE with any easement and/or access required (beyond those indicated) for overhead and underground electric facilities, and such facilities shall not cause the site to be out of compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8; (3) the owner is responsible for temporary erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and initial tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of proposed overhead electrical facilities, and AE's work area must be included within the limits of construction; (4) the property owner is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines; AE will not render service unless required clearances are maintained; failure costs are charged to the owner. In addition to these four standard notes, AE's land development review process under AEDC 1.16.0(7) requires site-specific plan notes: (a) a note identifying the site as lying within the Austin Energy service area, and (b) a note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current AE Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts.\n    14→\n    15→**Street Lighting.** AE develops a complete lighting installation plan for new residential and commercial developments. Developer conduit and infrastructure responsibilities vary by location type: for new residential subdivisions within city limits, the developer provides conduit, pull string, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs standard poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.4); for commercial areas within city limits, the developer pays all installation costs and provides conduit, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.6); outside city limits in the AE service area, the developer bears all costs including monthly energy charges (AEDC 1.6.5, 1.6.7). All street lighting must meet IESNA spacing and illumination standards. Non-standard poles require developer/AE collaboration and the developer pays the cost difference. For projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary — bounded by Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez St (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W) — Great Streets Chapter 6 lighting standards apply: 8 poles per block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures). All pole placement and spacing requires prior AE Design coordination and, where applicable, Urban Design coordination.\n    16→\n    17→**AULCC and ROW Utility Coordination.** The Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee (AULCC) — referred to as \"ULCC\" in the UCM — administers the ROW utility coordination process. Per UCM 3.2.0, all projects that impact infrastructure located in the public right-of-way must be processed through the AULCC before proceeding to the permitting and construction phase. AULCC review is triggered when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) **300 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW anywhere in the city; (2) **25 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within the **Downtown Austin Project Coordination Zone (DAPCZ)** — roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east (IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street), Oltorf Street/Barton Skyway to the south, Loop 1 (MoPac) to the west, and Enfield Road/Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd to the north; the formal metes-and-bounds boundary is in Code § 14-11-161(7) and includes the boundary streets; (3) **any utility pole relocation**, regardless of linear footage, anywhere in the city; or (4) **water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets** (UCM 2.9.1.B.1). Projects that do not meet any of these thresholds are not subject to AULCC review. Successful AULCC coordination results in an AULCC Completeness Letter (clearance letter) that must be on file before the ROW Review can be marked Approved. The ROW utility coordination case must be closed in the site plan case management system before approval can be issued. Additional external utility clearances required through the ROW coordination process include: Gattan (a specific utility provider — its service territory is not described in Bureau code files; applicability is determined by the presence of Gattan-labeled facilities on utility plan sheets or from AULCC correspondence; requires human verification if unknown), telecommunications providers, dry utility providers, and any active CIP project manager for projects in areas with active capital improvement work.\n    18→\n    19→**Moonlight Towers.** Moonlight towers are specific, fixed-location, City-owned steel lattice towers with supporting guy wires, historically used for citywide arc lighting and concentrated in downtown and central Austin. Two separate regulatory thresholds apply: **(1) 100-foot permit threshold (COA § 14-11-201):** no construction activity may occur within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower without a permit under COA Division 4, Chapter 14-11; construction on private property requires application to the building official; construction in the ROW requires application to the director; the guy wire buffer applies independently from the tower base, so a project can be more than 100 feet from the base but still require a permit if within 100 feet of a guy wire. **(2) 25-foot ROW prohibition (COA § 14-11-205):** no facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; a permittee must also obtain a temporary use of ROW permit to occupy the ROW near a tower. A 24-hour advance notification to Austin Energy before construction near a moonlight tower is an AE operational practice but is not codified in COA §§ 14-11-201 through 14-11-205. Moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files — the review agent cannot determine proximity from plans alone. For projects in downtown or central Austin, moonlight tower proximity items should be flagged for human verification unless plans affirmatively note the absence of nearby towers.\n    20→\n    21→**Administrative Closeout.** Final site plan approval requires: the AULCC Completeness Letter on file; the Additional Review Acknowledgement table completed on the cover sheet or transmittal listing all applicable external reviews and permits (TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, and Planning and Development Review Department approvals) per UCM 2.5.1.F; all proposed street names, including names for private drives, reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before approval; and an addressing plan approved by Address Management Services. **Receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan** — applicants frequently confuse these two documents and reviewers must confirm the addressing plan is approved, not just that an AVL was issued.\n    22→\n    23→**Key Thresholds:**\n    24→- **ESPA service type:** ≤350 A single-phase or ≤225 A three-phase (non-network) → Service-Only process; >350 A single-phase or >225 A three-phase, or any Network Area service → Design Services process with full AE Design involvement (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4)\n    25→- **Continuous load:** >320 A must upgrade to instrument-rated CT service (AEDC 1.4.0)\n    26→- **Moonlight tower permit:** Construction within 100 feet of a tower or its guy wires requires permit under COA § 14-11-201\n    27→- **Moonlight tower ROW prohibition:** No facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower (COA § 14-11-205)\n    28→- **AULCC trigger (citywide):** ≥300 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation (UCM 3.2.0)\n    29→- **AULCC trigger (DAPCZ):** ≥25 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation within DAPCZ (UCM 3.2.0)\n    30→- **AULCC trigger (pole):** Any utility pole relocation, anywhere in the city (UCM 3.2.0)\n    31→- **Great Streets lighting boundary:** MLK Blvd (N) / Lady Bird Lake–Cesar Chavez (S) / IH-35 (E) / Lamar Blvd (W) — 8 poles/block, 24-ft street / 12-ft pedestrian mounting heights (Great Streets Ch. 6)\n    32→\n    33→**Exceptions:**\n    34→- ESPA submission normally occurs after DSD site plan approval, not before; ESPA must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A)\n    35→- Electric Service Availability Letter is required only where there is a question as to which utility serves the site; not required for all projects in the established AE service area (AEDC 1.4.1)\n    36→- Non-standard street light poles are permitted with developer/AE collaboration and developer payment of cost difference\n    37→- Projects below all AULCC thresholds (no pole relocations, <300 LF citywide, <25 LF in DAPCZ, no main assignments) are exempt from AULCC review\n    38→\n    39→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    40→- Citing LDC § 25-9-173 for the electric service capacity/availability letter requirement — this section does not exist in the current LDC (chapter numbering jumps from 164 to 191 in that division); the correct authority is AEDC 1.4.1 and AEDC 1.3.2\n    41→- Citing COA § 14-11-205 for the 100-foot moonlight tower permit requirement — § 14-11-205 governs only the 25-foot ROW installation prohibition; the 100-foot permit requirement is in COA § 14-11-201\n    42→- Treating AULCC clearance as an \"all sites\" requirement — AULCC review is only triggered when one of the four specific thresholds (linear footage, DAPCZ linear footage, pole relocation, or main assignment) is met; universal application generates false deficiencies for small infill projects\n    43→- Accepting an Address Validation Letter (AVL) as an approved addressing plan — these are distinct documents; the addressing plan must be separately approved\n    44→\n    45→## Code References\n    46→\n    47→- AEDC 1.3.0 — Basic requirements of electric distribution service (customer vs. AE facility ownership, easement grants, access requirements, truck access dimensions)\n    48→- AEDC 1.4.0 — Requesting and obtaining electric distribution service; ESPA submission process and service type thresholds\n    49→- AEDC 1.4.1 — Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirming service area\n    50→- AEDC 1.6.0 — Street lighting standards, developer responsibilities, and IESNA requirements\n    51→- AEDC 1.16.0 — Land development review application requirements for Austin Energy (AutoCAD drawing, clearance demonstration, easement sufficiency, access, point of service, plat notes for transmission easements)\n    52→- Addressing Standards, Introduction — Address Management Services (AMS) is the addressing authority for the City of Austin and Travis County; addressing review occurs during subdivision and site plan review processes; approval required for permitting\n    53→- Addressing Standards, Streets — Private drive, access easement, and street naming criteria; all proposed street names must be reviewed by AMS and reserved before use; named driveways and easements must adhere to Austin and Travis County Street Name Standards\n    54→- COA § 14-11-201 — Permit required for construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or its guy wires\n    55→- COA § 14-11-205 — No facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; temporary use of ROW permit required\n    56→- Great Streets Chapter 6 — Street lighting design standards for projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), Lamar Blvd (W)): 8 poles/block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures)\n    57→- UCM 2.5.1.F — Additional authorizations required for final plan approval (TxDOT, railroad, gas company, TCEQ, water districts, etc.)\n    58→- UCM 3.2.0 — AULCC: designation as ROW utility coordination body; requirement that all projects impacting ROW infrastructure be processed through the AULCC\n    59→- UCM 3.4.0 — Utility coordination process phases (Pre-Design, Design, Final Design, Utility Adjustment)\n    60→- UCM 3.8.0 — Pre-construction and construction meeting requirements\n    61→- General Site Plan Notes — Standard Austin Energy Notes — Four mandatory notes required verbatim on all consolidated site plan applications\n    62→\n    63→## Documents to Review\n    64→\n    65→- **Site/Utility Plan Sheets** — Verify Austin Energy service area note, developer responsibility note for conduit/infrastructure, street light placement and dimensions, signal cabinet representations, utility conflicts in ROW (street lights vs. gas lines, storm inlets, etc.)\n    66→- **Plan Notes Block / General Notes Sheet** — Check for all four Standard Austin Energy Notes (verbatim), moonlight tower permit and notification language, preconstruction meeting requirement note, developer responsibility note for AE conduit standards\n    67→- **Cover Sheet or Transmittal** — Confirm Additional Review Acknowledgement table is complete with all external reviews/approvals (TxDOT, railroad, gas, TCEQ, water districts, etc.) per UCM 2.5.1.F\n    68→- **Approval/Clearance Letters on File** — ESPA approval from Austin Energy, AEU clearance letter, Electric Service Availability Letter (where service provider was uncertain), AULCC Completeness Letter, Gattan clearance, telecommunications provider clearances, dry utility clearances, CIP clearance\n    69→- **ROW Review Case Status** — Confirm utility coordination case is closed before ROW Review can be marked Approved in the Site Plan case\n    70→- **Addressing Records** — Confirm addressing plan has been approved and all street names (including private drives) are reserved with 911 Addressing before site plan approval\n    71→- **Easement Documents** — Verify electrical easements are dedicated using standard City forms where AE facilities are proposed on private property\n    72→\n    73→## Checklist Items\n    74→\n    75→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    76→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    77→| EL-3.1 | Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) not submitted to Austin Energy for review and approval, or ESPA approval not documented in the plan set | AEDC 1.4.2.1 | Only when new or modified electrical service is required. Check: project scope for new construction or electrical service upgrades |\n    78→| EL-3.2 | Austin Energy Utility (AEU) clearance or approval not obtained or not documented | Reviewer Convention | All sites served by Austin Energy. Check: submittal package for AEU clearance letter |\n    79→| EL-3.3 | Electric Service Availability Letter from Austin Energy not provided confirming that the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area; or for locations where the serving utility is uncertain, AE Public Involvement has not been contacted to obtain this letter | AEDC 1.4.1; AEDC 1.3.2 | Only when new electrical service is being obtained from Austin Energy and there is a question as to which electric utility serves the location. Check: project location relative to established AE service territory and ESPA documentation |\n    80→| EL-3.4 | Site plan does not include a note stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area | AEDC 1.16.0(7) | All sites within the Austin Energy service area. Check: plan notes sheet for AE service area note |\n    81→| EL-3.5 | Plans do not include the required developer responsibility note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts | AEDC 1.16.0(7); AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when new Austin Energy electrical infrastructure (conduit, conductors, pull-boxes, foundations) is required. Check: ESPA or electrical plans for new service |\n    82→| EL-3.6 | One or more of the four required Standard Austin Energy Notes is missing or altered from the required verbatim text: (1) AE tree pruning rights in easements; (2) owner/developer obligation to provide additional easements or access for AE facilities; (3) owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines; (4) owner responsibility for maintaining NESC/OSHA/City/state clearances near overhead power lines | General Site Plan Notes — Standard AE Notes | All consolidated site plan applications. Check: general notes sheet for all four required notes verbatim; changing the required text results in rejection |\n    83→| EL-3.7 | Austin Energy approval not obtained for electrical transformer and pull box design prior to construction | AEDC 1.4.2.4 | Only when new or relocated transformers or pull boxes are proposed. Check: utility plan sheets for proposed AE equipment |\n    84→| EL-3.8 | New driveway or site access improvement conflicts with existing Austin Energy power pole guy anchor support and coordination with Austin Energy has not been completed | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when new driveways or access improvements are proposed near existing power poles with guy anchors. Check: utility plan sheets for pole anchor locations adjacent to proposed driveways |\n    85→| EL-3.9 | Plans do not include a note that a preconstruction meeting with Austin Energy is required prior to starting demolition | UCM 3.8.0 | Only when demolition is proposed. Check: demolition plan or general notes for preconstruction meeting requirement |\n    86→| EL-3.10 | Final layout solution and electrical easement recording have not been coordinated with Austin Energy before approval sign-off; easement documents using standard City forms have not been returned to AE before AE begins installation | AEDC 1.16.0(3); AEDC 1.3.6 | Only when new electrical easements are required for proposed AE facilities on private property. Check: easement exhibits and AE design correspondence |\n    87→| EL-3.11 | Land development application submitted to Austin Energy does not include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) with all required content: public/private easements, existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, transformers), permanent structures with footprint and height, final topology, and property lines with building setbacks | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when submitting a land development application to Austin Energy for review. Check: AE submittal package for electronic drawing format compliance and required content |\n    88→| EL-3.12 | Plans do not demonstrate that the proposed development will not limit Austin Energy personnel access to current and proposed electric facilities (e.g., no access path shown to proposed transformers, pull boxes, or overhead line equipment; no truck access dimensions provided for AE equipment areas) | AEDC 1.16.0(4) | Only when development is adjacent to existing or proposed AE electric facilities. Check: site plan and grading plan for AE equipment access routes; verify minimum 12-ft width and 16-ft vertical clearance for truck access per AEDC 1.3.16 |\n    89→| EL-3.13 | Plan notes do not state that a permit from Austin Energy is required for any construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower, per COA § 14-11-201 | COA § 14-11-201 | Only when a moonlight tower or its guy wires is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: utility base map and Austin Energy moonlight tower records; if project is in downtown or central Austin, flag for human verification of proximity — moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files |\n    90→| EL-3.14 | Plan notes do not document Austin Energy's advance notification requirement before construction commences within 100 feet of a moonlight tower; this is an Austin Energy operational practice (not codified in COA § 14-11-205) and plans near moonlight towers should include coordination notes and current AE contact information for pre-construction notification | Austin Energy operational requirement | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: same proximity verification as EL-3.13; note that any phone number shown on plans should be confirmed current with Austin Energy |\n    91→| EL-3.15 | Plans propose installing a facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower, which is prohibited; additionally, no temporary use of ROW permit has been obtained or referenced for ROW occupancy in the vicinity of the tower | COA § 14-11-205 | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area and ROW facility installations are proposed. Check: proposed ROW facility locations vs. any annotated or known moonlight tower locations; flag for human verification if project is in downtown or central Austin and plans show ROW work within potential tower proximity |\n    92→| EL-3.16 | Proposed street light placement in the ROW conflicts with an existing utility (e.g., gas line, storm sewer) and the conflict has not been resolved on the plans | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in or adjacent to the ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for horizontal conflicts between proposed light poles and mapped utilities |\n    93→| EL-3.17 | Traffic signal cabinets shown on plans not updated to reflect current field conditions at affected intersections | Reviewer Convention | Only when traffic signal cabinets are shown on plans. Check: utility or streetscape plan sheets for signal cabinet locations vs. current field layout |\n    94→| EL-3.18 | Street light pole spacing and placement not coordinated with Austin Energy; pole locations not confirmed with AE Design (and with Urban Design where applicable) | AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for evidence of AE coordination on pole spacing |\n    95→| EL-3.19 | Electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans do not extend to all required utility infrastructure locations within the project area; plan coverage ends before reaching the full extent of proposed utility work along the project frontage | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans are part of the plan set and utility infrastructure extends across the project frontage. Check: plan coverage limits vs. full extent of proposed utility work |\n    96→| EL-3.20 | Street lighting labels on plan sheets are duplicated or incorrectly applied (e.g., extra Great Streets light label assigned to a tree well or non-light feature) | Great Streets Chapter 6 | Only when the site is within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (bounded by MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W)) and Great Streets lighting standards apply. Check: confirm project is within Great Streets boundary, then review streetscape plan sheets for label accuracy and duplication |\n    97→| EL-3.21 | Dimensions between street light poles not provided on plan sheets | AEDC 1.16.0(1); AEDC 1.6.0 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for dimensional callouts between pole locations |\n    98→| EL-3.22 | Street light pole placement conflicts with a storm drain inlet or other subsurface stormwater infrastructure, requiring pole relocation | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in close proximity to stormwater inlets. Check: utility plan sheets overlaying proposed pole locations with storm sewer infrastructure |\n    99→| EL-3.23 | AULCC (Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee) clearance not obtained before site plan approval; AULCC Completeness Letter not on file | UCM 3.2.0 | Only when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) 300 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW (citywide); (2) 25 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within DAPCZ (roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east — note: IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary only in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street — Oltorf/Barton Skyway (S), Loop 1/MoPac (W), Enfield Road/MLK Blvd (N); formal metes-and-bounds boundary in Code § 14-11-161(7); boundary streets included); (3) any utility pole relocation, regardless of linear footage; (4) water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets. Check: measure total linear footage of proposed ROW utility work on utility plan sheets; check for any pole relocations; determine if project is within DAPCZ |\n   100→| EL-3.24 | Gattan clearance or approval not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when Gattan infrastructure is present or affected. Check: utility plan sheets for Gattan-labeled facilities in the project area; if unknown, flag for human verification via AULCC correspondence |\n   101→| EL-3.25 | Telecommunications provider clearances not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when telecommunications infrastructure is affected by or located within the project area or ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for telecom facilities |\n   102→| EL-3.26 | Dry utility clearances not obtained from all applicable providers | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when dry utilities (electric, gas, telecom) are present in the ROW or affected by site work. Check: utility plan sheets for dry utility facilities |\n   103→| EL-3.27 | CIP (Capital Improvement Projects) clearance not obtained; a CIP project manager has not been identified or confirmed | Reviewer Convention | Only when a CIP project is active in or adjacent to the project area. Check: CIP project lookup for the project vicinity — requires human verification via City of Austin CIP project management records |\n   104→| EL-3.28 | ROW utility coordination case not closed; ROW Review cannot be marked Approved in the Site Plan case until the utility coordination case is complete | UCM 3.2.0 | All sites with proposed ROW work. Check: ROW Review case status in site plan case management system |\n   105→| EL-3.29 | Additional Review Acknowledgement table not completed on plans with all applicable external reviews, approvals, or permits required for the project (e.g., TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, Planning and Development Review Department approvals) | UCM 2.5.1.F | All sites. Check: cover sheet or transmittal for completed Additional Review Acknowledgement table |\n   106→| EL-3.30 | Proposed street names, including names for private drives, not approved and reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before site plan approval | Addressing Standards, Streets | Only when new streets or private drives are proposed. Check: plan set for any new street names; confirm 911 Addressing reservation status |\n   107→| EL-3.31 | Addressing plan not approved; or consultation meeting with 911 Addressing not scheduled before submittal when no approved addressing plan exists; note that receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan | Addressing Standards, Introduction | Only when new addresses are required for the development. Check: addressing records for an approved addressing plan separate from any AVL issued |\n   108→| EL-3.32 | Transmission easement plat notes missing or incomplete; when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, plans must include all required AEDC 1.16.0(8) plat notes (AE tree pruning rights; additional easement/access provision; erosion control and tree protection responsibilities; clearance maintenance; NESC compliance restrictions on structures; 24-hour AE access to easement; ≥48,000 lb tandem axle road/driveway capacity within easement; 48-hour notice before construction or grading within easement); additionally, land within the transmission easement must not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication per AEDC 1.16.0(9) | AEDC 1.16.0(8); AEDC 1.16.0(9) | Only when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property. Check: easement documents, plat sheets, and plan notes for all eight required AEDC 1.16.0(8) notes; verify that no portion of the transmission easement is labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60bcd99d-409f-4cd6-a909-b056cb4b2c38","uuid":"6f12022d-a096-4edb-bce9-b30b66df6e1f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/3.md","content":"# Austin Energy Service and ROW Utility Coordination\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers all coordination requirements with Austin Energy (for electrical service, street lighting, and moonlight tower compliance) and with other ROW utility stakeholders (AULCC, Gattan, telecommunications, dry utilities, and CIP) before a site plan can be approved. It also captures the administrative closeout requirements — ROW utility coordination case closure, the Additional Review Acknowledgement table, street name reservations, and addressing approvals — that gate final ROW Review approval.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Austin Energy Service Coordination.** Austin Energy (AE) is the City of Austin's municipal electric utility serving both the city and portions of the surrounding area. All new development or service modification requires submission of an Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) to the appropriate AE Design business unit along with required documents to obtain an electrical design (AEDC 1.4.2.1). For service ≤350 amps single-phase or ≤225 amps three-phase (non-network), the Service-Only process applies; for larger loads or any service within the downtown Network Area, the Design Services process applies and full AE Design involvement is required (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4). Normally, the ESPA is submitted after DSD approves the site plan and must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A). A Building Service Planning Application (BSPA) — distinct from the ESPA — is required for building permits when the exterior of a structure is modified. For locations where it is uncertain which utility serves the site, an Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirms whether the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area (AEDC 1.4.1). AE Design has sole authority over available service characteristics and voltage at any given location (AEDC 1.3.2). AE owns electric facilities in the public ROW; customers own facilities from the point of service toward the building, except for the meter (AEDC 1.3.5). Before AE begins installation, the customer must grant AE an electrical easement using standard City forms signed by the property owner (AEDC 1.3.6).\n\n**Land Development Review Requirements (AEDC 1.16.0).** All land development applications submitted for Austin Energy review must include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) showing: all public and private easements; all existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, and transformers); permanent structures with footprint and height; final topology (grades, floodplains, detention ponds); and property lines with building setbacks. Plans must demonstrate sufficient clearances for existing and proposed electric facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(2)), dedicate additional easements if insufficient (at owner's expense, with metes and bounds descriptions), demonstrate that the development will not limit AE personnel access to current and proposed facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(4)), identify one point of service and one service voltage (exceptions require AE approval), and show approximate location and space for electric facilities with projected load. When a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, AEDC 1.16.0(8) requires specific plat notes: (i) AE tree pruning rights; (ii) provision for additional easements and access; (iii) owner erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection responsibilities; (iv) clearance maintenance obligation; (v) restriction on permanent structures that would violate NESC clearances within the transmission easement; (vi) 24-hour AE access across the property to transmission easements; (vii) all roads and driveways in the transmission easement built to support ≥48,000 lb tandem axle loads; (viii) 48-hour notice to AE before construction or grading within a transmission easement. Land within transmission easements may not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication (AEDC 1.16.0(9)).\n\n**Required Austin Energy Plan Notes.** All consolidated site plan applications must include four Standard Austin Energy Notes verbatim — changing the required text results in rejection: (1) Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery, and other obstructions to keep easements clear, and AE will comply with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B; (2) the owner/developer must provide AE with any easement and/or access required (beyond those indicated) for overhead and underground electric facilities, and such facilities shall not cause the site to be out of compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8; (3) the owner is responsible for temporary erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and initial tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of proposed overhead electrical facilities, and AE's work area must be included within the limits of construction; (4) the property owner is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines; AE will not render service unless required clearances are maintained; failure costs are charged to the owner. In addition to these four standard notes, AE's land development review process under AEDC 1.16.0(7) requires site-specific plan notes: (a) a note identifying the site as lying within the Austin Energy service area, and (b) a note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current AE Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts.\n\n**Street Lighting.** AE develops a complete lighting installation plan for new residential and commercial developments. Developer conduit and infrastructure responsibilities vary by location type: for new residential subdivisions within city limits, the developer provides conduit, pull string, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs standard poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.4); for commercial areas within city limits, the developer pays all installation costs and provides conduit, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.6); outside city limits in the AE service area, the developer bears all costs including monthly energy charges (AEDC 1.6.5, 1.6.7). All street lighting must meet IESNA spacing and illumination standards. Non-standard poles require developer/AE collaboration and the developer pays the cost difference. For projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary — bounded by Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez St (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W) — Great Streets Chapter 6 lighting standards apply: 8 poles per block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures). All pole placement and spacing requires prior AE Design coordination and, where applicable, Urban Design coordination.\n\n**AULCC and ROW Utility Coordination.** The Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee (AULCC) — referred to as \"ULCC\" in the UCM — administers the ROW utility coordination process. Per UCM 3.2.0, all projects that impact infrastructure located in the public right-of-way must be processed through the AULCC before proceeding to the permitting and construction phase. AULCC review is triggered when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) **300 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW anywhere in the city; (2) **25 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within the **Downtown Austin Project Coordination Zone (DAPCZ)** — roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east (IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street), Oltorf Street/Barton Skyway to the south, Loop 1 (MoPac) to the west, and Enfield Road/Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd to the north; the formal metes-and-bounds boundary is in Code § 14-11-161(7) and includes the boundary streets; (3) **any utility pole relocation**, regardless of linear footage, anywhere in the city; or (4) **water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets** (UCM 2.9.1.B.1). Projects that do not meet any of these thresholds are not subject to AULCC review. Successful AULCC coordination results in an AULCC Completeness Letter (clearance letter) that must be on file before the ROW Review can be marked Approved. The ROW utility coordination case must be closed in the site plan case management system before approval can be issued. Additional external utility clearances required through the ROW coordination process include: Gattan (a specific utility provider — its service territory is not described in Bureau code files; applicability is determined by the presence of Gattan-labeled facilities on utility plan sheets or from AULCC correspondence; requires human verification if unknown), telecommunications providers, dry utility providers, and any active CIP project manager for projects in areas with active capital improvement work.\n\n**Moonlight Towers.** Moonlight towers are specific, fixed-location, City-owned steel lattice towers with supporting guy wires, historically used for citywide arc lighting and concentrated in downtown and central Austin. Two separate regulatory thresholds apply: **(1) 100-foot permit threshold (COA § 14-11-201):** no construction activity may occur within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower without a permit under COA Division 4, Chapter 14-11; construction on private property requires application to the building official; construction in the ROW requires application to the director; the guy wire buffer applies independently from the tower base, so a project can be more than 100 feet from the base but still require a permit if within 100 feet of a guy wire. **(2) 25-foot ROW prohibition (COA § 14-11-205):** no facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; a permittee must also obtain a temporary use of ROW permit to occupy the ROW near a tower. A 24-hour advance notification to Austin Energy before construction near a moonlight tower is an AE operational practice but is not codified in COA §§ 14-11-201 through 14-11-205. Moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files — the review agent cannot determine proximity from plans alone. For projects in downtown or central Austin, moonlight tower proximity items should be flagged for human verification unless plans affirmatively note the absence of nearby towers.\n\n**Administrative Closeout.** Final site plan approval requires: the AULCC Completeness Letter on file; the Additional Review Acknowledgement table completed on the cover sheet or transmittal listing all applicable external reviews and permits (TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, and Planning and Development Review Department approvals) per UCM 2.5.1.F; all proposed street names, including names for private drives, reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before approval; and an addressing plan approved by Address Management Services. **Receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan** — applicants frequently confuse these two documents and reviewers must confirm the addressing plan is approved, not just that an AVL was issued.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **ESPA service type:** ≤350 A single-phase or ≤225 A three-phase (non-network) → Service-Only process; >350 A single-phase or >225 A three-phase, or any Network Area service → Design Services process with full AE Design involvement (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4)\n- **Continuous load:** >320 A must upgrade to instrument-rated CT service (AEDC 1.4.0)\n- **Moonlight tower permit:** Construction within 100 feet of a tower or its guy wires requires permit under COA § 14-11-201\n- **Moonlight tower ROW prohibition:** No facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower (COA § 14-11-205)\n- **AULCC trigger (citywide):** ≥300 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **AULCC trigger (DAPCZ):** ≥25 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation within DAPCZ (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **AULCC trigger (pole):** Any utility pole relocation, anywhere in the city (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **Great Streets lighting boundary:** MLK Blvd (N) / Lady Bird Lake–Cesar Chavez (S) / IH-35 (E) / Lamar Blvd (W) — 8 poles/block, 24-ft street / 12-ft pedestrian mounting heights (Great Streets Ch. 6)\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- ESPA submission normally occurs after DSD site plan approval, not before; ESPA must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A)\n- Electric Service Availability Letter is required only where there is a question as to which utility serves the site; not required for all projects in the established AE service area (AEDC 1.4.1)\n- Non-standard street light poles are permitted with developer/AE collaboration and developer payment of cost difference\n- Projects below all AULCC thresholds (no pole relocations, <300 LF citywide, <25 LF in DAPCZ, no main assignments) are exempt from AULCC review\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Citing LDC § 25-9-173 for the electric service capacity/availability letter requirement — this section does not exist in the current LDC (chapter numbering jumps from 164 to 191 in that division); the correct authority is AEDC 1.4.1 and AEDC 1.3.2\n- Citing COA § 14-11-205 for the 100-foot moonlight tower permit requirement — § 14-11-205 governs only the 25-foot ROW installation prohibition; the 100-foot permit requirement is in COA § 14-11-201\n- Treating AULCC clearance as an \"all sites\" requirement — AULCC review is only triggered when one of the four specific thresholds (linear footage, DAPCZ linear footage, pole relocation, or main assignment) is met; universal application generates false deficiencies for small infill projects\n- Accepting an Address Validation Letter (AVL) as an approved addressing plan — these are distinct documents; the addressing plan must be separately approved\n\n## Code References\n\n- AEDC 1.3.0 — Basic requirements of electric distribution service (customer vs. AE facility ownership, easement grants, access requirements, truck access dimensions)\n- AEDC 1.4.0 — Requesting and obtaining electric distribution service; ESPA submission process and service type thresholds\n- AEDC 1.4.1 — Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirming service area\n- AEDC 1.6.0 — Street lighting standards, developer responsibilities, and IESNA requirements\n- AEDC 1.16.0 — Land development review application requirements for Austin Energy (AutoCAD drawing, clearance demonstration, easement sufficiency, access, point of service, plat notes for transmission easements)\n- Addressing Standards, Introduction — Address Management Services (AMS) is the addressing authority for the City of Austin and Travis County; addressing review occurs during subdivision and site plan review processes; approval required for permitting\n- Addressing Standards, Streets — Private drive, access easement, and street naming criteria; all proposed street names must be reviewed by AMS and reserved before use; named driveways and easements must adhere to Austin and Travis County Street Name Standards\n- COA § 14-11-201 — Permit required for construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or its guy wires\n- COA § 14-11-205 — No facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; temporary use of ROW permit required\n- Great Streets Chapter 6 — Street lighting design standards for projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), Lamar Blvd (W)): 8 poles/block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures)\n- UCM 2.5.1.F — Additional authorizations required for final plan approval (TxDOT, railroad, gas company, TCEQ, water districts, etc.)\n- UCM 3.2.0 — AULCC: designation as ROW utility coordination body; requirement that all projects impacting ROW infrastructure be processed through the AULCC\n- UCM 3.4.0 — Utility coordination process phases (Pre-Design, Design, Final Design, Utility Adjustment)\n- UCM 3.8.0 — Pre-construction and construction meeting requirements\n- General Site Plan Notes — Standard Austin Energy Notes — Four mandatory notes required verbatim on all consolidated site plan applications\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site/Utility Plan Sheets** — Verify Austin Energy service area note, developer responsibility note for conduit/infrastructure, street light placement and dimensions, signal cabinet representations, utility conflicts in ROW (street lights vs. gas lines, storm inlets, etc.)\n- **Plan Notes Block / General Notes Sheet** — Check for all four Standard Austin Energy Notes (verbatim), moonlight tower permit and notification language, preconstruction meeting requirement note, developer responsibility note for AE conduit standards\n- **Cover Sheet or Transmittal** — Confirm Additional Review Acknowledgement table is complete with all external reviews/approvals (TxDOT, railroad, gas, TCEQ, water districts, etc.) per UCM 2.5.1.F\n- **Approval/Clearance Letters on File** — ESPA approval from Austin Energy, AEU clearance letter, Electric Service Availability Letter (where service provider was uncertain), AULCC Completeness Letter, Gattan clearance, telecommunications provider clearances, dry utility clearances, CIP clearance\n- **ROW Review Case Status** — Confirm utility coordination case is closed before ROW Review can be marked Approved in the Site Plan case\n- **Addressing Records** — Confirm addressing plan has been approved and all street names (including private drives) are reserved with 911 Addressing before site plan approval\n- **Easement Documents** — Verify electrical easements are dedicated using standard City forms where AE facilities are proposed on private property\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-3.1 | Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) not submitted to Austin Energy for review and approval, or ESPA approval not documented in the plan set | AEDC 1.4.2.1 | Only when new or modified electrical service is required. Check: project scope for new construction or electrical service upgrades |\n| EL-3.2 | Austin Energy Utility (AEU) clearance or approval not obtained or not documented | Reviewer Convention | All sites served by Austin Energy. Check: submittal package for AEU clearance letter |\n| EL-3.3 | Electric Service Availability Letter from Austin Energy not provided confirming that the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area; or for locations where the serving utility is uncertain, AE Public Involvement has not been contacted to obtain this letter | AEDC 1.4.1; AEDC 1.3.2 | Only when new electrical service is being obtained from Austin Energy and there is a question as to which electric utility serves the location. Check: project location relative to established AE service territory and ESPA documentation |\n| EL-3.4 | Site plan does not include a note stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area | AEDC 1.16.0(7) | All sites within the Austin Energy service area. Check: plan notes sheet for AE service area note |\n| EL-3.5 | Plans do not include the required developer responsibility note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts | AEDC 1.16.0(7); AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when new Austin Energy electrical infrastructure (conduit, conductors, pull-boxes, foundations) is required. Check: ESPA or electrical plans for new service |\n| EL-3.6 | One or more of the four required Standard Austin Energy Notes is missing or altered from the required verbatim text: (1) AE tree pruning rights in easements; (2) owner/developer obligation to provide additional easements or access for AE facilities; (3) owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines; (4) owner responsibility for maintaining NESC/OSHA/City/state clearances near overhead power lines | General Site Plan Notes — Standard AE Notes | All consolidated site plan applications. Check: general notes sheet for all four required notes verbatim; changing the required text results in rejection |\n| EL-3.7 | Austin Energy approval not obtained for electrical transformer and pull box design prior to construction | AEDC 1.4.2.4 | Only when new or relocated transformers or pull boxes are proposed. Check: utility plan sheets for proposed AE equipment |\n| EL-3.8 | New driveway or site access improvement conflicts with existing Austin Energy power pole guy anchor support and coordination with Austin Energy has not been completed | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when new driveways or access improvements are proposed near existing power poles with guy anchors. Check: utility plan sheets for pole anchor locations adjacent to proposed driveways |\n| EL-3.9 | Plans do not include a note that a preconstruction meeting with Austin Energy is required prior to starting demolition | UCM 3.8.0 | Only when demolition is proposed. Check: demolition plan or general notes for preconstruction meeting requirement |\n| EL-3.10 | Final layout solution and electrical easement recording have not been coordinated with Austin Energy before approval sign-off; easement documents using standard City forms have not been returned to AE before AE begins installation | AEDC 1.16.0(3); AEDC 1.3.6 | Only when new electrical easements are required for proposed AE facilities on private property. Check: easement exhibits and AE design correspondence |\n| EL-3.11 | Land development application submitted to Austin Energy does not include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) with all required content: public/private easements, existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, transformers), permanent structures with footprint and height, final topology, and property lines with building setbacks | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when submitting a land development application to Austin Energy for review. Check: AE submittal package for electronic drawing format compliance and required content |\n| EL-3.12 | Plans do not demonstrate that the proposed development will not limit Austin Energy personnel access to current and proposed electric facilities (e.g., no access path shown to proposed transformers, pull boxes, or overhead line equipment; no truck access dimensions provided for AE equipment areas) | AEDC 1.16.0(4) | Only when development is adjacent to existing or proposed AE electric facilities. Check: site plan and grading plan for AE equipment access routes; verify minimum 12-ft width and 16-ft vertical clearance for truck access per AEDC 1.3.16 |\n| EL-3.13 | Plan notes do not state that a permit from Austin Energy is required for any construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower, per COA § 14-11-201 | COA § 14-11-201 | Only when a moonlight tower or its guy wires is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: utility base map and Austin Energy moonlight tower records; if project is in downtown or central Austin, flag for human verification of proximity — moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files |\n| EL-3.14 | Plan notes do not document Austin Energy's advance notification requirement before construction commences within 100 feet of a moonlight tower; this is an Austin Energy operational practice (not codified in COA § 14-11-205) and plans near moonlight towers should include coordination notes and current AE contact information for pre-construction notification | Austin Energy operational requirement | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: same proximity verification as EL-3.13; note that any phone number shown on plans should be confirmed current with Austin Energy |\n| EL-3.15 | Plans propose installing a facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower, which is prohibited; additionally, no temporary use of ROW permit has been obtained or referenced for ROW occupancy in the vicinity of the tower | COA § 14-11-205 | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area and ROW facility installations are proposed. Check: proposed ROW facility locations vs. any annotated or known moonlight tower locations; flag for human verification if project is in downtown or central Austin and plans show ROW work within potential tower proximity |\n| EL-3.16 | Proposed street light placement in the ROW conflicts with an existing utility (e.g., gas line, storm sewer) and the conflict has not been resolved on the plans | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in or adjacent to the ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for horizontal conflicts between proposed light poles and mapped utilities |\n| EL-3.17 | Traffic signal cabinets shown on plans not updated to reflect current field conditions at affected intersections | Reviewer Convention | Only when traffic signal cabinets are shown on plans. Check: utility or streetscape plan sheets for signal cabinet locations vs. current field layout |\n| EL-3.18 | Street light pole spacing and placement not coordinated with Austin Energy; pole locations not confirmed with AE Design (and with Urban Design where applicable) | AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for evidence of AE coordination on pole spacing |\n| EL-3.19 | Electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans do not extend to all required utility infrastructure locations within the project area; plan coverage ends before reaching the full extent of proposed utility work along the project frontage | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans are part of the plan set and utility infrastructure extends across the project frontage. Check: plan coverage limits vs. full extent of proposed utility work |\n| EL-3.20 | Street lighting labels on plan sheets are duplicated or incorrectly applied (e.g., extra Great Streets light label assigned to a tree well or non-light feature) | Great Streets Chapter 6 | Only when the site is within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (bounded by MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W)) and Great Streets lighting standards apply. Check: confirm project is within Great Streets boundary, then review streetscape plan sheets for label accuracy and duplication |\n| EL-3.21 | Dimensions between street light poles not provided on plan sheets | AEDC 1.16.0(1); AEDC 1.6.0 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for dimensional callouts between pole locations |\n| EL-3.22 | Street light pole placement conflicts with a storm drain inlet or other subsurface stormwater infrastructure, requiring pole relocation | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in close proximity to stormwater inlets. Check: utility plan sheets overlaying proposed pole locations with storm sewer infrastructure |\n| EL-3.23 | AULCC (Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee) clearance not obtained before site plan approval; AULCC Completeness Letter not on file | UCM 3.2.0 | Only when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) 300 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW (citywide); (2) 25 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within DAPCZ (roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east — note: IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary only in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street — Oltorf/Barton Skyway (S), Loop 1/MoPac (W), Enfield Road/MLK Blvd (N); formal metes-and-bounds boundary in Code § 14-11-161(7); boundary streets included); (3) any utility pole relocation, regardless of linear footage; (4) water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets. Check: measure total linear footage of proposed ROW utility work on utility plan sheets; check for any pole relocations; determine if project is within DAPCZ |\n| EL-3.24 | Gattan clearance or approval not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when Gattan infrastructure is present or affected. Check: utility plan sheets for Gattan-labeled facilities in the project area; if unknown, flag for human verification via AULCC correspondence |\n| EL-3.25 | Telecommunications provider clearances not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when telecommunications infrastructure is affected by or located within the project area or ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for telecom facilities |\n| EL-3.26 | Dry utility clearances not obtained from all applicable providers | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when dry utilities (electric, gas, telecom) are present in the ROW or affected by site work. Check: utility plan sheets for dry utility facilities |\n| EL-3.27 | CIP (Capital Improvement Projects) clearance not obtained; a CIP project manager has not been identified or confirmed | Reviewer Convention | Only when a CIP project is active in or adjacent to the project area. Check: CIP project lookup for the project vicinity — requires human verification via City of Austin CIP project management records |\n| EL-3.28 | ROW utility coordination case not closed; ROW Review cannot be marked Approved in the Site Plan case until the utility coordination case is complete | UCM 3.2.0 | All sites with proposed ROW work. Check: ROW Review case status in site plan case management system |\n| EL-3.29 | Additional Review Acknowledgement table not completed on plans with all applicable external reviews, approvals, or permits required for the project (e.g., TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, Planning and Development Review Department approvals) | UCM 2.5.1.F | All sites. Check: cover sheet or transmittal for completed Additional Review Acknowledgement table |\n| EL-3.30 | Proposed street names, including names for private drives, not approved and reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before site plan approval | Addressing Standards, Streets | Only when new streets or private drives are proposed. Check: plan set for any new street names; confirm 911 Addressing reservation status |\n| EL-3.31 | Addressing plan not approved; or consultation meeting with 911 Addressing not scheduled before submittal when no approved addressing plan exists; note that receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan | Addressing Standards, Introduction | Only when new addresses are required for the development. Check: addressing records for an approved addressing plan separate from any AVL issued |\n| EL-3.32 | Transmission easement plat notes missing or incomplete; when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, plans must include all required AEDC 1.16.0(8) plat notes (AE tree pruning rights; additional easement/access provision; erosion control and tree protection responsibilities; clearance maintenance; NESC compliance restrictions on structures; 24-hour AE access to easement; ≥48,000 lb tandem axle road/driveway capacity within easement; 48-hour notice before construction or grading within easement); additionally, land within the transmission easement must not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication per AEDC 1.16.0(9) | AEDC 1.16.0(8); AEDC 1.16.0(9) | Only when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property. Check: easement documents, plat sheets, and plan notes for all eight required AEDC 1.16.0(8) notes; verify that no portion of the transmission easement is labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication |","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732666,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GmYsF5YSFh35N2SPemBmxH","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Underground Utility Routing and Placement\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers requirements governing where utility lines must be placed underground, which locations prohibit underground utility routing entirely, and how utility lines must be horizontally routed relative to specific site features such as parking areas, ponds, planting zones, parkland, and light rail corridors. It applies whenever utilities are proposed, relocated, or abandoned as part of a site development or infrastructure project.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→Austin's utility undergrounding requirements operate through multiple parallel regulatory frameworks. The applicable framework depends on the site's location, street frontage classification, and whether a superseding regulating plan applies. The review agent must identify which framework governs before evaluating specific deficiencies.\n    10→\n    11→**LDC Subchapter E Framework (CTC and Urban Roadway Sites):** For sites subject to LDC Subchapter E (Design Standards and Mixed Use) and not within a superseding regulating plan district, two roadway types trigger utility undergrounding requirements. *Core Transit Corridors (CTCs)* are the following 20 specifically enumerated roadway segments (segment limits are determinative — only the listed portion qualifies): South First Street (north of Ben White Blvd), East Seventh Street (west of Pleasant Valley Rd), East Fifth Street (I-35 to Pleasant Valley Rd), West Fifth Street (Guadalupe to Mopac), East Sixth Street (I-35 to Pleasant Valley Rd), West Sixth Street (Guadalupe to Pressler), West 35th/38th Street (Mopac to Guadalupe), Airport Boulevard (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Lane (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Road (east of Robert E. Lee Dr), Burnet Road (45th St to Anderson Ln only), South Congress Avenue (north of Stassney Ln only), Guadalupe Street, Lamar Boulevard (Banyon St to Ben White Blvd only), Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (Pearl St to Airport Blvd), Riverside Drive (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Road (51st St to US 290), 51st Street (Cameron to Manor Rd), Gaston Place (Westminster to Wellington), and Briarcliff Boulevard (Berkman to Westminster). On a corner lot, the CTC designation takes priority as the principal street. *Urban Roadways* are all roads within the Subchapter E boundary polygon that are not CTCs or Highways — the boundary runs roughly: 183 from Burnet to Hwy 71 → Hwy 71 to Loop 1 → Loop 1 to Lake Austin → Lake Austin to Exposition → Exposition to 35th → 35th to Loop 1 → Loop 1 (38th to RM 2222) → RM 2222 to Mesa → Mesa to Spicewood Springs → Spicewood Springs to 360 → 360 to Great Hills Trail → Great Hills Trail to 183 → 183 to Braker → Braker to Burnet → Burnet back to 183. For both CTC and Urban Roadway sites under Subchapter E, LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.a (CTC) and § 2.2.3.B.3 (Urban Roadway, via cross-reference to § 2.2.2.B.3) require: (a) all utility lines underground from building to property line; and (b) ROW utility lines placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable. Where overhead utilities remain behind the curb, no portion of the building may be within a 10-foot radius of the energized conductor (§ 2.2.2.B.3.b). Exception for short lots: on CTC lots with depth ≤120 feet where overhead utilities remain, utility-compatible alternative tree species may be used beneath (rather than offset from) the overhead utilities (§ 2.2.2.B.3.c).\n    12→\n    13→**Regulating Plan Districts — Plaza Saltillo TOD:** The Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area is a mapped district centered on the Plaza Saltillo MetroRail Station, bounded roughly by Rosewood Ave/E. 8th St (north), Chalmers Ave/Chicon St (east), E. 2nd St/Cesar Chavez (south), and IH-35/San Marcos St (west). It supersedes LDC Subchapter E in full for all properties within its boundary. Within the district, streets are classified as: *TOD Core Transit Corridor* (E. 7th Street only, per Plaza Saltillo TOD § 3.3.2); *TOD Pedestrian Priority Streets* (E. 5th St, E. 6th St, Waller St, Comal St, Chalmers Ave, and Chicon St, per Plaza Saltillo TOD § 3.3.3); and *TOD Local Streets* (all other streets within the Station Area, per Plaza Saltillo TOD § 3.3.4). All three street types are subject to the same utility undergrounding standard: utility lines underground from building to property line; ROW utilities placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable. Where overhead utilities remain, the building must be set back so no portion is within 10 feet of the energized conductor; street trees must also maintain a 10-foot setback from the conductor (Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.b). Confirm Plaza Saltillo applicability by the TOD zoning designation and the regulating plan notation on the cover sheet.\n    14→\n    15→**Regulating Plan Districts — North Burnet/Gateway (NBG):** The NBG Zoning District covers approximately 2,300 acres in north-central Austin (primarily the Burnet Road corridor and the Stonelake/Capital of Texas area). It also supersedes LDC Subchapter E. NBG § 3.3.2.B.4.a imposes utility undergrounding requirements for all street frontage types in the district — not gated by the frontage street's NBG roadway type classification (CTC, PPC, etc.). The requirement is identical in text: all utility lines underground from building to property line; ROW utilities underground or relocated to the rear to the maximum extent practicable as determined by the Director. NBG § 3.3.2.B.4.b also requires the 10-foot overhead utility zone. Confirm NBG applicability from the Zoning field in facts.md (any NBG subdistrict designation: TOD, CMU, NMU, NR, WMU, or CI) or the NBG Regulating Plan notation on the cover sheet.\n    16→\n    17→**Hill Country Roadway Corridors:** LDC § 25-2-1125 requires all on-site utilities to be underground (unless otherwise required by the utility provider) within Hill Country Roadway Corridors. A Hill Country Roadway Corridor is land within the City's zoning jurisdiction located within 1,000 feet of any of the five designated Hill Country Roadways: Loop 360 (US 290W to US 183), RM 620 (SH 71 to Anderson Mill Rd), RM 2222 (Highland Hills Dr to RM 620), RM 2244 (Loop 360 to SH 71), and Southwest Parkway (full length) (LDC § 25-2-1103). This requirement does NOT apply to development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West (LDC § 25-2-1104(A)). Corridor membership for sites not directly fronting a Hill Country Roadway requires either a City corridor map lookup or a proximity measurement; the applicant's cover sheet or environmental calculations should indicate Hill Country Roadway corridor status. This is a distinct, site-specific requirement — not the same as the Subchapter E CTC/Urban Roadway framework.\n    18→\n    19→**Planting Zone Prohibition (Austin Water):** UCM § 2.9.1.C prohibits Austin Water (AW) mains, services, and appurtenances from being placed within the ROW planting zone associated with street trees. Root barriers shall be placed on all sides of the planting zone where AW mains/services are located, positioned no closer than 7 feet from the tree trunk. AW utilities must be placed no closer than 2 feet from the root barrier. The minimum width of a \"Street Tree Utility Gap\" — the unobstructed space between adjacent planting zones — is 8 feet (measured between root barriers). This prohibition applies exclusively to: (a) the ROW planting zone (not private property planting beds), (b) Austin Water infrastructure specifically (not all utility types). If no street trees exist in the ROW, no planting zone exists, and this prohibition does not apply.\n    20→\n    21→**Transformer Pad and Clearances:** UCM § 1.5.2.6 requires the customer to furnish and install a concrete pad for AE pad-mounted transformers; pad dimensions are determined by AE Design based on customer-submitted AutoCAD site drawings — no universal fixed dimension (such as \"10×10 feet\") exists in the UCM text. UCM § 1.10.4 specifies minimum clearances around pad-mounted equipment: hot-stick use area (padlocked side) — 10 feet from any adjacent building or structure; non-operator sides — 3 feet from removable ventilated fence; operator/control sides — 5 feet from removable ventilated fence; all sides of oil-filled equipment — 20-foot radial clearance to fire escape/stairs; pads must be within 6 feet of a parking or traffic area to support AE truck access; pads must be at least 2 feet from back of sidewalks. No covering, building, or structure is permitted above the pad or its minimum clearance area. All padmount equipment must be on the customer's property — not in the public ROW or dedicated parkland.\n    22→\n    23→**AE Facility Access and Relocation:** UCM § 1.3.16 requires truck access to AE equipment with a minimum 12-foot horizontal width and 16-foot vertical clearance, on a surface supporting 72,180 lbs (32,530 lbs max rear axle), with a minimum 20 ft × 35 ft set-up area with 35-foot vertical clearance in front of equipment. UCM § 1.3.15 requires AE facilities to be installed at the front of the property or adjacent to a street or paved surface; rear lot line construction is permitted only where permanent paved alleys or AE-truck-accessible roadways exist. Under UCM § 1.3.19, AE will relocate facilities at the customer's expense only where feasible; AE will NOT relocate distribution lines from one property to an adjacent property without written consent of the receiving property owner — obtaining that consent and any required easement is the customer's responsibility.\n    24→\n    25→**Utility Easement Licensing:** Two separate licensing regimes apply depending on easement type. Items placed within *private AE easements* (easements granted on customer's private property) require a License Agreement from AE Public Involvement before installation, per UCM § 1.10.10.3. Permanent structures, swimming pools, and buildings are prohibited in AE easements regardless of licensing; fences are permissible only if removable for AE crew/equipment access; 8-foot minimum clearance is required around all pedestals and subsurface AE vaults/manholes. Items placed within *public utility easements* or public ROW require a license from the City Manager per § 14-11-41. Plans showing structures, paving, or landscaping within recorded easement boundaries without notation of a License Agreement or Chapter 14-11 license are deficient.\n    26→\n    27→**Table B Connectivity Measures (Utility Routing in Parking Areas):** LDC Subchapter E § 2.3.1.B.2 establishes a menu of connectivity improvement options (Table B), one of which is routing internal utility lines in drive aisles or Internal Circulation Routes rather than under parking areas. Table B applies only to sites subject to § 2.3.1: sites with net area ≥ 3 acres in nonresidential zoning districts (NO, P, LO, GO, CR, LR, GR, W/LO, CS, CS-1, CH, IP, MI, LI, R&D), or any zoning/size where surface parking is located between the building and the principal street. General Subchapter E exemptions apply (AG/AV/TN districts; UNO projects; single-family uses; industrial uses not on CTCs; interior remodeling). Subject sites must select at least 2 Table B options (3 options if surface parking exceeds 125% of formerly required parking). Routing utilities under parking stalls constitutes a deficiency only if the site has selected this specific Table B option but failed to implement it, or if routing utilities under parking prevents the site from satisfying its minimum Table B option count through other options.\n    28→\n    29→**Key Thresholds:**\n    30→- Overhead utility zone: no portion of building within 10 feet of energized conductor where overhead utilities remain on CTC, Urban Roadway, Plaza Saltillo TOD, or NBG frontages (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.b; Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.b; NBG 3.3.2.B.4.b)\n    31→- Hill Country Roadway Corridor: within 1,000 feet of Loop 360, RM 620, RM 2222, RM 2244, or Southwest Parkway (LDC § 25-2-1103); US 183/US 290 West exception excludes development within 1,000 feet of those cross-roads (LDC § 25-2-1104(A))\n    32→- Root barrier minimum setback from tree trunk: 7 feet (UCM § 2.9.1.C)\n    33→- AW utility clearance from root barrier: 2 feet (UCM § 2.9.1.C)\n    34→- Street Tree Utility Gap minimum width: 8 feet between root barriers (UCM § 2.9.1.C)\n    35→- Transformer clearance (hot-stick side): 10 feet from any adjacent building or structure (UCM § 1.10.4)\n    36→- Transformer clearance (non-operator sides): 3 feet from removable ventilated fence (UCM § 1.10.4)\n    37→- Transformer pad truck access: within 6 feet of a parking or traffic area (UCM § 1.10.4)\n    38→- AE truck access path: 12-ft horizontal width, 16-ft vertical clearance; 20 × 35 ft set-up area (UCM § 1.3.16)\n    39→- Table B minimum selections: 2 options (3 if surface parking >125% of required) (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.B.2)\n    40→\n    41→**Exceptions:**\n    42→- Hill Country underground requirement does not apply to development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West (LDC § 25-2-1104(A))\n    43→- Alternative/utility-compatible trees may be used beneath (rather than offset from) overhead utilities on CTC lots ≤120 feet deep (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.c); same exception available in Plaza Saltillo TOD (Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.c — also available when meeting all requirements would set the building facade ≥30 feet beyond the curb face)\n    44→- AE easement fences are permissible if removable for AE access; permanent structures/swimming pools/buildings are prohibited (UCM § 1.10.10.2)\n    45→- AE will not relocate lines to an adjacent property without written consent of the receiving property owner (UCM § 1.3.19)\n    46→- UCM § 2.9.6 provides a waiver pathway for projects that cannot meet UCM Section 2 requirements — this is an applicant resolution pathway, not a reviewer-flagged deficiency\n    47→\n    48→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    49→- Building-to-property-line undergrounding: Plans show overhead service lines crossing from the building to the property line; designers sometimes assume overhead is acceptable if the utility provider operates overhead in the vicinity, but Subchapter E § 2.2.2.B.3.a and parallel regulating plan provisions apply regardless\n    50→- ROW undergrounding: Existing overhead pole lines in the ROW shown as \"to remain\" without a maximum-extent-practicable analysis or rear-of-site relocation alternative; common pattern is no annotation on overhead utility lines in the ROW planting zone\n    51→- Planting zone violations: Utility plan shows AW mains or services routed through root barrier lines (within the planting zone) rather than through the Utility Gap between zones\n    52→- Easement licensing: Site plan shows retaining walls, paving, or landscaping within recorded AE or public utility easement boundaries without any notation of a License Agreement from AE or a City Manager license\n    53→- Transformer clearance: Pad shown within 5 feet of a non-masonry wall, or no AE-truck-accessible path (6-foot access to a paved traffic area) to the pad; or pad shown in public ROW\n    54→- Transformer easement: Transformer pad area not encumbered by a dedicated AE easement shown on the utility plan\n    55→- Utility routing in parking: Underground conduit shown crossing perpendicular through parking stall striping on utility/paving plan overlay, on a site subject to Table B\n    56→\n    57→## Code References\n    58→\n    59→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Utility undergrounding, overhead utility zone, and alternative tree provisions for Core Transit Corridor frontages\n    60→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Urban Roadway utility undergrounding (incorporates CTC standard at § 2.2.2.B.3 by reference)\n    61→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.B.2 — Table B connectivity measures; utility routing in drive aisles rather than under parking areas\n    62→- LDC § 25-2-1103 — Hill Country Roadway Corridor definition: five named roadways and 1,000-foot corridor boundary\n    63→- LDC § 25-2-1104(A) — Exception to Hill Country requirements for development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West\n    64→- LDC § 25-2-1125 — On-site utilities must be underground in Hill Country Roadway Corridors\n    65→- Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3 — Utility undergrounding on the TOD Core Transit Corridor (E. 7th Street) within the Plaza Saltillo Station Area\n    66→- Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.3.A.3 — Utility undergrounding on TOD Pedestrian Priority Streets within the Plaza Saltillo Station Area\n    67→- Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.4.A.3 — Utility undergrounding on TOD Local Streets within the Plaza Saltillo Station Area\n    68→- NBG 3.3.2.B.4 — Utility undergrounding for all frontage street types within the NBG Zoning District\n    69→- UCM § 1.3.15 — AE front lot line construction requirement; truck accessibility standard for AE facilities\n    70→- UCM § 1.3.16 — Access to AE facilities: truck access dimensions and set-up area requirements\n    71→- UCM § 1.3.19 — Relocation of AE facilities: process, conditions, and consent requirements\n    72→- UCM § 1.5.2.6 — Pad-mounted transformer requirements for commercial secondary voltage service\n    73→- UCM § 1.10.4 — Clearances from AE padmount equipment and distribution vaults\n    74→- UCM § 1.10.10.2 — Fences and structures in AE utility easements\n    75→- UCM § 1.10.10.3 — Landscaping in AE utility easements; License Agreement requirement from AE Public Involvement\n    76→- UCM § 2.9.1.A.1 — Utility easement minimum width (15 feet or twice the depth of the infrastructure, whichever is greater); AW infrastructure outside the ROW must be within a dedicated utility easement; easement boundaries must not conflict with proposed public ROW\n    77→- UCM § 2.9.1.C — AW utility prohibition within ROW planting zones associated with street trees; root barrier and Utility Gap requirements\n    78→- UCM § 5.2.2 — Downtown Rail Corridor Permit requirement for construction operations within the East-West Downtown 4th Street Rail Corridor\n    79→- § 14-11-41 — License for private use of public property (public utility easements and public ROW)\n    80→- ECM Section 4 — Underground storage tank separation from utility easements\n    81→\n    82→## Documents to Review\n    83→\n    84→- **Electrical/Utility Site Plan** — Verify underground vs. overhead designation, routing paths relative to prohibited zones (ponds, planting zones, parkland), and corridor frontage compliance\n    85→- **Site Plan / Paving Plan** — Check utility routing relative to parking spaces and drive aisles; verify utilities are not shown crossing through or under stalls\n    86→- **Planting and Landscape Plan** — Confirm no AW utility infrastructure is shown within designated ROW planting zones or utility exclusion zones; verify root barrier placement and Street Tree Utility Gap widths\n    87→- **ROW Improvement Plans** — Verify existing and proposed utility disposition within public right-of-way; check for required relocations and abandonments\n    88→- **Utility Easement Exhibit / Plat** — Confirm utility easement boundaries do not encroach into proposed ROW; verify items within easements are licensed per UCM § 1.10.10.3 or § 14-11-41 as applicable\n    89→- **Grading and Drainage Plans** — Check for utility routing under proposed detention or retention ponds\n    90→- **Demolition Plans** — Confirm abandoned utilities with clearance conflicts are removed; verify public utilities in ROW are terminated at property line\n    91→- **Austin Energy Coordination Documentation** — Confirm transformer pad placement, AE easement, clearances per UCM § 1.10.4, and power pole relocation coordination letters\n    92→\n    93→## Checklist Items\n    94→\n    95→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    96→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    97→| EL-2.1 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line as required along a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway frontage | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.a | Only when site fronts one of the 20 enumerated CTC segments or an Urban Roadway under LDC Subchapter E AND is not within the Plaza Saltillo TOD or NBG district (which have parallel requirements under their own plans — see EL-2.5 through EL-2.8). Check: principal street classification on cover sheet; confirm no superseding regulating plan applies. |\n    98→| EL-2.2 | All on-site utility lines are not underground; no documentation from the utility provider requiring an exception | LDC § 25-2-1125 | Only when site is within a Hill Country Roadway Corridor (within 1,000 feet of Loop 360, RM 620, RM 2222, RM 2244, or Southwest Parkway per LDC § 25-2-1103). Exception: this requirement does not apply to development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West (LDC § 25-2-1104(A)). Check: Hill Country Roadway corridor maps or applicability notation on site cover sheet; measure or verify site distance from named roadway ROW. |\n    99→| EL-2.3 | Utility lines within the ROW are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable along a Core Transit Corridor frontage | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 | Only when site fronts one of the 20 enumerated CTC segments under LDC Subchapter E AND is not within the Plaza Saltillo TOD or NBG district. Check: corridor designation maps and site address relative to CTC segment limits (segment endpoints are determinative — Burnet Road qualifies only from 45th St to Anderson Ln; South Congress only north of Stassney Ln; Lamar Blvd only from Banyon St to Ben White Blvd; etc.). |\n   100→| EL-2.4 | Utility lines within the ROW are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable along an Urban Roadway frontage | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when site fronts an Urban Roadway (within the Subchapter E boundary polygon, not CTC or Highway) AND is not within the Plaza Saltillo TOD or NBG district, and overhead utilities are present in the ROW. Check: street classification on cover sheet or plans; utility site plan for overhead lines in ROW frontage. |\n   101→| EL-2.5 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site, along the TOD Core Transit Corridor (E. 7th Street) within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area | Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3 | Only when site is within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area AND fronts E. 7th Street (the TOD Core Transit Corridor within the Station Area). Check: Plaza Saltillo TOD applicability on cover sheet or TOD zoning designation; confirm E. 7th Street frontage via address or site plan. |\n   102→| EL-2.6 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site, along a TOD Pedestrian Priority Street within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area | Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.3.A.3 | Only when site is within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area AND fronts E. 5th Street, E. 6th Street, Waller Street, Comal Street, Chalmers Avenue, or Chicon Street. Check: Plaza Saltillo TOD applicability on cover sheet; match frontage street name against the six designated TOD Pedestrian Priority Streets shown on Figure 3-4. |\n   103→| EL-2.7 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site, along a TOD Local Street within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area | Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.4.A.3 | Only when site is within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area AND fronts a TOD Local Street (any street within the Station Area not designated as a TOD Core Transit Corridor or TOD Pedestrian Priority Street per Figure 3-4). Check: Plaza Saltillo TOD applicability; confirm frontage is not E. 7th St (TCTC) or one of the six TPPS streets. |\n   104→| EL-2.8 | Utility lines within the NBG Zoning District are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable | NBG 3.3.2.B.4.a | Only when site is within the NBG Zoning District. Undergrounding applies to all frontage street types in the district — not limited to NBG Core Transit Corridors or Pedestrian Priority Collectors. Check: Zoning field for an NBG subdistrict designation (any of: TOD, CMU, NMU, NR, WMU, or CI prefix); confirm NBG Regulating Plan is listed on cover sheet. |\n   105→| EL-2.9 | A portion of the building is located within a 10-foot radius of an energized overhead conductor where overhead utilities have not been fully removed or undergrounded | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.b | Only when overhead utilities remain after proposed improvements on a CTC, Urban Roadway, Plaza Saltillo TOD, or NBG frontage. Check: utility site plan and building footprint relative to overhead line location. Note: Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.b also requires street trees to maintain a 10-foot setback from the energized conductor; NBG 3.3.2.B.4.b contains the identical 10-foot zone requirement applicable to NBG sites. Exception: on CTC or Plaza Saltillo TOD lots with depth ≤120 feet, utility-compatible alternative trees may be used beneath the overhead utilities. |\n   106→| EL-2.10 | Utilities are routed underneath a proposed detention or retention pond | Reviewer Convention | Only when detention or retention ponds are proposed. Check: grading/drainage plan and utility site plan overlay for utility routing beneath pond footprints. |\n   107→| EL-2.11 | Austin Water mains, services, or appurtenances are proposed within the designated ROW planting zone associated with street trees | UCM § 2.9.1.C | Only when street trees in the public ROW create a designated planting zone with root barrier boundaries. The prohibition applies to the ROW planting zone only (not private property planting beds) and to AW infrastructure specifically (not all utility types). If no ROW street trees are present, no planting zone exists and this item does not apply. Check: landscape plan for ROW planting zone boundaries (root barriers positioned minimum 7 feet from tree trunk); utility plan for AW mains or services within those boundaries. A minimum 8-foot-wide Street Tree Utility Gap must be maintained between adjacent planting zones. |\n   108→| EL-2.12 | A utility exclusion planting zone labeled on plans along a specific street frontage contains Austin Water mains, services, or appurtenances | UCM § 2.9.1.C | Only when a utility exclusion planting zone is explicitly labeled or delineated on landscape or utility plans along a street frontage. Check: landscape plan boundary markings and utility site plan overlay for AW infrastructure within the labeled zone. |\n   109→| EL-2.13 | Transformers or utility boxes are located within parkland boundaries; utilities must be removed or the parkland boundary reconfigured to exclude them | UCM § 1.10.4 | Only when site is adjacent to or includes parkland. Check: parkland boundaries on site plan vs. utility equipment locations. All padmount/vault/niche equipment must be on the customer's property — not in public ROW or dedicated parkland. |\n   110→| EL-2.14 | New utilities are routed through or adjacent to a proposed light rail corridor without UROP consultation or, for the downtown 4th Street corridor, without a required Downtown Rail Corridor Permit | UCM § 5.2.2 | Only when site is adjacent to or crossed by a designated light rail guideway alignment. Cannot be determined from plans alone — flag for human verification unless Capital Metro coordination letters or a Downtown Rail Corridor Permit are included in the submittal. For the East-West Downtown 4th Street Rail Corridor (between IH-35 and the Seaholm District, per UCM Figure 5-1), UCM § 5.2.2 requires a Downtown Rail Corridor Permit before ROW excavation permits are issued. For all other Project Connect light rail alignments, the Utility Rules of Practice (UROP) for Light Rail Transit Program consultation is required. Check: Project Context/Facts for proximity to light rail alignments; presence of coordination letters. |\n   111→| EL-2.15 | A utility easement boundary encroaches into proposed public right-of-way; conflicting easement has not been relocated or an approved modification documented | UCM § 2.9.1.A.1 | Only when utility easements and ROW dedications are both proposed or existing. Check: plat or easement exhibit vs. proposed ROW lines. |\n   112→| EL-2.16 | Items placed within a utility easement (structures, paving, landscaping, retaining walls) have not been licensed as required | UCM § 1.10.10.3; § 14-11-41 | Only when non-utility items are proposed or shown within recorded utility easements. Check: easement exhibit and site plan for encroachments. For items within private AE easements (on customer's property): a License Agreement from AE Public Involvement is required per UCM § 1.10.10.3. For items within public utility easements or public ROW: a license from the City Manager is required per § 14-11-41. Permanent structures, swimming pools, and buildings are prohibited in AE easements regardless of licensing; fences are permissible only if removable for AE access. |\n   113→| EL-2.17 | Private utilities located in public right-of-way have not been relocated to private property or within a dedicated private utility easement | Reviewer Convention | Only when private utilities are shown within existing or proposed public ROW. Check: ROW improvement plans and utility site plan. |\n   114→| EL-2.18 | Abandoned utilities that create clearance conflicts with proposed improvements have not been removed | Reviewer Convention | Only when abandoned utility infrastructure is present on site or in ROW. Check: demolition plan and utility site plan for abandoned vs. active lines. |\n   115→| EL-2.19 | All public or franchise utility facilities and existing service connections in the ROW have not been removed, abandoned, plugged, capped, or otherwise terminated at the ROW/property line boundary by the respective utility companies prior to construction | Reviewer Convention | Only when existing public or franchise utilities occupy the ROW of proposed improvements. Check: demolition/ROW improvement plans vs. existing utility locations. |\n   116→| EL-2.20 | Underground storage tanks are not maintained at adequate distance from utility easement boundaries | ECM Section 4 | Only when underground storage tanks are proposed or existing on site. Check: site plan for tank location relative to utility easement boundaries. |\n   117→| EL-2.21 | Power pole relocation has not been coordinated with Austin Energy; relocated pole location does not meet clearance, access, or front-lot-line requirements | UCM § 1.3.19; UCM § 1.3.15; UCM § 1.3.16 | Only when power pole relocation is required or proposed. Check: utility site plan and Austin Energy coordination letters. AE will not relocate lines to an adjacent property without written consent of the receiving property owner — obtaining consent and easement is the customer's responsibility (UCM § 1.3.19). New pole location must be AE-truck-accessible per UCM § 1.3.16 (12-ft wide, 16-ft vertical clearance access; 20×35 ft set-up area) and must normally be at the front of the property or adjacent to a paved surface per UCM § 1.3.15. |\n   118→| EL-2.22 | Transformer pad is not sized per AE Design specifications, does not satisfy required clearances, is not accessible to AE service trucks, or lacks a dedicated AE easement encumbering the pad area | UCM § 1.5.2.6; UCM § 1.10.4 | Only when a transformer pad is proposed or required. Check: utility site plan for pad location, AE easement boundaries, and clearances per UCM § 1.10.4 (hot-stick side: 10 ft from any adjacent building; non-operator sides: 3 ft from removable ventilated fence; operator/control sides: 5 ft from removable ventilated fence; pad within 6 ft of parking/traffic area for truck access; 2 ft from back of sidewalks). Pad dimensions are specified by AE Design based on customer's AutoCAD drawings — no universal fixed dimension is established in UCM text. Confirm pad is on customer's property (not in public ROW); confirm AE easement encumbers the pad. |\n   119→| EL-2.23 | Tree wells have not been provided with an electrical outlet for seasonal lighting | Reviewer Convention | Only when tree wells are proposed in pedestrian or streetscape areas. Check: landscape plan and electrical site plan for outlet provisions at tree well locations. |\n   120→| EL-2.24 | Electric, telecommunications, or other internal utility lines are routed through or directly under parking spaces rather than in drive aisles or an alternate approved location, on a site that has selected the Table B utility-routing option or has not otherwise satisfied the Table B minimum option count | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.B.2 | Only when utility lines cross a parking area AND the site is subject to LDC Subchapter E § 2.3.1 (sites with net area ≥ 3 acres in nonresidential zoning districts, OR any zoning where surface parking is between the building and the principal street; subject to general Subchapter E exemptions). This is a deficiency only if: (a) the utility-in-drive-aisles option was selected as one of the required Table B measures but not implemented; OR (b) the site has not satisfied the minimum Table B option count (2 options; 3 if surface parking exceeds 125% of formerly required) through other available options. Check: § 2.3.1 applicability thresholds; utility site plan overlaid on parking/paving plan; Table B compliance documentation. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8937ea6-a85f-452c-ba14-5b8849922a4d","uuid":"0f43ae15-697b-4f14-b80a-2855e4b7d6cf","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/2.md","content":"# Underground Utility Routing and Placement\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers requirements governing where utility lines must be placed underground, which locations prohibit underground utility routing entirely, and how utility lines must be horizontally routed relative to specific site features such as parking areas, ponds, planting zones, parkland, and light rail corridors. It applies whenever utilities are proposed, relocated, or abandoned as part of a site development or infrastructure project.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\nAustin's utility undergrounding requirements operate through multiple parallel regulatory frameworks. The applicable framework depends on the site's location, street frontage classification, and whether a superseding regulating plan applies. The review agent must identify which framework governs before evaluating specific deficiencies.\n\n**LDC Subchapter E Framework (CTC and Urban Roadway Sites):** For sites subject to LDC Subchapter E (Design Standards and Mixed Use) and not within a superseding regulating plan district, two roadway types trigger utility undergrounding requirements. *Core Transit Corridors (CTCs)* are the following 20 specifically enumerated roadway segments (segment limits are determinative — only the listed portion qualifies): South First Street (north of Ben White Blvd), East Seventh Street (west of Pleasant Valley Rd), East Fifth Street (I-35 to Pleasant Valley Rd), West Fifth Street (Guadalupe to Mopac), East Sixth Street (I-35 to Pleasant Valley Rd), West Sixth Street (Guadalupe to Pressler), West 35th/38th Street (Mopac to Guadalupe), Airport Boulevard (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Lane (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Road (east of Robert E. Lee Dr), Burnet Road (45th St to Anderson Ln only), South Congress Avenue (north of Stassney Ln only), Guadalupe Street, Lamar Boulevard (Banyon St to Ben White Blvd only), Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (Pearl St to Airport Blvd), Riverside Drive (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Road (51st St to US 290), 51st Street (Cameron to Manor Rd), Gaston Place (Westminster to Wellington), and Briarcliff Boulevard (Berkman to Westminster). On a corner lot, the CTC designation takes priority as the principal street. *Urban Roadways* are all roads within the Subchapter E boundary polygon that are not CTCs or Highways — the boundary runs roughly: 183 from Burnet to Hwy 71 → Hwy 71 to Loop 1 → Loop 1 to Lake Austin → Lake Austin to Exposition → Exposition to 35th → 35th to Loop 1 → Loop 1 (38th to RM 2222) → RM 2222 to Mesa → Mesa to Spicewood Springs → Spicewood Springs to 360 → 360 to Great Hills Trail → Great Hills Trail to 183 → 183 to Braker → Braker to Burnet → Burnet back to 183. For both CTC and Urban Roadway sites under Subchapter E, LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.a (CTC) and § 2.2.3.B.3 (Urban Roadway, via cross-reference to § 2.2.2.B.3) require: (a) all utility lines underground from building to property line; and (b) ROW utility lines placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable. Where overhead utilities remain behind the curb, no portion of the building may be within a 10-foot radius of the energized conductor (§ 2.2.2.B.3.b). Exception for short lots: on CTC lots with depth ≤120 feet where overhead utilities remain, utility-compatible alternative tree species may be used beneath (rather than offset from) the overhead utilities (§ 2.2.2.B.3.c).\n\n**Regulating Plan Districts — Plaza Saltillo TOD:** The Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area is a mapped district centered on the Plaza Saltillo MetroRail Station, bounded roughly by Rosewood Ave/E. 8th St (north), Chalmers Ave/Chicon St (east), E. 2nd St/Cesar Chavez (south), and IH-35/San Marcos St (west). It supersedes LDC Subchapter E in full for all properties within its boundary. Within the district, streets are classified as: *TOD Core Transit Corridor* (E. 7th Street only, per Plaza Saltillo TOD § 3.3.2); *TOD Pedestrian Priority Streets* (E. 5th St, E. 6th St, Waller St, Comal St, Chalmers Ave, and Chicon St, per Plaza Saltillo TOD § 3.3.3); and *TOD Local Streets* (all other streets within the Station Area, per Plaza Saltillo TOD § 3.3.4). All three street types are subject to the same utility undergrounding standard: utility lines underground from building to property line; ROW utilities placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable. Where overhead utilities remain, the building must be set back so no portion is within 10 feet of the energized conductor; street trees must also maintain a 10-foot setback from the conductor (Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.b). Confirm Plaza Saltillo applicability by the TOD zoning designation and the regulating plan notation on the cover sheet.\n\n**Regulating Plan Districts — North Burnet/Gateway (NBG):** The NBG Zoning District covers approximately 2,300 acres in north-central Austin (primarily the Burnet Road corridor and the Stonelake/Capital of Texas area). It also supersedes LDC Subchapter E. NBG § 3.3.2.B.4.a imposes utility undergrounding requirements for all street frontage types in the district — not gated by the frontage street's NBG roadway type classification (CTC, PPC, etc.). The requirement is identical in text: all utility lines underground from building to property line; ROW utilities underground or relocated to the rear to the maximum extent practicable as determined by the Director. NBG § 3.3.2.B.4.b also requires the 10-foot overhead utility zone. Confirm NBG applicability from the Zoning field in facts.md (any NBG subdistrict designation: TOD, CMU, NMU, NR, WMU, or CI) or the NBG Regulating Plan notation on the cover sheet.\n\n**Hill Country Roadway Corridors:** LDC § 25-2-1125 requires all on-site utilities to be underground (unless otherwise required by the utility provider) within Hill Country Roadway Corridors. A Hill Country Roadway Corridor is land within the City's zoning jurisdiction located within 1,000 feet of any of the five designated Hill Country Roadways: Loop 360 (US 290W to US 183), RM 620 (SH 71 to Anderson Mill Rd), RM 2222 (Highland Hills Dr to RM 620), RM 2244 (Loop 360 to SH 71), and Southwest Parkway (full length) (LDC § 25-2-1103). This requirement does NOT apply to development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West (LDC § 25-2-1104(A)). Corridor membership for sites not directly fronting a Hill Country Roadway requires either a City corridor map lookup or a proximity measurement; the applicant's cover sheet or environmental calculations should indicate Hill Country Roadway corridor status. This is a distinct, site-specific requirement — not the same as the Subchapter E CTC/Urban Roadway framework.\n\n**Planting Zone Prohibition (Austin Water):** UCM § 2.9.1.C prohibits Austin Water (AW) mains, services, and appurtenances from being placed within the ROW planting zone associated with street trees. Root barriers shall be placed on all sides of the planting zone where AW mains/services are located, positioned no closer than 7 feet from the tree trunk. AW utilities must be placed no closer than 2 feet from the root barrier. The minimum width of a \"Street Tree Utility Gap\" — the unobstructed space between adjacent planting zones — is 8 feet (measured between root barriers). This prohibition applies exclusively to: (a) the ROW planting zone (not private property planting beds), (b) Austin Water infrastructure specifically (not all utility types). If no street trees exist in the ROW, no planting zone exists, and this prohibition does not apply.\n\n**Transformer Pad and Clearances:** UCM § 1.5.2.6 requires the customer to furnish and install a concrete pad for AE pad-mounted transformers; pad dimensions are determined by AE Design based on customer-submitted AutoCAD site drawings — no universal fixed dimension (such as \"10×10 feet\") exists in the UCM text. UCM § 1.10.4 specifies minimum clearances around pad-mounted equipment: hot-stick use area (padlocked side) — 10 feet from any adjacent building or structure; non-operator sides — 3 feet from removable ventilated fence; operator/control sides — 5 feet from removable ventilated fence; all sides of oil-filled equipment — 20-foot radial clearance to fire escape/stairs; pads must be within 6 feet of a parking or traffic area to support AE truck access; pads must be at least 2 feet from back of sidewalks. No covering, building, or structure is permitted above the pad or its minimum clearance area. All padmount equipment must be on the customer's property — not in the public ROW or dedicated parkland.\n\n**AE Facility Access and Relocation:** UCM § 1.3.16 requires truck access to AE equipment with a minimum 12-foot horizontal width and 16-foot vertical clearance, on a surface supporting 72,180 lbs (32,530 lbs max rear axle), with a minimum 20 ft × 35 ft set-up area with 35-foot vertical clearance in front of equipment. UCM § 1.3.15 requires AE facilities to be installed at the front of the property or adjacent to a street or paved surface; rear lot line construction is permitted only where permanent paved alleys or AE-truck-accessible roadways exist. Under UCM § 1.3.19, AE will relocate facilities at the customer's expense only where feasible; AE will NOT relocate distribution lines from one property to an adjacent property without written consent of the receiving property owner — obtaining that consent and any required easement is the customer's responsibility.\n\n**Utility Easement Licensing:** Two separate licensing regimes apply depending on easement type. Items placed within *private AE easements* (easements granted on customer's private property) require a License Agreement from AE Public Involvement before installation, per UCM § 1.10.10.3. Permanent structures, swimming pools, and buildings are prohibited in AE easements regardless of licensing; fences are permissible only if removable for AE crew/equipment access; 8-foot minimum clearance is required around all pedestals and subsurface AE vaults/manholes. Items placed within *public utility easements* or public ROW require a license from the City Manager per § 14-11-41. Plans showing structures, paving, or landscaping within recorded easement boundaries without notation of a License Agreement or Chapter 14-11 license are deficient.\n\n**Table B Connectivity Measures (Utility Routing in Parking Areas):** LDC Subchapter E § 2.3.1.B.2 establishes a menu of connectivity improvement options (Table B), one of which is routing internal utility lines in drive aisles or Internal Circulation Routes rather than under parking areas. Table B applies only to sites subject to § 2.3.1: sites with net area ≥ 3 acres in nonresidential zoning districts (NO, P, LO, GO, CR, LR, GR, W/LO, CS, CS-1, CH, IP, MI, LI, R&D), or any zoning/size where surface parking is located between the building and the principal street. General Subchapter E exemptions apply (AG/AV/TN districts; UNO projects; single-family uses; industrial uses not on CTCs; interior remodeling). Subject sites must select at least 2 Table B options (3 options if surface parking exceeds 125% of formerly required parking). Routing utilities under parking stalls constitutes a deficiency only if the site has selected this specific Table B option but failed to implement it, or if routing utilities under parking prevents the site from satisfying its minimum Table B option count through other options.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- Overhead utility zone: no portion of building within 10 feet of energized conductor where overhead utilities remain on CTC, Urban Roadway, Plaza Saltillo TOD, or NBG frontages (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.b; Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.b; NBG 3.3.2.B.4.b)\n- Hill Country Roadway Corridor: within 1,000 feet of Loop 360, RM 620, RM 2222, RM 2244, or Southwest Parkway (LDC § 25-2-1103); US 183/US 290 West exception excludes development within 1,000 feet of those cross-roads (LDC § 25-2-1104(A))\n- Root barrier minimum setback from tree trunk: 7 feet (UCM § 2.9.1.C)\n- AW utility clearance from root barrier: 2 feet (UCM § 2.9.1.C)\n- Street Tree Utility Gap minimum width: 8 feet between root barriers (UCM § 2.9.1.C)\n- Transformer clearance (hot-stick side): 10 feet from any adjacent building or structure (UCM § 1.10.4)\n- Transformer clearance (non-operator sides): 3 feet from removable ventilated fence (UCM § 1.10.4)\n- Transformer pad truck access: within 6 feet of a parking or traffic area (UCM § 1.10.4)\n- AE truck access path: 12-ft horizontal width, 16-ft vertical clearance; 20 × 35 ft set-up area (UCM § 1.3.16)\n- Table B minimum selections: 2 options (3 if surface parking >125% of required) (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.B.2)\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- Hill Country underground requirement does not apply to development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West (LDC § 25-2-1104(A))\n- Alternative/utility-compatible trees may be used beneath (rather than offset from) overhead utilities on CTC lots ≤120 feet deep (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.c); same exception available in Plaza Saltillo TOD (Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.c — also available when meeting all requirements would set the building facade ≥30 feet beyond the curb face)\n- AE easement fences are permissible if removable for AE access; permanent structures/swimming pools/buildings are prohibited (UCM § 1.10.10.2)\n- AE will not relocate lines to an adjacent property without written consent of the receiving property owner (UCM § 1.3.19)\n- UCM § 2.9.6 provides a waiver pathway for projects that cannot meet UCM Section 2 requirements — this is an applicant resolution pathway, not a reviewer-flagged deficiency\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Building-to-property-line undergrounding: Plans show overhead service lines crossing from the building to the property line; designers sometimes assume overhead is acceptable if the utility provider operates overhead in the vicinity, but Subchapter E § 2.2.2.B.3.a and parallel regulating plan provisions apply regardless\n- ROW undergrounding: Existing overhead pole lines in the ROW shown as \"to remain\" without a maximum-extent-practicable analysis or rear-of-site relocation alternative; common pattern is no annotation on overhead utility lines in the ROW planting zone\n- Planting zone violations: Utility plan shows AW mains or services routed through root barrier lines (within the planting zone) rather than through the Utility Gap between zones\n- Easement licensing: Site plan shows retaining walls, paving, or landscaping within recorded AE or public utility easement boundaries without any notation of a License Agreement from AE or a City Manager license\n- Transformer clearance: Pad shown within 5 feet of a non-masonry wall, or no AE-truck-accessible path (6-foot access to a paved traffic area) to the pad; or pad shown in public ROW\n- Transformer easement: Transformer pad area not encumbered by a dedicated AE easement shown on the utility plan\n- Utility routing in parking: Underground conduit shown crossing perpendicular through parking stall striping on utility/paving plan overlay, on a site subject to Table B\n\n## Code References\n\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Utility undergrounding, overhead utility zone, and alternative tree provisions for Core Transit Corridor frontages\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Urban Roadway utility undergrounding (incorporates CTC standard at § 2.2.2.B.3 by reference)\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.B.2 — Table B connectivity measures; utility routing in drive aisles rather than under parking areas\n- LDC § 25-2-1103 — Hill Country Roadway Corridor definition: five named roadways and 1,000-foot corridor boundary\n- LDC § 25-2-1104(A) — Exception to Hill Country requirements for development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West\n- LDC § 25-2-1125 — On-site utilities must be underground in Hill Country Roadway Corridors\n- Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3 — Utility undergrounding on the TOD Core Transit Corridor (E. 7th Street) within the Plaza Saltillo Station Area\n- Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.3.A.3 — Utility undergrounding on TOD Pedestrian Priority Streets within the Plaza Saltillo Station Area\n- Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.4.A.3 — Utility undergrounding on TOD Local Streets within the Plaza Saltillo Station Area\n- NBG 3.3.2.B.4 — Utility undergrounding for all frontage street types within the NBG Zoning District\n- UCM § 1.3.15 — AE front lot line construction requirement; truck accessibility standard for AE facilities\n- UCM § 1.3.16 — Access to AE facilities: truck access dimensions and set-up area requirements\n- UCM § 1.3.19 — Relocation of AE facilities: process, conditions, and consent requirements\n- UCM § 1.5.2.6 — Pad-mounted transformer requirements for commercial secondary voltage service\n- UCM § 1.10.4 — Clearances from AE padmount equipment and distribution vaults\n- UCM § 1.10.10.2 — Fences and structures in AE utility easements\n- UCM § 1.10.10.3 — Landscaping in AE utility easements; License Agreement requirement from AE Public Involvement\n- UCM § 2.9.1.A.1 — Utility easement minimum width (15 feet or twice the depth of the infrastructure, whichever is greater); AW infrastructure outside the ROW must be within a dedicated utility easement; easement boundaries must not conflict with proposed public ROW\n- UCM § 2.9.1.C — AW utility prohibition within ROW planting zones associated with street trees; root barrier and Utility Gap requirements\n- UCM § 5.2.2 — Downtown Rail Corridor Permit requirement for construction operations within the East-West Downtown 4th Street Rail Corridor\n- § 14-11-41 — License for private use of public property (public utility easements and public ROW)\n- ECM Section 4 — Underground storage tank separation from utility easements\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Electrical/Utility Site Plan** — Verify underground vs. overhead designation, routing paths relative to prohibited zones (ponds, planting zones, parkland), and corridor frontage compliance\n- **Site Plan / Paving Plan** — Check utility routing relative to parking spaces and drive aisles; verify utilities are not shown crossing through or under stalls\n- **Planting and Landscape Plan** — Confirm no AW utility infrastructure is shown within designated ROW planting zones or utility exclusion zones; verify root barrier placement and Street Tree Utility Gap widths\n- **ROW Improvement Plans** — Verify existing and proposed utility disposition within public right-of-way; check for required relocations and abandonments\n- **Utility Easement Exhibit / Plat** — Confirm utility easement boundaries do not encroach into proposed ROW; verify items within easements are licensed per UCM § 1.10.10.3 or § 14-11-41 as applicable\n- **Grading and Drainage Plans** — Check for utility routing under proposed detention or retention ponds\n- **Demolition Plans** — Confirm abandoned utilities with clearance conflicts are removed; verify public utilities in ROW are terminated at property line\n- **Austin Energy Coordination Documentation** — Confirm transformer pad placement, AE easement, clearances per UCM § 1.10.4, and power pole relocation coordination letters\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-2.1 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line as required along a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway frontage | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.a | Only when site fronts one of the 20 enumerated CTC segments or an Urban Roadway under LDC Subchapter E AND is not within the Plaza Saltillo TOD or NBG district (which have parallel requirements under their own plans — see EL-2.5 through EL-2.8). Check: principal street classification on cover sheet; confirm no superseding regulating plan applies. |\n| EL-2.2 | All on-site utility lines are not underground; no documentation from the utility provider requiring an exception | LDC § 25-2-1125 | Only when site is within a Hill Country Roadway Corridor (within 1,000 feet of Loop 360, RM 620, RM 2222, RM 2244, or Southwest Parkway per LDC § 25-2-1103). Exception: this requirement does not apply to development within 1,000 feet of US 183 or US 290 West (LDC § 25-2-1104(A)). Check: Hill Country Roadway corridor maps or applicability notation on site cover sheet; measure or verify site distance from named roadway ROW. |\n| EL-2.3 | Utility lines within the ROW are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable along a Core Transit Corridor frontage | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 | Only when site fronts one of the 20 enumerated CTC segments under LDC Subchapter E AND is not within the Plaza Saltillo TOD or NBG district. Check: corridor designation maps and site address relative to CTC segment limits (segment endpoints are determinative — Burnet Road qualifies only from 45th St to Anderson Ln; South Congress only north of Stassney Ln; Lamar Blvd only from Banyon St to Ben White Blvd; etc.). |\n| EL-2.4 | Utility lines within the ROW are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable along an Urban Roadway frontage | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when site fronts an Urban Roadway (within the Subchapter E boundary polygon, not CTC or Highway) AND is not within the Plaza Saltillo TOD or NBG district, and overhead utilities are present in the ROW. Check: street classification on cover sheet or plans; utility site plan for overhead lines in ROW frontage. |\n| EL-2.5 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site, along the TOD Core Transit Corridor (E. 7th Street) within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area | Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3 | Only when site is within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area AND fronts E. 7th Street (the TOD Core Transit Corridor within the Station Area). Check: Plaza Saltillo TOD applicability on cover sheet or TOD zoning designation; confirm E. 7th Street frontage via address or site plan. |\n| EL-2.6 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site, along a TOD Pedestrian Priority Street within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area | Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.3.A.3 | Only when site is within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area AND fronts E. 5th Street, E. 6th Street, Waller Street, Comal Street, Chalmers Avenue, or Chicon Street. Check: Plaza Saltillo TOD applicability on cover sheet; match frontage street name against the six designated TOD Pedestrian Priority Streets shown on Figure 3-4. |\n| EL-2.7 | Utility lines are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site, along a TOD Local Street within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area | Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.4.A.3 | Only when site is within the Plaza Saltillo TOD Station Area AND fronts a TOD Local Street (any street within the Station Area not designated as a TOD Core Transit Corridor or TOD Pedestrian Priority Street per Figure 3-4). Check: Plaza Saltillo TOD applicability; confirm frontage is not E. 7th St (TCTC) or one of the six TPPS streets. |\n| EL-2.8 | Utility lines within the NBG Zoning District are not placed underground from the building to the property line and/or ROW utility lines are not placed underground or relocated to the rear of the site to the maximum extent practicable | NBG 3.3.2.B.4.a | Only when site is within the NBG Zoning District. Undergrounding applies to all frontage street types in the district — not limited to NBG Core Transit Corridors or Pedestrian Priority Collectors. Check: Zoning field for an NBG subdistrict designation (any of: TOD, CMU, NMU, NR, WMU, or CI prefix); confirm NBG Regulating Plan is listed on cover sheet. |\n| EL-2.9 | A portion of the building is located within a 10-foot radius of an energized overhead conductor where overhead utilities have not been fully removed or undergrounded | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3.b | Only when overhead utilities remain after proposed improvements on a CTC, Urban Roadway, Plaza Saltillo TOD, or NBG frontage. Check: utility site plan and building footprint relative to overhead line location. Note: Plaza Saltillo TOD 3.3.2.A.3.b also requires street trees to maintain a 10-foot setback from the energized conductor; NBG 3.3.2.B.4.b contains the identical 10-foot zone requirement applicable to NBG sites. Exception: on CTC or Plaza Saltillo TOD lots with depth ≤120 feet, utility-compatible alternative trees may be used beneath the overhead utilities. |\n| EL-2.10 | Utilities are routed underneath a proposed detention or retention pond | Reviewer Convention | Only when detention or retention ponds are proposed. Check: grading/drainage plan and utility site plan overlay for utility routing beneath pond footprints. |\n| EL-2.11 | Austin Water mains, services, or appurtenances are proposed within the designated ROW planting zone associated with street trees | UCM § 2.9.1.C | Only when street trees in the public ROW create a designated planting zone with root barrier boundaries. The prohibition applies to the ROW planting zone only (not private property planting beds) and to AW infrastructure specifically (not all utility types). If no ROW street trees are present, no planting zone exists and this item does not apply. Check: landscape plan for ROW planting zone boundaries (root barriers positioned minimum 7 feet from tree trunk); utility plan for AW mains or services within those boundaries. A minimum 8-foot-wide Street Tree Utility Gap must be maintained between adjacent planting zones. |\n| EL-2.12 | A utility exclusion planting zone labeled on plans along a specific street frontage contains Austin Water mains, services, or appurtenances | UCM § 2.9.1.C | Only when a utility exclusion planting zone is explicitly labeled or delineated on landscape or utility plans along a street frontage. Check: landscape plan boundary markings and utility site plan overlay for AW infrastructure within the labeled zone. |\n| EL-2.13 | Transformers or utility boxes are located within parkland boundaries; utilities must be removed or the parkland boundary reconfigured to exclude them | UCM § 1.10.4 | Only when site is adjacent to or includes parkland. Check: parkland boundaries on site plan vs. utility equipment locations. All padmount/vault/niche equipment must be on the customer's property — not in public ROW or dedicated parkland. |\n| EL-2.14 | New utilities are routed through or adjacent to a proposed light rail corridor without UROP consultation or, for the downtown 4th Street corridor, without a required Downtown Rail Corridor Permit | UCM § 5.2.2 | Only when site is adjacent to or crossed by a designated light rail guideway alignment. Cannot be determined from plans alone — flag for human verification unless Capital Metro coordination letters or a Downtown Rail Corridor Permit are included in the submittal. For the East-West Downtown 4th Street Rail Corridor (between IH-35 and the Seaholm District, per UCM Figure 5-1), UCM § 5.2.2 requires a Downtown Rail Corridor Permit before ROW excavation permits are issued. For all other Project Connect light rail alignments, the Utility Rules of Practice (UROP) for Light Rail Transit Program consultation is required. Check: Project Context/Facts for proximity to light rail alignments; presence of coordination letters. |\n| EL-2.15 | A utility easement boundary encroaches into proposed public right-of-way; conflicting easement has not been relocated or an approved modification documented | UCM § 2.9.1.A.1 | Only when utility easements and ROW dedications are both proposed or existing. Check: plat or easement exhibit vs. proposed ROW lines. |\n| EL-2.16 | Items placed within a utility easement (structures, paving, landscaping, retaining walls) have not been licensed as required | UCM § 1.10.10.3; § 14-11-41 | Only when non-utility items are proposed or shown within recorded utility easements. Check: easement exhibit and site plan for encroachments. For items within private AE easements (on customer's property): a License Agreement from AE Public Involvement is required per UCM § 1.10.10.3. For items within public utility easements or public ROW: a license from the City Manager is required per § 14-11-41. Permanent structures, swimming pools, and buildings are prohibited in AE easements regardless of licensing; fences are permissible only if removable for AE access. |\n| EL-2.17 | Private utilities located in public right-of-way have not been relocated to private property or within a dedicated private utility easement | Reviewer Convention | Only when private utilities are shown within existing or proposed public ROW. Check: ROW improvement plans and utility site plan. |\n| EL-2.18 | Abandoned utilities that create clearance conflicts with proposed improvements have not been removed | Reviewer Convention | Only when abandoned utility infrastructure is present on site or in ROW. Check: demolition plan and utility site plan for abandoned vs. active lines. |\n| EL-2.19 | All public or franchise utility facilities and existing service connections in the ROW have not been removed, abandoned, plugged, capped, or otherwise terminated at the ROW/property line boundary by the respective utility companies prior to construction | Reviewer Convention | Only when existing public or franchise utilities occupy the ROW of proposed improvements. Check: demolition/ROW improvement plans vs. existing utility locations. |\n| EL-2.20 | Underground storage tanks are not maintained at adequate distance from utility easement boundaries | ECM Section 4 | Only when underground storage tanks are proposed or existing on site. Check: site plan for tank location relative to utility easement boundaries. |\n| EL-2.21 | Power pole relocation has not been coordinated with Austin Energy; relocated pole location does not meet clearance, access, or front-lot-line requirements | UCM § 1.3.19; UCM § 1.3.15; UCM § 1.3.16 | Only when power pole relocation is required or proposed. Check: utility site plan and Austin Energy coordination letters. AE will not relocate lines to an adjacent property without written consent of the receiving property owner — obtaining consent and easement is the customer's responsibility (UCM § 1.3.19). New pole location must be AE-truck-accessible per UCM § 1.3.16 (12-ft wide, 16-ft vertical clearance access; 20×35 ft set-up area) and must normally be at the front of the property or adjacent to a paved surface per UCM § 1.3.15. |\n| EL-2.22 | Transformer pad is not sized per AE Design specifications, does not satisfy required clearances, is not accessible to AE service trucks, or lacks a dedicated AE easement encumbering the pad area | UCM § 1.5.2.6; UCM § 1.10.4 | Only when a transformer pad is proposed or required. Check: utility site plan for pad location, AE easement boundaries, and clearances per UCM § 1.10.4 (hot-stick side: 10 ft from any adjacent building; non-operator sides: 3 ft from removable ventilated fence; operator/control sides: 5 ft from removable ventilated fence; pad within 6 ft of parking/traffic area for truck access; 2 ft from back of sidewalks). Pad dimensions are specified by AE Design based on customer's AutoCAD drawings — no universal fixed dimension is established in UCM text. Confirm pad is on customer's property (not in public ROW); confirm AE easement encumbers the pad. |\n| EL-2.23 | Tree wells have not been provided with an electrical outlet for seasonal lighting | Reviewer Convention | Only when tree wells are proposed in pedestrian or streetscape areas. Check: landscape plan and electrical site plan for outlet provisions at tree well locations. |\n| EL-2.24 | Electric, telecommunications, or other internal utility lines are routed through or directly under parking spaces rather than in drive aisles or an alternate approved location, on a site that has selected the Table B utility-routing option or has not otherwise satisfied the Table B minimum option count | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.B.2 | Only when utility lines cross a parking area AND the site is subject to LDC Subchapter E § 2.3.1 (sites with net area ≥ 3 acres in nonresidential zoning districts, OR any zoning where surface parking is between the building and the principal street; subject to general Subchapter E exemptions). This is a deficiency only if: (a) the utility-in-drive-aisles option was selected as one of the required Table B measures but not implemented; OR (b) the site has not satisfied the minimum Table B option count (2 options; 3 if surface parking exceeds 125% of formerly required) through other available options. Check: § 2.3.1 applicability thresholds; utility site plan overlaid on parking/paving plan; Table B compliance documentation. |","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":120}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732668,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018VzsUwjJoMgNQ9Z3RfwwdH","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n    10→\n    11→Customer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n    12→\n    13→The Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n    14→\n    15→**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n    16→\n    17→**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n    18→\n    19→Service-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n    20→\n    21→**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n    22→\n    23→AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n    24→\n    25→**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n    26→\n    27→Transmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n    28→\n    29→**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n    30→\n    31→Permanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n    32→\n    33→**Key Thresholds:**\n    34→- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n    35→- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n    36→- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n    37→- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n    38→- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n    39→- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n    40→- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n    41→- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n    42→- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n    43→- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n    44→- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n    45→\n    46→**Exceptions:**\n    47→- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n    48→- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n    49→- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n    50→\n    51→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    52→- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n    53→- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n    54→- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n    55→- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n    56→- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n    57→- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n    58→\n    59→## Code References\n    60→\n    61→- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n    62→- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n    63→- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n    64→- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n    65→- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n    66→- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n    67→- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n    68→- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n    69→- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n    70→- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n    71→- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n    72→- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n    73→- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n    74→- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n    75→- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n    76→- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n    77→- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n    78→- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n    79→- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n    80→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n    81→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n    82→- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n    83→\n    84→*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n    85→- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    86→- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n    87→\n    88→## Documents to Review\n    89→\n    90→- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n    91→- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n    92→- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n    93→- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n    94→- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n    95→- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n    96→- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n    97→- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n    98→\n    99→## Checklist Items\n   100→\n   101→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   102→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   103→| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n   104→| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n   105→| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n   106→| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n   107→| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n   108→| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n   109→| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n   110→| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n   111→| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n   112→| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n   113→| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n   114→| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n   115→| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n   116→| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n   117→| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n   118→| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n   119→| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n   120→| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n   121→| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n   122→| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n   123→| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n   124→| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n   125→| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n   126→| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n   127→| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n   128→| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n   129→| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n   130→| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a849ead6-fbef-4e89-ab5d-465127c74861","uuid":"8fb2ef14-9d58-4510-bd1c-eb3abbbf09b6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/1.md","content":"# Overhead and Vertical Electrical Clearances\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers minimum clearance requirements between structures, utilities, and energized overhead electrical conductors, as well as minimum separations between utilities at crossings and in parallel routing. It also encompasses vertical clearance requirements for staging areas, maintenance access drives, and work zone operations during construction. Reviewers apply these checks whenever overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to a site, utility crossings are proposed, or construction activities occur near electrical infrastructure.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Permanent Clearances from AE Overhead Distribution Lines (UCM 1.10.3)**\n\nCustomer facilities — defined broadly as any customer-side structure, improvement, or installation, including buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, and vaults — must maintain two simultaneous and independent clearances from AE overhead distribution conductors: (1) a **7-foot 6-inch horizontal sky-to-ground clearance** measured from the outermost (outside) conductor, and (2) a **15-foot-radius clearance** from overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors. No customer facility may be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities — this prohibition is absolute and separate from the numerical clearance requirements. Where a vault is proposed near overhead power lines, the applicant must provide AE with a survey showing proximity to AE primary voltage facilities, and Austin Energy written clearance approval must be obtained and included in the project file. Both clearance thresholds must be satisfied simultaneously; an installation that clears 7.5 feet horizontally but falls within the 15-foot radial zone still violates UCM 1.10.3.\n\nThe Land Development Code separately requires an **overhead utility zone** — a 10-foot-radius buffer from the energized conductor — to be graphically delineated on site plans when overhead utilities remain on the site. No building or structure may encroach into this zone. This LDC requirement applies **only when the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway** (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 for CTCs; extended to Urban Roadways by LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3). **Core Transit Corridors** are 20 enumerated street segments: South First St (north of Ben White), E 7th St (west of Pleasant Valley), E/W 5th St (various limits), E/W 6th St (various limits), W 35th/38th St corridor, Airport Blvd (Lamar to I-35), Anderson Ln (Burnet to Mopac), Barton Springs Rd (east of Robert E. Lee), Burnet Rd (45th to Anderson), South Congress Ave (north of Stassney), Guadalupe St, Lamar Blvd (Banyon to Ben White), MLK Jr. Blvd (Pearl to Airport), Riverside Dr (Lamar to E. Ben White/Hwy 71), Cameron Rd (51st to US-290), 51st St (Cameron to Manor), Gaston Place, and Briarcliff Blvd. **Urban Roadways** are roads within a boundary polygon roughly bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th St/Loop 1/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/183/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. The UCM 1.10.3 clearances (7.5-ft horizontal and 15-ft radius) apply universally to all customer facilities regardless of street type; the LDC 10-ft zone is an additional, street-type-dependent requirement.\n\n**Critical measurement rule:** All clearances must be measured from the **outermost (outside) conductor**, not from the utility pole centerline. On three-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet offset from the pole center. Plans showing clearance dimensions anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. This is the single most common measurement error on AE clearance exhibits.\n\n**Service-Drop Clearances and Customer Service Entrance Facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1 and 1.10.6.2)**\n\nService-drop conductors must maintain minimum vertical clearance from final grade based on the area type: **12 ft** over pedestrian-only residential areas; **16 ft** over residential driveways, or parking lots and alleys not subject to truck traffic, and commercial services over pedestrian areas; **18 ft** over roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, and any other area subject to truck traffic. An area is \"subject to truck traffic\" when commercial freight, delivery, refuse trucks, or other heavy commercial vehicles are reasonably expected based on land use. Public streets always require 18 ft. Alleys and parking lots may require either 16-ft or 18-ft depending on use — when ambiguous, apply 18-ft as the conservative default. Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack) must also maintain a **10-foot minimum horizontal clearance** from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop (UCM 1.10.6.1).\n\n**AE Underground Facility Separations and Utility Easement Routing (UCM 1.10.5.A and UCM 2.9.1.A.3)**\n\nAE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) require a **12-inch minimum clearance** — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at **perpendicular crossings only**; fuel lines and high-pressure gas (≥60 psi) require 36-inch minimum separation. This rule governs perpendicular crossings only; parallel routing of other utilities alongside AE underground conduit requires a separate written joint-trench agreement from AE. Non-electrical utilities may cross a public utility easement (PUE) or electrical easement only perpendicularly or up to 45° from perpendicular — **parallel routing within easement boundaries is prohibited at all times** (UCM 2.9.1.A.3). Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains are: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3). For trenchless crossings under water lines, the minimum clearance is the greater of (a) two times the excavation diameter/span or (b) 36 inches (UCM 2.9.2.B.17). UCM 2.9.2.B.19 governs bedding and backfill material (CLSM) for open-cut crossings under water mains — it is a material specification, not an additional vertical clearance standard; the separation standard for open-cut crossings is in UCM 2.9.1.B.3.\n\n**Transmission Line Requirements (UCM 1.14 series)**\n\nTransmission lines are electric facilities at **60 kV or higher** (UCM §1.17.0 Glossary). Austin Energy operates transmission at 69 kV, 138 kV, and 345 kV. Physical indicators: substantially taller poles or lattice towers, long insulators (≥3 ft), conductors spread widely apart, shield wire at top. Transmission clearance envelopes are complex, site-specific, three-dimensional shapes that cannot be reduced to a table; Austin Energy engineering must evaluate specific locations (UCM 1.14.3.1). OSHA working clearances during construction, by voltage: **10 ft for 69 kV; 11.33 ft for 138 kV; 15.33 ft for 345 kV** (UCM 1.14.3.2). Transmission circuits cannot be de-energized for nearby construction, even temporarily. Key construction requirements (UCM 1.14.9): pre-construction safety meeting with AE ≥48 hours before commencement; barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within construction area; warning signs under overhead facilities; no materials storage or spoils within transmission easement; tower cranes and temporary structures positioned so they could not fall within 20 ft of transmission facility. The transmission ROW access corridor must be maintained at 25 ft wide with **16-ft minimum vertical clearance** along the full ROW length (UCM 1.14.4).\n\n**SCM Staging Areas and Maintenance Access Drives (DCM 1.2.4.E)**\n\nPermanent SCM staging areas (required for COA-maintained SCMs and residential single-family/duplex development SCMs) must maintain **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e). Maintenance access drives (required for all residential development SCMs, all COA-maintained SCMs, and commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade exceeds 20%) must maintain the same **14-ft minimum vertical clearance** (DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii). Both requirements include vegetation heights at maturity. Neither requirement applies if combined total SCM storage volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND maximum ponding depth ≤3 ft.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- 10 ft: Overhead utility zone radius from energized conductor (LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — CTC/Urban Roadway only)\n- 7.5 ft: Horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead conductors for all customer facilities (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 15 ft: Radial clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors (UCM 1.10.3)\n- 10 ft: Minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles for customer service entrance facilities (UCM 1.10.6.1); OSHA working clearance from lines ≤50 kV (UCM 1.10.2)\n- 18 ft: Service-drop clearance over truck traffic areas (UCM 1.10.6.2)\n- 16 ft: Service-drop clearance over residential driveways / non-truck alleys/lots (UCM 1.10.6.2); transmission ROW access corridor vertical clearance (UCM 1.14.4)\n- 12 in: AE underground facility separation from other utilities at perpendicular crossings (UCM 1.10.5.A)\n- 18 in / 12 in: Water/wastewater main crossing separation — AW main above / AW main below (UCM 2.9.1.B.3)\n- 36 in (or 2× excavation diameter): Trenchless crossing clearance from water line (UCM 2.9.2.B.17)\n- 14 ft: SCM staging area and maintenance access drive vertical clearance (DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e; DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii)\n- 24 in: ROW facility proximity requiring AULCC ROW assignment change approval (§ 14-11-180(C))\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- SCM staging area and maintenance access drive clearance requirements exempt when combined pond volume ≤5,000 cu ft AND ponding depth ≤3 ft\n- AW may approve reduced crossing vertical separations under constrained conditions (6-in vs. 12-in; 12-in vs. 18-in)\n- LDC overhead utility zone (10-ft radius) applies only on Core Transit Corridors and Urban Roadways; UCM 1.10.3 permanent clearances apply universally regardless of street type\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Clearances dimensioned from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost conductor\n- Plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter in the project file\n- Proposed utility within 24 inches of existing ROW facility with no AULCC approval or deviation documentation\n- Non-electrical utilities running parallel within an electrical or PUE easement boundary\n- Maintenance access drive shown with tree canopy overhead where canopy at maturity would reduce clearance below 14 ft, or no clearance labeled\n- AE underground conduit in joint trench with other utilities without written AE agreement\n\n## Code References\n\n- UCM 1.10.2 — Working clearances from energized overhead electric utility lines; incorporates OSHA 1910.333 (10-ft from lines ≤50 kV for unqualified persons) and Texas H&S Code § 752.004 requirements by reference\n- UCM 1.10.3 — Permanent clearances from AE overhead distribution lines: 7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground and 15-ft radius for customer facilities; AE survey and written clearance requirements; delineation of clearance envelopes; clearances measured from overhead conductors (not from utility pole centerline)\n- UCM 1.10.5.A — 12-inch minimum clearance from AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) to other utilities at perpendicular crossings; 36-inch from high-pressure gas lines (≥60 psi); joint trench with other utilities requires written AE agreement\n- UCM 1.10.6.1 — 10-foot minimum horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines (other than service drop) for customer service entrance facilities\n- UCM 1.10.6.2 — Service-drop conductor clearances: 12-ft (pedestrian-only), 16-ft (residential driveways and non-truck areas), 18-ft (truck traffic areas)\n- UCM 1.14.3.1 — Transmission line permanent clearance envelope (site-specific, three-dimensional; requires AE engineering determination)\n- UCM 1.14.3.2 — Working clearances from transmission lines during construction (10-ft for 69 kV; 11.33-ft for 138 kV; 15.33-ft for 345 kV)\n- UCM 1.14.4 — Transmission ROW access corridor requirements: 25-ft wide, 16-ft minimum vertical clearance along full ROW length\n- UCM 1.14.9 — Construction requirements near transmission lines: 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting; barricades 10-ft from transmission structures; warning signs; prohibition on materials/spoils in easement; 20-ft crane buffer\n- UCM 1.3.16 — Minimum 16-ft vertical clearance and 12-ft horizontal width for AE truck access routes to AE equipment; 35-ft vertical clearance for equipment set-up areas\n- UCM 2.9.1.A.3 — Prohibition on parallel routing within PUE or utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular or up to 45° from perpendicular crossings permitted\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.2 — Minimum 5-foot horizontal separation between AW infrastructure and all other non-AW infrastructure\n- UCM 2.9.1.B.3 — Vertical separation standards for crossings with AW water/wastewater mains: 12-inch minimum when AW main is above; 18-inch minimum when AW main is below\n- UCM 2.9.1.D — Encasement piping specifications for utilities crossing under roadways and structures\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.17 — Trenchless crossing under water lines: minimum clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 inches\n- UCM 2.9.2.B.19 — Bedding and backfill specification (CLSM material) for open-cut crossings under water mains; this is a material specification, not a vertical clearance standard\n- UCM 2.9.4.G.5 — Large-diameter cleanout requirements for wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter; maximum 14-foot depth\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM maintenance access drives\n- DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e — 14-ft minimum vertical clearance for SCM staging areas\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3 — Overhead utility zone: 10-ft radius from energized conductor, required along Core Transit Corridors; must be delineated in addition to all other sidewalk zones\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 — Extends Core Transit Corridor overhead utility zone standards to Urban Roadways\n- § 14-11-180(C) — AULCC ROW assignment change approval required when installing a facility within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility (Austin City Code, Title 14)\n\n*External references confirmed via UCM cross-reference (not in Bureau):*\n- OSHA 1910.333 — Electrical safety clearances for unqualified persons near live overhead lines; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n- Texas H&S Code § 752.004 — Overhead power line safety clearance requirements; confirmed via UCM 1.10.2\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Utility Plan Sheets** — Overhead utility locations, crossing configurations, easement boundaries, and clearance dimensions from buildings, vaults, FDCs, cleanouts, and storm drain manholes; AE underground conduit routing and joint-trench configurations\n- **Site/Civil Plan Sheets** — Building footprint locations relative to overhead conductor radius zones and delineated overhead utility zones; overhead utility zone graphic shown on plan\n- **Demolition Plan Sheets** — Demolition sequence safety requirements relative to transmission lines; equipment/scaffolding restrictions; warning/caution notes near at-grade and overhead utility appurtenances\n- **Staging and Construction Management Plan** — Staging area and temporary spoils locations relative to utility alignments and transmission easements; vertical clearance dimensions at staging areas and driveway access points; transmission ROW access corridor\n- **General Notes / Construction Specifications** — Field verification requirement for utility separations prior to final acceptance; contractor caution language near at-grade appurtenances\n- **Austin Energy Written Clearance Approval** — Required in project file when vaults are proposed near overhead power lines (UCM 1.10.3)\n- **Austin Energy Survey Documentation** — Required per UCM 1.10.3 when customer facilities are proposed near AE primary voltage facilities; applicant must provide AE with survey showing proximity to AE overhead primary conductors\n- **Texas Gas Services Coordination Documentation** — Required when utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines (2-ft vertical clearance verification; confirm current TGS contact directly)\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-1.1 | Building or structure located within 10-ft radius of energized overhead conductors, or overhead utility zone not graphically delineated on plans | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.2.B.3; LDC Subchapter E, § 2.2.3.B.3 | Only when overhead utilities remain on or adjacent to the site AND the site fronts a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway. CTCs are the 20 enumerated segments (e.g., South Congress Ave north of Stassney, Lamar Blvd from Banyon to Ben White, Guadalupe St, Burnet Rd from 45th to Anderson — full list in definition file). Urban Roadways are roads within the boundary polygon bounded by 183/Hwy 71/Loop 1/Lake Austin/Exposition/35th/RM 2222/Mesa/Spicewood Springs/360/Great Hills Trail/Braker/Burnet, excluding CTCs and Highways. Check: confirm street classification; if applicable, confirm 10-ft radius buffer is graphically shown centered on each energized conductor and no building falls within the zone. UCM 1.10.3 clearances (EL-1.2 and EL-1.3) apply universally regardless of street type. |\n| EL-1.2 | Customer facilities (buildings, parking garages, light poles, signs, billboards, chimneys, antennas, tanks, or other customer installations) not maintaining minimum 7-ft 6-in horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from AE overhead primary, neutral, or secondary conductors | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead electric distribution utilities are present on or adjacent to the site. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimensions from the outside (outermost) conductor to any customer facility edge. Clearance must be from conductor, not pole centerline (see EL-1.5). |\n| EL-1.3 | Customer facilities not maintaining minimum 15-ft radius clearance from AE overhead distribution primary and neutral conductors; or customer facility proposed to be installed under or over AE overhead distribution facilities | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead distribution primary or neutral conductors are present and customer facilities are proposed. Check: utility plan for 15-ft radial clearance from the outside conductor to any customer facility. This threshold applies simultaneously with EL-1.2; both must be satisfied. |\n| EL-1.4 | Vault vertical clearance from top of proposed structure to sag of lowest overhead power line not labeled on plans; or written clearance approval from Austin Energy not obtained or not included in project file | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when a vault is proposed in proximity to overhead power lines. Check: utility plan for labeled vertical clearance dimension from vault top to lowest conductor sag; project file for Austin Energy written clearance approval letter. Violation: plan states \"vault approved by AE Design\" without including the approval letter. |\n| EL-1.5 | Clearance dimensions on plans shown measured from utility pole centerline rather than from the outermost (outside) conductor; clearances appear to comply but are anchored to the pole symbol rather than the conductor position | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when items EL-1.2, EL-1.3, or EL-1.4 apply. Check: verify all clearance dimension lines on the utility plan or clearance exhibit are anchored to the outside conductor. On three-phase or multi-phase configurations, the outside conductor may be several feet from the pole center; pole-anchored dimensions are non-compliant even when stated distances appear adequate. |\n| EL-1.6 | Customer service entrance facilities (service equipment, service conductors, one-point rack, FDC installations, or meter vaults) not maintaining minimum 10-ft horizontal clearance from AE poles and overhead lines other than the service drop; or electrical appurtenances not maintaining 10-ft clearance from live energized overhead lines | UCM 1.10.6.1; UCM 1.10.2 | Only when overhead AE utilities (poles, conductors) are present and customer service entrance facilities, meter vaults, FDCs, or electrical appurtenances are proposed. Check: utility plan for horizontal dimension from service entrance equipment or appurtenances to nearest AE pole or overhead line other than the service drop. |\n| EL-1.7 | Overhead service-drop conductors crossing areas subject to truck traffic (roads, streets, alleys, parking lots, or loading zones with commercial vehicle access) not demonstrating minimum 18-ft vertical clearance from final grade | UCM 1.10.6.2 | Only when overhead service drops cross areas accessible by vehicles. Check: utility plan and site layout for service drop locations and the area type below. Public streets and loading areas always require 18-ft. Residential driveways not subject to truck traffic require 16-ft. Alleys and parking lots require judgment: apply 18-ft unless truck access is affirmatively excluded. Unlabeled or ambiguous areas default to 18-ft. |\n| EL-1.8 | Utilities crossing Texas Gas Service lines not maintaining minimum 2-ft vertical clearance; or coordination with Texas Gas Services not documented in project file | Reviewer Convention | Only when proposed utilities cross Texas Gas Service lines. Check: utility plan for gas crossing locations and project file for TGS coordination documentation confirming 2-ft vertical clearance. Confirm current TGS contact directly; department-provided contact information may be outdated. |\n| EL-1.9 | AE underground facilities (cable, conduit, duct structure, pull-boxes) not maintaining minimum 12-inch clearance — both horizontally and vertically — from other utilities at perpendicular crossings | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when proposed utilities cross AE underground facilities at perpendicular angles. Check: utility plan and crossing details for labeled clearance dimensions between AE underground conduit or cable and crossing utilities. This rule applies only to perpendicular crossings; parallel runs require a separate written joint-trench agreement (see EL-1.10). |\n| EL-1.10 | Non-AE utilities proposed in joint trench (parallel run) with AE underground conduit or cable without written AE joint-trench agreement referenced on plans or in project file | UCM 1.10.5.A | Only when non-AE utilities are shown running parallel to or in the same trench corridor as AE underground conduit or cable. Check: utility plan for any parallel routing of non-AE utilities along AE underground duct banks or cable runs; confirm project file contains or references a written AE joint-trench agreement. |\n| EL-1.11 | Proposed utility in public ROW shown within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility without AULCC ROW assignment change approval documentation; plans show close-proximity utilities with no notation of AULCC authorization | § 14-11-180(C) | Only when utilities are proposed within public ROW. Check: utility plan for any proposed facility located within 24 inches of an existing ROW facility. The requirement is not a flat prohibition but an approval process: AULCC Completeness Letter (ROW assignment change approval) must be in the project file. Violation pattern: utilities shown at close spacing with no acknowledgment of AULCC review or approval. |\n| EL-1.12 | Utility crossing method (trenchless vs. open-cut) not identified on plans or in crossing details, preventing verification of the applicable separation standard (UCM 2.9.2.B.17 for trenchless — clearance = greater of 2× excavation diameter or 36 in; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 for open-cut — 12-in when AW main above; 18-in when AW main below) | UCM 2.9.2.B.17; UCM 2.9.1.B.3 | Only when utility crossings are proposed over or under water, reclaimed water, or wastewater mains. Check: utility plan and crossing detail sheets for crossing method designation; profile drawings for labeled clearance or separation dimensions. |\n| EL-1.13 | Encasement specifications not provided for utilities crossing under roadways, structures, or other barriers; or bored section design criteria missing from crossing detail sheets | UCM 2.9.1.D | Only when utilities are bored or encased under roads, structures, or barriers. Check: utility plan crossing details and notes for encasement material type, dimensions, and longitudinal extent specifications. |\n| EL-1.14 | Large-diameter cleanouts (serving wastewater service lines 8–12 inches in diameter) not maintaining minimum 5-ft horizontal separation from existing utility poles and guy wires | UCM 2.9.1.B.2 | Only when large-diameter cleanouts are proposed. A large-diameter cleanout is required when the wastewater service line is 8\", 10\", or 12\" in diameter (per UCM 2.9.4.G.5); it is located at the property line or within a 5×5 ft easement. Check: utility and drainage plans for cleanout locations relative to utility poles and guy wires; flag any separation less than 5 feet. |\n| EL-1.15 | Storm drain manholes proposed with less than 5-ft horizontal clearance from Austin Energy overhead electric poles | Reviewer Convention | Only when storm drain manholes are proposed near Austin Energy overhead electric poles. Check: drainage and utility plans for manhole-to-pole horizontal dimensions. |\n| EL-1.16 | Non-electrical utilities (water, wastewater, gas, communications, or other non-AE utilities) routed parallel within electrical or public utility easement boundaries; only perpendicular crossings (or up to 45° from perpendicular) are permitted | UCM 2.9.1.A.3 | Only when utilities are proposed within or adjacent to electrical easements or PUEs. Identify easement boundaries from the plat or easement exhibit; easement labels may read \"electric easement,\" \"AE easement,\" \"transmission easement,\" or \"aerial electric easement.\" Check: utility plan for any non-electrical utility running parallel within the easement rather than crossing perpendicularly. |\n| EL-1.17 | Electric utility poles proposed within water easements or within 10 ft of large electric transmission structures | Requires human verification | Only when utility poles are proposed near water easements or electric transmission structures (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV — identifiable by voltage label, lattice towers or substantially taller structures, long insulators, or labeled transmission easement). ⚠️ The specific prohibition previously attributed to UCM 2.9.1.A.1 cannot be confirmed in current code text — that section governs easement width requirements, not pole placement restrictions. Flag for department review to identify the current applicable provision before issuing a deficiency comment. |\n| EL-1.18 | Demolition plan sequence notes do not address required safety clearances from transmission lines: pre-construction safety meeting ≥48 hours before work, barricades erected 10 ft from all transmission structures (including guys and anchors) within the construction area, warning signs under overhead facilities, and prohibition on materials or spoils storage within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9 | Only when demolition activities are proposed near electric transmission lines (69 kV, 138 kV, or 345 kV overhead facilities, or within a labeled transmission easement). Check: demolition plan sequence notes for all four requirements listed in the deficiency. |\n| EL-1.19 | Construction plans do not demonstrate maintenance of a 25-ft-wide access corridor with minimum 16-ft vertical clearance along the full transmission ROW length for the duration of construction | UCM 1.14.4 | Only when construction activities occur adjacent to a transmission ROW or transmission easement. Check: staging and construction plans for a labeled access corridor along the transmission ROW with 25-ft minimum width and 16-ft minimum vertical clearance dimensions. |\n| EL-1.20 | Demolition plan does not include a note requiring contractor to use extreme caution when working near at-grade utility appurtenances | Reviewer Convention | Only when demolition activities are proposed. Check: demolition plan general notes for contractor caution language near at-grade utility appurtenances. |\n| EL-1.21 | Plans do not include a requirement for field verification of all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance | Reviewer Convention | All sites with proposed utilities. Check: general notes or construction specifications on plan set for field verification language. |\n| EL-1.22 | SCM staging area vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft | DCM 1.2.4.E.2.e | Only when permanent SCM staging areas are required: site contains a COA-maintained or residential development (single-family/duplex) SCM with combined total storage volume >5,000 cu ft OR maximum ponding depth >3 ft. Commercial privately-maintained SCMs are not subject to this requirement unless COA-maintained. Check: staging plan or drainage detail sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance from all objects above the staging area. |\n| EL-1.23 | Maintenance access drive vertical clearance from existing and proposed vegetation and all other objects not demonstrated at minimum 14 ft, including vegetation at maturity | DCM 1.2.4.E.1.e.ii | Only when maintenance access drives are present: site contains a qualifying SCM (all residential development SCMs regardless of maintenance type; all COA-maintained SCMs; commercial SCMs where access from ROW or grade between single-family lots exceeds 20%), above the 5,000 cu ft / 3-ft depth exemption thresholds. Check: plan sheets for labeled 14-ft vertical clearance along maintenance access drive corridors; vegetation canopy heights at maturity must be verified against the 14-ft standard. |\n| EL-1.24 | Work zone or construction access driveway below overhead utilities does not demonstrate required vertical clearance: 18 ft where truck traffic is anticipated; 16 ft minimum for driveways affirmatively restricted to non-truck vehicles | UCM 1.10.6.2; UCM 1.3.16 | Only when work zones or construction access points are adjacent to or below overhead service drops or distribution lines. Check: staging/construction plan for labeled clearance dimensions at driveway access locations below overhead lines; apply 18-ft for any area not affirmatively restricted from truck access. Note: a 15'6\" figure used in prior versions of this item cannot be confirmed in current UCM or AEDC. |\n| EL-1.25 | Staging or temporary spoils areas shown overlapping with proposed utility line alignments or placed within the transmission easement | UCM 1.14.9; Reviewer Convention | Only when staging or temporary spoils areas are proposed. Check: staging/construction plan against utility alignment plan for spatial conflicts; the transmission easement boundary must be kept clear of all construction materials and spoils under any circumstances. |\n| EL-1.26 | Overhead electrical clearance zones not graphically delineated on plans: distribution clearance envelope (7.5-ft horizontal sky-to-ground boundary and 15-ft radial zone from outside conductor) and/or transmission clearance envelope (AE-provided site-specific analysis) not shown on site plan or utility plan sheets | UCM 1.10.3; UCM 1.14.3.1 | Only when overhead electric utilities (distribution or transmission) are present on or adjacent to the site. For distribution: check for graphical representation of both clearance envelopes anchored to the outside conductor, not the pole. For transmission: AE-provided clearance analysis or AE transmission review documentation must be present; transmission clearance zones cannot be independently verified from plan data and require AE engineering. |\n| EL-1.27 | Horizontal dashed height limit lines not shown on plans to delineate AE overhead conductor clearance envelope boundaries where overhead utility hazards are present | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present. Check: site plan and utility plan sheets for horizontal height limit line annotations delineating clearance envelope boundaries from overhead conductors. |\n| EL-1.28 | Sloped or stepped dashed height limit lines not shown on plans where overhead utilities are present on sites with non-uniform grade conditions | UCM 1.10.3 | Only when overhead utilities are present on sites with sloped or stepped terrain. Check: grading plan and utility plan sheets for sloped or stepped height limit line annotations that reflect actual grade changes beneath the overhead conductor span. |","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":130}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732725,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01CKxqho2eS62dyttVbb8ZWs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EVYgX6RbHWbfCVQWUqhhCk","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/2.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":22307,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":22307},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f19fb8e-f97f-429a-bf6c-feca3089c5bd","uuid":"2eac1460-3437-4e68-9daf-5915f3138992"}
{"level":30,"time":1776951732878,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"3.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015SHPCS9oEiXdKXfPSHbiLq","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Austin Energy Service and ROW Utility Coordination\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers all coordination requirements with Austin Energy (for electrical service, street lighting, and moonlight tower compliance) and with other ROW utility stakeholders (AULCC, Gattan, telecommunications, dry utilities, and CIP) before a site plan can be approved. It also captures the administrative closeout requirements — ROW utility coordination case closure, the Additional Review Acknowledgement table, street name reservations, and addressing approvals — that gate final ROW Review approval.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Austin Energy Service Coordination.** Austin Energy (AE) is the City of Austin's municipal electric utility serving both the city and portions of the surrounding area. All new development or service modification requires submission of an Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) to the appropriate AE Design business unit along with required documents to obtain an electrical design (AEDC 1.4.2.1). For service ≤350 amps single-phase or ≤225 amps three-phase (non-network), the Service-Only process applies; for larger loads or any service within the downtown Network Area, the Design Services process applies and full AE Design involvement is required (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4). Normally, the ESPA is submitted after DSD approves the site plan and must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A). A Building Service Planning Application (BSPA) — distinct from the ESPA — is required for building permits when the exterior of a structure is modified. For locations where it is uncertain which utility serves the site, an Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirms whether the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area (AEDC 1.4.1). AE Design has sole authority over available service characteristics and voltage at any given location (AEDC 1.3.2). AE owns electric facilities in the public ROW; customers own facilities from the point of service toward the building, except for the meter (AEDC 1.3.5). Before AE begins installation, the customer must grant AE an electrical easement using standard City forms signed by the property owner (AEDC 1.3.6).\n    10→\n    11→**Land Development Review Requirements (AEDC 1.16.0).** All land development applications submitted for Austin Energy review must include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) showing: all public and private easements; all existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, and transformers); permanent structures with footprint and height; final topology (grades, floodplains, detention ponds); and property lines with building setbacks. Plans must demonstrate sufficient clearances for existing and proposed electric facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(2)), dedicate additional easements if insufficient (at owner's expense, with metes and bounds descriptions), demonstrate that the development will not limit AE personnel access to current and proposed facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(4)), identify one point of service and one service voltage (exceptions require AE approval), and show approximate location and space for electric facilities with projected load. When a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, AEDC 1.16.0(8) requires specific plat notes: (i) AE tree pruning rights; (ii) provision for additional easements and access; (iii) owner erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection responsibilities; (iv) clearance maintenance obligation; (v) restriction on permanent structures that would violate NESC clearances within the transmission easement; (vi) 24-hour AE access across the property to transmission easements; (vii) all roads and driveways in the transmission easement built to support ≥48,000 lb tandem axle loads; (viii) 48-hour notice to AE before construction or grading within a transmission easement. Land within transmission easements may not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication (AEDC 1.16.0(9)).\n    12→\n    13→**Required Austin Energy Plan Notes.** All consolidated site plan applications must include four Standard Austin Energy Notes verbatim — changing the required text results in rejection: (1) Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery, and other obstructions to keep easements clear, and AE will comply with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B; (2) the owner/developer must provide AE with any easement and/or access required (beyond those indicated) for overhead and underground electric facilities, and such facilities shall not cause the site to be out of compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8; (3) the owner is responsible for temporary erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and initial tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of proposed overhead electrical facilities, and AE's work area must be included within the limits of construction; (4) the property owner is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines; AE will not render service unless required clearances are maintained; failure costs are charged to the owner. In addition to these four standard notes, AE's land development review process under AEDC 1.16.0(7) requires site-specific plan notes: (a) a note identifying the site as lying within the Austin Energy service area, and (b) a note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current AE Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts.\n    14→\n    15→**Street Lighting.** AE develops a complete lighting installation plan for new residential and commercial developments. Developer conduit and infrastructure responsibilities vary by location type: for new residential subdivisions within city limits, the developer provides conduit, pull string, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs standard poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.4); for commercial areas within city limits, the developer pays all installation costs and provides conduit, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.6); outside city limits in the AE service area, the developer bears all costs including monthly energy charges (AEDC 1.6.5, 1.6.7). All street lighting must meet IESNA spacing and illumination standards. Non-standard poles require developer/AE collaboration and the developer pays the cost difference. For projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary — bounded by Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez St (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W) — Great Streets Chapter 6 lighting standards apply: 8 poles per block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures). All pole placement and spacing requires prior AE Design coordination and, where applicable, Urban Design coordination.\n    16→\n    17→**AULCC and ROW Utility Coordination.** The Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee (AULCC) — referred to as \"ULCC\" in the UCM — administers the ROW utility coordination process. Per UCM 3.2.0, all projects that impact infrastructure located in the public right-of-way must be processed through the AULCC before proceeding to the permitting and construction phase. AULCC review is triggered when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) **300 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW anywhere in the city; (2) **25 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within the **Downtown Austin Project Coordination Zone (DAPCZ)** — roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east (IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street), Oltorf Street/Barton Skyway to the south, Loop 1 (MoPac) to the west, and Enfield Road/Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd to the north; the formal metes-and-bounds boundary is in Code § 14-11-161(7) and includes the boundary streets; (3) **any utility pole relocation**, regardless of linear footage, anywhere in the city; or (4) **water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets** (UCM 2.9.1.B.1). Projects that do not meet any of these thresholds are not subject to AULCC review. Successful AULCC coordination results in an AULCC Completeness Letter (clearance letter) that must be on file before the ROW Review can be marked Approved. The ROW utility coordination case must be closed in the site plan case management system before approval can be issued. Additional external utility clearances required through the ROW coordination process include: Gattan (a specific utility provider — its service territory is not described in Bureau code files; applicability is determined by the presence of Gattan-labeled facilities on utility plan sheets or from AULCC correspondence; requires human verification if unknown), telecommunications providers, dry utility providers, and any active CIP project manager for projects in areas with active capital improvement work.\n    18→\n    19→**Moonlight Towers.** Moonlight towers are specific, fixed-location, City-owned steel lattice towers with supporting guy wires, historically used for citywide arc lighting and concentrated in downtown and central Austin. Two separate regulatory thresholds apply: **(1) 100-foot permit threshold (COA § 14-11-201):** no construction activity may occur within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower without a permit under COA Division 4, Chapter 14-11; construction on private property requires application to the building official; construction in the ROW requires application to the director; the guy wire buffer applies independently from the tower base, so a project can be more than 100 feet from the base but still require a permit if within 100 feet of a guy wire. **(2) 25-foot ROW prohibition (COA § 14-11-205):** no facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; a permittee must also obtain a temporary use of ROW permit to occupy the ROW near a tower. A 24-hour advance notification to Austin Energy before construction near a moonlight tower is an AE operational practice but is not codified in COA §§ 14-11-201 through 14-11-205. Moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files — the review agent cannot determine proximity from plans alone. For projects in downtown or central Austin, moonlight tower proximity items should be flagged for human verification unless plans affirmatively note the absence of nearby towers.\n    20→\n    21→**Administrative Closeout.** Final site plan approval requires: the AULCC Completeness Letter on file; the Additional Review Acknowledgement table completed on the cover sheet or transmittal listing all applicable external reviews and permits (TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, and Planning and Development Review Department approvals) per UCM 2.5.1.F; all proposed street names, including names for private drives, reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before approval; and an addressing plan approved by Address Management Services. **Receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan** — applicants frequently confuse these two documents and reviewers must confirm the addressing plan is approved, not just that an AVL was issued.\n    22→\n    23→**Key Thresholds:**\n    24→- **ESPA service type:** ≤350 A single-phase or ≤225 A three-phase (non-network) → Service-Only process; >350 A single-phase or >225 A three-phase, or any Network Area service → Design Services process with full AE Design involvement (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4)\n    25→- **Continuous load:** >320 A must upgrade to instrument-rated CT service (AEDC 1.4.0)\n    26→- **Moonlight tower permit:** Construction within 100 feet of a tower or its guy wires requires permit under COA § 14-11-201\n    27→- **Moonlight tower ROW prohibition:** No facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower (COA § 14-11-205)\n    28→- **AULCC trigger (citywide):** ≥300 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation (UCM 3.2.0)\n    29→- **AULCC trigger (DAPCZ):** ≥25 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation within DAPCZ (UCM 3.2.0)\n    30→- **AULCC trigger (pole):** Any utility pole relocation, anywhere in the city (UCM 3.2.0)\n    31→- **Great Streets lighting boundary:** MLK Blvd (N) / Lady Bird Lake–Cesar Chavez (S) / IH-35 (E) / Lamar Blvd (W) — 8 poles/block, 24-ft street / 12-ft pedestrian mounting heights (Great Streets Ch. 6)\n    32→\n    33→**Exceptions:**\n    34→- ESPA submission normally occurs after DSD site plan approval, not before; ESPA must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A)\n    35→- Electric Service Availability Letter is required only where there is a question as to which utility serves the site; not required for all projects in the established AE service area (AEDC 1.4.1)\n    36→- Non-standard street light poles are permitted with developer/AE collaboration and developer payment of cost difference\n    37→- Projects below all AULCC thresholds (no pole relocations, <300 LF citywide, <25 LF in DAPCZ, no main assignments) are exempt from AULCC review\n    38→\n    39→**Common Violation Patterns:**\n    40→- Citing LDC § 25-9-173 for the electric service capacity/availability letter requirement — this section does not exist in the current LDC (chapter numbering jumps from 164 to 191 in that division); the correct authority is AEDC 1.4.1 and AEDC 1.3.2\n    41→- Citing COA § 14-11-205 for the 100-foot moonlight tower permit requirement — § 14-11-205 governs only the 25-foot ROW installation prohibition; the 100-foot permit requirement is in COA § 14-11-201\n    42→- Treating AULCC clearance as an \"all sites\" requirement — AULCC review is only triggered when one of the four specific thresholds (linear footage, DAPCZ linear footage, pole relocation, or main assignment) is met; universal application generates false deficiencies for small infill projects\n    43→- Accepting an Address Validation Letter (AVL) as an approved addressing plan — these are distinct documents; the addressing plan must be separately approved\n    44→\n    45→## Code References\n    46→\n    47→- AEDC 1.3.0 — Basic requirements of electric distribution service (customer vs. AE facility ownership, easement grants, access requirements, truck access dimensions)\n    48→- AEDC 1.4.0 — Requesting and obtaining electric distribution service; ESPA submission process and service type thresholds\n    49→- AEDC 1.4.1 — Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirming service area\n    50→- AEDC 1.6.0 — Street lighting standards, developer responsibilities, and IESNA requirements\n    51→- AEDC 1.16.0 — Land development review application requirements for Austin Energy (AutoCAD drawing, clearance demonstration, easement sufficiency, access, point of service, plat notes for transmission easements)\n    52→- Addressing Standards, Introduction — Address Management Services (AMS) is the addressing authority for the City of Austin and Travis County; addressing review occurs during subdivision and site plan review processes; approval required for permitting\n    53→- Addressing Standards, Streets — Private drive, access easement, and street naming criteria; all proposed street names must be reviewed by AMS and reserved before use; named driveways and easements must adhere to Austin and Travis County Street Name Standards\n    54→- COA § 14-11-201 — Permit required for construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or its guy wires\n    55→- COA § 14-11-205 — No facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; temporary use of ROW permit required\n    56→- Great Streets Chapter 6 — Street lighting design standards for projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), Lamar Blvd (W)): 8 poles/block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures)\n    57→- UCM 2.5.1.F — Additional authorizations required for final plan approval (TxDOT, railroad, gas company, TCEQ, water districts, etc.)\n    58→- UCM 3.2.0 — AULCC: designation as ROW utility coordination body; requirement that all projects impacting ROW infrastructure be processed through the AULCC\n    59→- UCM 3.4.0 — Utility coordination process phases (Pre-Design, Design, Final Design, Utility Adjustment)\n    60→- UCM 3.8.0 — Pre-construction and construction meeting requirements\n    61→- General Site Plan Notes — Standard Austin Energy Notes — Four mandatory notes required verbatim on all consolidated site plan applications\n    62→\n    63→## Documents to Review\n    64→\n    65→- **Site/Utility Plan Sheets** — Verify Austin Energy service area note, developer responsibility note for conduit/infrastructure, street light placement and dimensions, signal cabinet representations, utility conflicts in ROW (street lights vs. gas lines, storm inlets, etc.)\n    66→- **Plan Notes Block / General Notes Sheet** — Check for all four Standard Austin Energy Notes (verbatim), moonlight tower permit and notification language, preconstruction meeting requirement note, developer responsibility note for AE conduit standards\n    67→- **Cover Sheet or Transmittal** — Confirm Additional Review Acknowledgement table is complete with all external reviews/approvals (TxDOT, railroad, gas, TCEQ, water districts, etc.) per UCM 2.5.1.F\n    68→- **Approval/Clearance Letters on File** — ESPA approval from Austin Energy, AEU clearance letter, Electric Service Availability Letter (where service provider was uncertain), AULCC Completeness Letter, Gattan clearance, telecommunications provider clearances, dry utility clearances, CIP clearance\n    69→- **ROW Review Case Status** — Confirm utility coordination case is closed before ROW Review can be marked Approved in the Site Plan case\n    70→- **Addressing Records** — Confirm addressing plan has been approved and all street names (including private drives) are reserved with 911 Addressing before site plan approval\n    71→- **Easement Documents** — Verify electrical easements are dedicated using standard City forms where AE facilities are proposed on private property\n    72→\n    73→## Checklist Items\n    74→\n    75→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    76→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    77→| EL-3.1 | Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) not submitted to Austin Energy for review and approval, or ESPA approval not documented in the plan set | AEDC 1.4.2.1 | Only when new or modified electrical service is required. Check: project scope for new construction or electrical service upgrades |\n    78→| EL-3.2 | Austin Energy Utility (AEU) clearance or approval not obtained or not documented | Reviewer Convention | All sites served by Austin Energy. Check: submittal package for AEU clearance letter |\n    79→| EL-3.3 | Electric Service Availability Letter from Austin Energy not provided confirming that the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area; or for locations where the serving utility is uncertain, AE Public Involvement has not been contacted to obtain this letter | AEDC 1.4.1; AEDC 1.3.2 | Only when new electrical service is being obtained from Austin Energy and there is a question as to which electric utility serves the location. Check: project location relative to established AE service territory and ESPA documentation |\n    80→| EL-3.4 | Site plan does not include a note stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area | AEDC 1.16.0(7) | All sites within the Austin Energy service area. Check: plan notes sheet for AE service area note |\n    81→| EL-3.5 | Plans do not include the required developer responsibility note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts | AEDC 1.16.0(7); AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when new Austin Energy electrical infrastructure (conduit, conductors, pull-boxes, foundations) is required. Check: ESPA or electrical plans for new service |\n    82→| EL-3.6 | One or more of the four required Standard Austin Energy Notes is missing or altered from the required verbatim text: (1) AE tree pruning rights in easements; (2) owner/developer obligation to provide additional easements or access for AE facilities; (3) owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines; (4) owner responsibility for maintaining NESC/OSHA/City/state clearances near overhead power lines | General Site Plan Notes — Standard AE Notes | All consolidated site plan applications. Check: general notes sheet for all four required notes verbatim; changing the required text results in rejection |\n    83→| EL-3.7 | Austin Energy approval not obtained for electrical transformer and pull box design prior to construction | AEDC 1.4.2.4 | Only when new or relocated transformers or pull boxes are proposed. Check: utility plan sheets for proposed AE equipment |\n    84→| EL-3.8 | New driveway or site access improvement conflicts with existing Austin Energy power pole guy anchor support and coordination with Austin Energy has not been completed | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when new driveways or access improvements are proposed near existing power poles with guy anchors. Check: utility plan sheets for pole anchor locations adjacent to proposed driveways |\n    85→| EL-3.9 | Plans do not include a note that a preconstruction meeting with Austin Energy is required prior to starting demolition | UCM 3.8.0 | Only when demolition is proposed. Check: demolition plan or general notes for preconstruction meeting requirement |\n    86→| EL-3.10 | Final layout solution and electrical easement recording have not been coordinated with Austin Energy before approval sign-off; easement documents using standard City forms have not been returned to AE before AE begins installation | AEDC 1.16.0(3); AEDC 1.3.6 | Only when new electrical easements are required for proposed AE facilities on private property. Check: easement exhibits and AE design correspondence |\n    87→| EL-3.11 | Land development application submitted to Austin Energy does not include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) with all required content: public/private easements, existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, transformers), permanent structures with footprint and height, final topology, and property lines with building setbacks | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when submitting a land development application to Austin Energy for review. Check: AE submittal package for electronic drawing format compliance and required content |\n    88→| EL-3.12 | Plans do not demonstrate that the proposed development will not limit Austin Energy personnel access to current and proposed electric facilities (e.g., no access path shown to proposed transformers, pull boxes, or overhead line equipment; no truck access dimensions provided for AE equipment areas) | AEDC 1.16.0(4) | Only when development is adjacent to existing or proposed AE electric facilities. Check: site plan and grading plan for AE equipment access routes; verify minimum 12-ft width and 16-ft vertical clearance for truck access per AEDC 1.3.16 |\n    89→| EL-3.13 | Plan notes do not state that a permit from Austin Energy is required for any construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower, per COA § 14-11-201 | COA § 14-11-201 | Only when a moonlight tower or its guy wires is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: utility base map and Austin Energy moonlight tower records; if project is in downtown or central Austin, flag for human verification of proximity — moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files |\n    90→| EL-3.14 | Plan notes do not document Austin Energy's advance notification requirement before construction commences within 100 feet of a moonlight tower; this is an Austin Energy operational practice (not codified in COA § 14-11-205) and plans near moonlight towers should include coordination notes and current AE contact information for pre-construction notification | Austin Energy operational requirement | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: same proximity verification as EL-3.13; note that any phone number shown on plans should be confirmed current with Austin Energy |\n    91→| EL-3.15 | Plans propose installing a facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower, which is prohibited; additionally, no temporary use of ROW permit has been obtained or referenced for ROW occupancy in the vicinity of the tower | COA § 14-11-205 | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area and ROW facility installations are proposed. Check: proposed ROW facility locations vs. any annotated or known moonlight tower locations; flag for human verification if project is in downtown or central Austin and plans show ROW work within potential tower proximity |\n    92→| EL-3.16 | Proposed street light placement in the ROW conflicts with an existing utility (e.g., gas line, storm sewer) and the conflict has not been resolved on the plans | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in or adjacent to the ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for horizontal conflicts between proposed light poles and mapped utilities |\n    93→| EL-3.17 | Traffic signal cabinets shown on plans not updated to reflect current field conditions at affected intersections | Reviewer Convention | Only when traffic signal cabinets are shown on plans. Check: utility or streetscape plan sheets for signal cabinet locations vs. current field layout |\n    94→| EL-3.18 | Street light pole spacing and placement not coordinated with Austin Energy; pole locations not confirmed with AE Design (and with Urban Design where applicable) | AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for evidence of AE coordination on pole spacing |\n    95→| EL-3.19 | Electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans do not extend to all required utility infrastructure locations within the project area; plan coverage ends before reaching the full extent of proposed utility work along the project frontage | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans are part of the plan set and utility infrastructure extends across the project frontage. Check: plan coverage limits vs. full extent of proposed utility work |\n    96→| EL-3.20 | Street lighting labels on plan sheets are duplicated or incorrectly applied (e.g., extra Great Streets light label assigned to a tree well or non-light feature) | Great Streets Chapter 6 | Only when the site is within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (bounded by MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W)) and Great Streets lighting standards apply. Check: confirm project is within Great Streets boundary, then review streetscape plan sheets for label accuracy and duplication |\n    97→| EL-3.21 | Dimensions between street light poles not provided on plan sheets | AEDC 1.16.0(1); AEDC 1.6.0 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for dimensional callouts between pole locations |\n    98→| EL-3.22 | Street light pole placement conflicts with a storm drain inlet or other subsurface stormwater infrastructure, requiring pole relocation | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in close proximity to stormwater inlets. Check: utility plan sheets overlaying proposed pole locations with storm sewer infrastructure |\n    99→| EL-3.23 | AULCC (Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee) clearance not obtained before site plan approval; AULCC Completeness Letter not on file | UCM 3.2.0 | Only when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) 300 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW (citywide); (2) 25 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within DAPCZ (roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east — note: IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary only in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street — Oltorf/Barton Skyway (S), Loop 1/MoPac (W), Enfield Road/MLK Blvd (N); formal metes-and-bounds boundary in Code § 14-11-161(7); boundary streets included); (3) any utility pole relocation, regardless of linear footage; (4) water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets. Check: measure total linear footage of proposed ROW utility work on utility plan sheets; check for any pole relocations; determine if project is within DAPCZ |\n   100→| EL-3.24 | Gattan clearance or approval not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when Gattan infrastructure is present or affected. Check: utility plan sheets for Gattan-labeled facilities in the project area; if unknown, flag for human verification via AULCC correspondence |\n   101→| EL-3.25 | Telecommunications provider clearances not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when telecommunications infrastructure is affected by or located within the project area or ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for telecom facilities |\n   102→| EL-3.26 | Dry utility clearances not obtained from all applicable providers | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when dry utilities (electric, gas, telecom) are present in the ROW or affected by site work. Check: utility plan sheets for dry utility facilities |\n   103→| EL-3.27 | CIP (Capital Improvement Projects) clearance not obtained; a CIP project manager has not been identified or confirmed | Reviewer Convention | Only when a CIP project is active in or adjacent to the project area. Check: CIP project lookup for the project vicinity — requires human verification via City of Austin CIP project management records |\n   104→| EL-3.28 | ROW utility coordination case not closed; ROW Review cannot be marked Approved in the Site Plan case until the utility coordination case is complete | UCM 3.2.0 | All sites with proposed ROW work. Check: ROW Review case status in site plan case management system |\n   105→| EL-3.29 | Additional Review Acknowledgement table not completed on plans with all applicable external reviews, approvals, or permits required for the project (e.g., TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, Planning and Development Review Department approvals) | UCM 2.5.1.F | All sites. Check: cover sheet or transmittal for completed Additional Review Acknowledgement table |\n   106→| EL-3.30 | Proposed street names, including names for private drives, not approved and reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before site plan approval | Addressing Standards, Streets | Only when new streets or private drives are proposed. Check: plan set for any new street names; confirm 911 Addressing reservation status |\n   107→| EL-3.31 | Addressing plan not approved; or consultation meeting with 911 Addressing not scheduled before submittal when no approved addressing plan exists; note that receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan | Addressing Standards, Introduction | Only when new addresses are required for the development. Check: addressing records for an approved addressing plan separate from any AVL issued |\n   108→| EL-3.32 | Transmission easement plat notes missing or incomplete; when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, plans must include all required AEDC 1.16.0(8) plat notes (AE tree pruning rights; additional easement/access provision; erosion control and tree protection responsibilities; clearance maintenance; NESC compliance restrictions on structures; 24-hour AE access to easement; ≥48,000 lb tandem axle road/driveway capacity within easement; 48-hour notice before construction or grading within easement); additionally, land within the transmission easement must not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication per AEDC 1.16.0(9) | AEDC 1.16.0(8); AEDC 1.16.0(9) | Only when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property. Check: easement documents, plat sheets, and plan notes for all eight required AEDC 1.16.0(8) notes; verify that no portion of the transmission easement is labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b9a1b17-16ac-4098-9a13-aa52e9d6aac3","uuid":"3070b998-4e6a-4092-a48f-154f8aff11a5","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/el/3.md","content":"# Austin Energy Service and ROW Utility Coordination\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers all coordination requirements with Austin Energy (for electrical service, street lighting, and moonlight tower compliance) and with other ROW utility stakeholders (AULCC, Gattan, telecommunications, dry utilities, and CIP) before a site plan can be approved. It also captures the administrative closeout requirements — ROW utility coordination case closure, the Additional Review Acknowledgement table, street name reservations, and addressing approvals — that gate final ROW Review approval.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Austin Energy Service Coordination.** Austin Energy (AE) is the City of Austin's municipal electric utility serving both the city and portions of the surrounding area. All new development or service modification requires submission of an Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) to the appropriate AE Design business unit along with required documents to obtain an electrical design (AEDC 1.4.2.1). For service ≤350 amps single-phase or ≤225 amps three-phase (non-network), the Service-Only process applies; for larger loads or any service within the downtown Network Area, the Design Services process applies and full AE Design involvement is required (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4). Normally, the ESPA is submitted after DSD approves the site plan and must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A). A Building Service Planning Application (BSPA) — distinct from the ESPA — is required for building permits when the exterior of a structure is modified. For locations where it is uncertain which utility serves the site, an Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirms whether the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area (AEDC 1.4.1). AE Design has sole authority over available service characteristics and voltage at any given location (AEDC 1.3.2). AE owns electric facilities in the public ROW; customers own facilities from the point of service toward the building, except for the meter (AEDC 1.3.5). Before AE begins installation, the customer must grant AE an electrical easement using standard City forms signed by the property owner (AEDC 1.3.6).\n\n**Land Development Review Requirements (AEDC 1.16.0).** All land development applications submitted for Austin Energy review must include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) showing: all public and private easements; all existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, and transformers); permanent structures with footprint and height; final topology (grades, floodplains, detention ponds); and property lines with building setbacks. Plans must demonstrate sufficient clearances for existing and proposed electric facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(2)), dedicate additional easements if insufficient (at owner's expense, with metes and bounds descriptions), demonstrate that the development will not limit AE personnel access to current and proposed facilities (AEDC 1.16.0(4)), identify one point of service and one service voltage (exceptions require AE approval), and show approximate location and space for electric facilities with projected load. When a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, AEDC 1.16.0(8) requires specific plat notes: (i) AE tree pruning rights; (ii) provision for additional easements and access; (iii) owner erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection responsibilities; (iv) clearance maintenance obligation; (v) restriction on permanent structures that would violate NESC clearances within the transmission easement; (vi) 24-hour AE access across the property to transmission easements; (vii) all roads and driveways in the transmission easement built to support ≥48,000 lb tandem axle loads; (viii) 48-hour notice to AE before construction or grading within a transmission easement. Land within transmission easements may not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication (AEDC 1.16.0(9)).\n\n**Required Austin Energy Plan Notes.** All consolidated site plan applications must include four Standard Austin Energy Notes verbatim — changing the required text results in rejection: (1) Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery, and other obstructions to keep easements clear, and AE will comply with LDC Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B; (2) the owner/developer must provide AE with any easement and/or access required (beyond those indicated) for overhead and underground electric facilities, and such facilities shall not cause the site to be out of compliance with LDC Chapter 25-8; (3) the owner is responsible for temporary erosion control, revegetation, tree protection, and initial tree pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of proposed overhead electrical facilities, and AE's work area must be included within the limits of construction; (4) the property owner is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas state laws near overhead power lines; AE will not render service unless required clearances are maintained; failure costs are charged to the owner. In addition to these four standard notes, AE's land development review process under AEDC 1.16.0(7) requires site-specific plan notes: (a) a note identifying the site as lying within the Austin Energy service area, and (b) a note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current AE Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts.\n\n**Street Lighting.** AE develops a complete lighting installation plan for new residential and commercial developments. Developer conduit and infrastructure responsibilities vary by location type: for new residential subdivisions within city limits, the developer provides conduit, pull string, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs standard poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.4); for commercial areas within city limits, the developer pays all installation costs and provides conduit, pull-boxes, and foundations and AE installs poles and fixtures (AEDC 1.6.6); outside city limits in the AE service area, the developer bears all costs including monthly energy charges (AEDC 1.6.5, 1.6.7). All street lighting must meet IESNA spacing and illumination standards. Non-standard poles require developer/AE collaboration and the developer pays the cost difference. For projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary — bounded by Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez St (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W) — Great Streets Chapter 6 lighting standards apply: 8 poles per block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures). All pole placement and spacing requires prior AE Design coordination and, where applicable, Urban Design coordination.\n\n**AULCC and ROW Utility Coordination.** The Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee (AULCC) — referred to as \"ULCC\" in the UCM — administers the ROW utility coordination process. Per UCM 3.2.0, all projects that impact infrastructure located in the public right-of-way must be processed through the AULCC before proceeding to the permitting and construction phase. AULCC review is triggered when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) **300 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW anywhere in the city; (2) **25 or more linear feet** of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within the **Downtown Austin Project Coordination Zone (DAPCZ)** — roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east (IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street), Oltorf Street/Barton Skyway to the south, Loop 1 (MoPac) to the west, and Enfield Road/Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd to the north; the formal metes-and-bounds boundary is in Code § 14-11-161(7) and includes the boundary streets; (3) **any utility pole relocation**, regardless of linear footage, anywhere in the city; or (4) **water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets** (UCM 2.9.1.B.1). Projects that do not meet any of these thresholds are not subject to AULCC review. Successful AULCC coordination results in an AULCC Completeness Letter (clearance letter) that must be on file before the ROW Review can be marked Approved. The ROW utility coordination case must be closed in the site plan case management system before approval can be issued. Additional external utility clearances required through the ROW coordination process include: Gattan (a specific utility provider — its service territory is not described in Bureau code files; applicability is determined by the presence of Gattan-labeled facilities on utility plan sheets or from AULCC correspondence; requires human verification if unknown), telecommunications providers, dry utility providers, and any active CIP project manager for projects in areas with active capital improvement work.\n\n**Moonlight Towers.** Moonlight towers are specific, fixed-location, City-owned steel lattice towers with supporting guy wires, historically used for citywide arc lighting and concentrated in downtown and central Austin. Two separate regulatory thresholds apply: **(1) 100-foot permit threshold (COA § 14-11-201):** no construction activity may occur within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower without a permit under COA Division 4, Chapter 14-11; construction on private property requires application to the building official; construction in the ROW requires application to the director; the guy wire buffer applies independently from the tower base, so a project can be more than 100 feet from the base but still require a permit if within 100 feet of a guy wire. **(2) 25-foot ROW prohibition (COA § 14-11-205):** no facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; a permittee must also obtain a temporary use of ROW permit to occupy the ROW near a tower. A 24-hour advance notification to Austin Energy before construction near a moonlight tower is an AE operational practice but is not codified in COA §§ 14-11-201 through 14-11-205. Moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files — the review agent cannot determine proximity from plans alone. For projects in downtown or central Austin, moonlight tower proximity items should be flagged for human verification unless plans affirmatively note the absence of nearby towers.\n\n**Administrative Closeout.** Final site plan approval requires: the AULCC Completeness Letter on file; the Additional Review Acknowledgement table completed on the cover sheet or transmittal listing all applicable external reviews and permits (TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, and Planning and Development Review Department approvals) per UCM 2.5.1.F; all proposed street names, including names for private drives, reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before approval; and an addressing plan approved by Address Management Services. **Receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan** — applicants frequently confuse these two documents and reviewers must confirm the addressing plan is approved, not just that an AVL was issued.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **ESPA service type:** ≤350 A single-phase or ≤225 A three-phase (non-network) → Service-Only process; >350 A single-phase or >225 A three-phase, or any Network Area service → Design Services process with full AE Design involvement (AEDC 1.4.2.3–1.4.2.4)\n- **Continuous load:** >320 A must upgrade to instrument-rated CT service (AEDC 1.4.0)\n- **Moonlight tower permit:** Construction within 100 feet of a tower or its guy wires requires permit under COA § 14-11-201\n- **Moonlight tower ROW prohibition:** No facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower (COA § 14-11-205)\n- **AULCC trigger (citywide):** ≥300 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **AULCC trigger (DAPCZ):** ≥25 linear feet of ROW utility construction/excavation within DAPCZ (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **AULCC trigger (pole):** Any utility pole relocation, anywhere in the city (UCM 3.2.0)\n- **Great Streets lighting boundary:** MLK Blvd (N) / Lady Bird Lake–Cesar Chavez (S) / IH-35 (E) / Lamar Blvd (W) — 8 poles/block, 24-ft street / 12-ft pedestrian mounting heights (Great Streets Ch. 6)\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- ESPA submission normally occurs after DSD site plan approval, not before; ESPA must be resubmitted after receiving an Electric Permit number (AEDC 1.4.2.7.A)\n- Electric Service Availability Letter is required only where there is a question as to which utility serves the site; not required for all projects in the established AE service area (AEDC 1.4.1)\n- Non-standard street light poles are permitted with developer/AE collaboration and developer payment of cost difference\n- Projects below all AULCC thresholds (no pole relocations, <300 LF citywide, <25 LF in DAPCZ, no main assignments) are exempt from AULCC review\n\n**Common Violation Patterns:**\n- Citing LDC § 25-9-173 for the electric service capacity/availability letter requirement — this section does not exist in the current LDC (chapter numbering jumps from 164 to 191 in that division); the correct authority is AEDC 1.4.1 and AEDC 1.3.2\n- Citing COA § 14-11-205 for the 100-foot moonlight tower permit requirement — § 14-11-205 governs only the 25-foot ROW installation prohibition; the 100-foot permit requirement is in COA § 14-11-201\n- Treating AULCC clearance as an \"all sites\" requirement — AULCC review is only triggered when one of the four specific thresholds (linear footage, DAPCZ linear footage, pole relocation, or main assignment) is met; universal application generates false deficiencies for small infill projects\n- Accepting an Address Validation Letter (AVL) as an approved addressing plan — these are distinct documents; the addressing plan must be separately approved\n\n## Code References\n\n- AEDC 1.3.0 — Basic requirements of electric distribution service (customer vs. AE facility ownership, easement grants, access requirements, truck access dimensions)\n- AEDC 1.4.0 — Requesting and obtaining electric distribution service; ESPA submission process and service type thresholds\n- AEDC 1.4.1 — Electric Service Availability Letter from AE Public Involvement confirming service area\n- AEDC 1.6.0 — Street lighting standards, developer responsibilities, and IESNA requirements\n- AEDC 1.16.0 — Land development review application requirements for Austin Energy (AutoCAD drawing, clearance demonstration, easement sufficiency, access, point of service, plat notes for transmission easements)\n- Addressing Standards, Introduction — Address Management Services (AMS) is the addressing authority for the City of Austin and Travis County; addressing review occurs during subdivision and site plan review processes; approval required for permitting\n- Addressing Standards, Streets — Private drive, access easement, and street naming criteria; all proposed street names must be reviewed by AMS and reserved before use; named driveways and easements must adhere to Austin and Travis County Street Name Standards\n- COA § 14-11-201 — Permit required for construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or its guy wires\n- COA § 14-11-205 — No facility may be installed in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower; temporary use of ROW permit required\n- Great Streets Chapter 6 — Street lighting design standards for projects within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), Lamar Blvd (W)): 8 poles/block, 24-ft street light mounting height, 12-ft pedestrian light mounting height, 100W and 50W metal halide lamps (Gardco Circa/Form 10 street fixtures; Kim Lighting ERA pedestrian fixtures)\n- UCM 2.5.1.F — Additional authorizations required for final plan approval (TxDOT, railroad, gas company, TCEQ, water districts, etc.)\n- UCM 3.2.0 — AULCC: designation as ROW utility coordination body; requirement that all projects impacting ROW infrastructure be processed through the AULCC\n- UCM 3.4.0 — Utility coordination process phases (Pre-Design, Design, Final Design, Utility Adjustment)\n- UCM 3.8.0 — Pre-construction and construction meeting requirements\n- General Site Plan Notes — Standard Austin Energy Notes — Four mandatory notes required verbatim on all consolidated site plan applications\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site/Utility Plan Sheets** — Verify Austin Energy service area note, developer responsibility note for conduit/infrastructure, street light placement and dimensions, signal cabinet representations, utility conflicts in ROW (street lights vs. gas lines, storm inlets, etc.)\n- **Plan Notes Block / General Notes Sheet** — Check for all four Standard Austin Energy Notes (verbatim), moonlight tower permit and notification language, preconstruction meeting requirement note, developer responsibility note for AE conduit standards\n- **Cover Sheet or Transmittal** — Confirm Additional Review Acknowledgement table is complete with all external reviews/approvals (TxDOT, railroad, gas, TCEQ, water districts, etc.) per UCM 2.5.1.F\n- **Approval/Clearance Letters on File** — ESPA approval from Austin Energy, AEU clearance letter, Electric Service Availability Letter (where service provider was uncertain), AULCC Completeness Letter, Gattan clearance, telecommunications provider clearances, dry utility clearances, CIP clearance\n- **ROW Review Case Status** — Confirm utility coordination case is closed before ROW Review can be marked Approved in the Site Plan case\n- **Addressing Records** — Confirm addressing plan has been approved and all street names (including private drives) are reserved with 911 Addressing before site plan approval\n- **Easement Documents** — Verify electrical easements are dedicated using standard City forms where AE facilities are proposed on private property\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| EL-3.1 | Electric Service Planning Application (ESPA) not submitted to Austin Energy for review and approval, or ESPA approval not documented in the plan set | AEDC 1.4.2.1 | Only when new or modified electrical service is required. Check: project scope for new construction or electrical service upgrades |\n| EL-3.2 | Austin Energy Utility (AEU) clearance or approval not obtained or not documented | Reviewer Convention | All sites served by Austin Energy. Check: submittal package for AEU clearance letter |\n| EL-3.3 | Electric Service Availability Letter from Austin Energy not provided confirming that the point of service is within the Austin Energy service area; or for locations where the serving utility is uncertain, AE Public Involvement has not been contacted to obtain this letter | AEDC 1.4.1; AEDC 1.3.2 | Only when new electrical service is being obtained from Austin Energy and there is a question as to which electric utility serves the location. Check: project location relative to established AE service territory and ESPA documentation |\n| EL-3.4 | Site plan does not include a note stating that the site lies within the Austin Energy service area | AEDC 1.16.0(7) | All sites within the Austin Energy service area. Check: plan notes sheet for AE service area note |\n| EL-3.5 | Plans do not include the required developer responsibility note stating that the developer is responsible for designing and constructing all conduit and related infrastructure to current Austin Energy Electric Service Standards, including conductor installation costs, and for coordinating with other utilities to resolve all conflicts | AEDC 1.16.0(7); AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when new Austin Energy electrical infrastructure (conduit, conductors, pull-boxes, foundations) is required. Check: ESPA or electrical plans for new service |\n| EL-3.6 | One or more of the four required Standard Austin Energy Notes is missing or altered from the required verbatim text: (1) AE tree pruning rights in easements; (2) owner/developer obligation to provide additional easements or access for AE facilities; (3) owner responsibility for erosion control, revegetation, and tree protection within 10 feet of proposed overhead electrical facility centerlines; (4) owner responsibility for maintaining NESC/OSHA/City/state clearances near overhead power lines | General Site Plan Notes — Standard AE Notes | All consolidated site plan applications. Check: general notes sheet for all four required notes verbatim; changing the required text results in rejection |\n| EL-3.7 | Austin Energy approval not obtained for electrical transformer and pull box design prior to construction | AEDC 1.4.2.4 | Only when new or relocated transformers or pull boxes are proposed. Check: utility plan sheets for proposed AE equipment |\n| EL-3.8 | New driveway or site access improvement conflicts with existing Austin Energy power pole guy anchor support and coordination with Austin Energy has not been completed | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when new driveways or access improvements are proposed near existing power poles with guy anchors. Check: utility plan sheets for pole anchor locations adjacent to proposed driveways |\n| EL-3.9 | Plans do not include a note that a preconstruction meeting with Austin Energy is required prior to starting demolition | UCM 3.8.0 | Only when demolition is proposed. Check: demolition plan or general notes for preconstruction meeting requirement |\n| EL-3.10 | Final layout solution and electrical easement recording have not been coordinated with Austin Energy before approval sign-off; easement documents using standard City forms have not been returned to AE before AE begins installation | AEDC 1.16.0(3); AEDC 1.3.6 | Only when new electrical easements are required for proposed AE facilities on private property. Check: easement exhibits and AE design correspondence |\n| EL-3.11 | Land development application submitted to Austin Energy does not include a georeferenced AutoCAD drawing (version 2016 or newer) with all required content: public/private easements, existing AE facilities (transmission/distribution structures, guys, anchors, transformers), permanent structures with footprint and height, final topology, and property lines with building setbacks | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when submitting a land development application to Austin Energy for review. Check: AE submittal package for electronic drawing format compliance and required content |\n| EL-3.12 | Plans do not demonstrate that the proposed development will not limit Austin Energy personnel access to current and proposed electric facilities (e.g., no access path shown to proposed transformers, pull boxes, or overhead line equipment; no truck access dimensions provided for AE equipment areas) | AEDC 1.16.0(4) | Only when development is adjacent to existing or proposed AE electric facilities. Check: site plan and grading plan for AE equipment access routes; verify minimum 12-ft width and 16-ft vertical clearance for truck access per AEDC 1.3.16 |\n| EL-3.13 | Plan notes do not state that a permit from Austin Energy is required for any construction activity within 100 feet of a moonlight tower or within 100 feet of a guy wire supporting a moonlight tower, per COA § 14-11-201 | COA § 14-11-201 | Only when a moonlight tower or its guy wires is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: utility base map and Austin Energy moonlight tower records; if project is in downtown or central Austin, flag for human verification of proximity — moonlight tower locations are not mapped in Bureau code files |\n| EL-3.14 | Plan notes do not document Austin Energy's advance notification requirement before construction commences within 100 feet of a moonlight tower; this is an Austin Energy operational practice (not codified in COA § 14-11-205) and plans near moonlight towers should include coordination notes and current AE contact information for pre-construction notification | Austin Energy operational requirement | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area. Check: same proximity verification as EL-3.13; note that any phone number shown on plans should be confirmed current with Austin Energy |\n| EL-3.15 | Plans propose installing a facility in the public ROW within 25 feet of a moonlight tower, which is prohibited; additionally, no temporary use of ROW permit has been obtained or referenced for ROW occupancy in the vicinity of the tower | COA § 14-11-205 | Only when a moonlight tower is located within or adjacent to the project area and ROW facility installations are proposed. Check: proposed ROW facility locations vs. any annotated or known moonlight tower locations; flag for human verification if project is in downtown or central Austin and plans show ROW work within potential tower proximity |\n| EL-3.16 | Proposed street light placement in the ROW conflicts with an existing utility (e.g., gas line, storm sewer) and the conflict has not been resolved on the plans | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in or adjacent to the ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for horizontal conflicts between proposed light poles and mapped utilities |\n| EL-3.17 | Traffic signal cabinets shown on plans not updated to reflect current field conditions at affected intersections | Reviewer Convention | Only when traffic signal cabinets are shown on plans. Check: utility or streetscape plan sheets for signal cabinet locations vs. current field layout |\n| EL-3.18 | Street light pole spacing and placement not coordinated with Austin Energy; pole locations not confirmed with AE Design (and with Urban Design where applicable) | AEDC 1.6.4; AEDC 1.6.6 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for evidence of AE coordination on pole spacing |\n| EL-3.19 | Electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans do not extend to all required utility infrastructure locations within the project area; plan coverage ends before reaching the full extent of proposed utility work along the project frontage | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when electrical, MEP, or plumbing plans are part of the plan set and utility infrastructure extends across the project frontage. Check: plan coverage limits vs. full extent of proposed utility work |\n| EL-3.20 | Street lighting labels on plan sheets are duplicated or incorrectly applied (e.g., extra Great Streets light label assigned to a tree well or non-light feature) | Great Streets Chapter 6 | Only when the site is within the Great Streets Master Plan boundary (bounded by MLK Blvd (N), Lady Bird Lake/Cesar Chavez (S), IH-35 (E), and Lamar Blvd (W)) and Great Streets lighting standards apply. Check: confirm project is within Great Streets boundary, then review streetscape plan sheets for label accuracy and duplication |\n| EL-3.21 | Dimensions between street light poles not provided on plan sheets | AEDC 1.16.0(1); AEDC 1.6.0 | Only when street lights are proposed. Check: streetscape or utility plan sheets for dimensional callouts between pole locations |\n| EL-3.22 | Street light pole placement conflicts with a storm drain inlet or other subsurface stormwater infrastructure, requiring pole relocation | AEDC 1.16.0(1) | Only when street lights are proposed in close proximity to stormwater inlets. Check: utility plan sheets overlaying proposed pole locations with storm sewer infrastructure |\n| EL-3.23 | AULCC (Austin Utility Location and Coordination Committee) clearance not obtained before site plan approval; AULCC Completeness Letter not on file | UCM 3.2.0 | Only when any of the following thresholds are met: (1) 300 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW (citywide); (2) 25 or more linear feet of utility construction or excavation in the public ROW within DAPCZ (roughly bounded by Chicon Street to the east — note: IH-35 serves as the eastern boundary only in the southern segment from Town Lake to Oltorf Street — Oltorf/Barton Skyway (S), Loop 1/MoPac (W), Enfield Road/MLK Blvd (N); formal metes-and-bounds boundary in Code § 14-11-161(7); boundary streets included); (3) any utility pole relocation, regardless of linear footage; (4) water/wastewater/reclaimed water main assignments in city streets. Check: measure total linear footage of proposed ROW utility work on utility plan sheets; check for any pole relocations; determine if project is within DAPCZ |\n| EL-3.24 | Gattan clearance or approval not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when Gattan infrastructure is present or affected. Check: utility plan sheets for Gattan-labeled facilities in the project area; if unknown, flag for human verification via AULCC correspondence |\n| EL-3.25 | Telecommunications provider clearances not obtained | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when telecommunications infrastructure is affected by or located within the project area or ROW. Check: utility plan sheets for telecom facilities |\n| EL-3.26 | Dry utility clearances not obtained from all applicable providers | UCM 3.2.0; UCM 3.4.0 | Only when dry utilities (electric, gas, telecom) are present in the ROW or affected by site work. Check: utility plan sheets for dry utility facilities |\n| EL-3.27 | CIP (Capital Improvement Projects) clearance not obtained; a CIP project manager has not been identified or confirmed | Reviewer Convention | Only when a CIP project is active in or adjacent to the project area. Check: CIP project lookup for the project vicinity — requires human verification via City of Austin CIP project management records |\n| EL-3.28 | ROW utility coordination case not closed; ROW Review cannot be marked Approved in the Site Plan case until the utility coordination case is complete | UCM 3.2.0 | All sites with proposed ROW work. Check: ROW Review case status in site plan case management system |\n| EL-3.29 | Additional Review Acknowledgement table not completed on plans with all applicable external reviews, approvals, or permits required for the project (e.g., TxDOT permit, railroad permit, gas company permit, easement acquisition with volume/page or document number, county approval, water district or MUD approval, Texas Department of Health approval, TCEQ approval, non-occupancy letter, service extension approval, Planning and Development Review Department approvals) | UCM 2.5.1.F | All sites. Check: cover sheet or transmittal for completed Additional Review Acknowledgement table |\n| EL-3.30 | Proposed street names, including names for private drives, not approved and reserved with 911 Addressing/Address Management Services before site plan approval | Addressing Standards, Streets | Only when new streets or private drives are proposed. Check: plan set for any new street names; confirm 911 Addressing reservation status |\n| EL-3.31 | Addressing plan not approved; or consultation meeting with 911 Addressing not scheduled before submittal when no approved addressing plan exists; note that receipt of an Address Validation Letter (AVL) does not constitute site plan approval or an approved addressing plan | Addressing Standards, Introduction | Only when new addresses are required for the development. Check: addressing records for an approved addressing plan separate from any AVL issued |\n| EL-3.32 | Transmission easement plat notes missing or incomplete; when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property, plans must include all required AEDC 1.16.0(8) plat notes (AE tree pruning rights; additional easement/access provision; erosion control and tree protection responsibilities; clearance maintenance; NESC compliance restrictions on structures; 24-hour AE access to easement; ≥48,000 lb tandem axle road/driveway capacity within easement; 48-hour notice before construction or grading within easement); additionally, land within the transmission easement must not be labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication per AEDC 1.16.0(9) | AEDC 1.16.0(8); AEDC 1.16.0(9) | Only when a transmission easement is present on or crosses the property. Check: easement documents, plat sheets, and plan notes for all eight required AEDC 1.16.0(8) notes; verify that no portion of the transmission easement is labeled as parkland or proposed for parkland dedication |","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
