{"level":30,"time":1776951757612,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PnRgW4mqaMqLEQ1Tmq4fdG","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 2 — Plat\n     2→\n     3→Subdivision plat map showing legal boundaries, easements, and signatures.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet displays the historical recorded plat for the Mecey Subdivision (Travis County, 1975), which serves as the base property boundary framework for the current Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to establish the legal boundary dimensions, right-of-way dedications, existing easements, and historical surveyor certifications for Lots 1 and 2 between Valley View Road and Clawson Road.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   **Property Recording:** Book 73, Page 76 (Filed December 11, 1975, at 2:10 PM)\n    11→*   **Original Survey Date:** August 20, 1975\n    12→*   **Base Property Reference:** Lot A, Trico Subdivision (Book 69, Page 14) / Volume 4916, Page 2385\n    13→*   **Scale:** 1\" = 100 Feet\n    14→*   **Curve Data:** Delta (Δ) = 31°25', Tangent (T) = 20.84', Radius (R) = 74.10', Long Chord (L.C.) = 39.12', Arc = 40.63'\n    15→*   **Surveyor:** Forrest B. Scott, Reg. Public Surveyor #399 (Austin, Texas)\n    16→*   **Current Project Address:** 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas 78704\n    17→\n    18→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    19→\n    20→*   **Block 1: drawing — Mecey Subdivision Plat**\n    21→    The main drawing area containing the historical plat map, legal descriptions, owner's dedication, surveyor's certification, and approval signatures. \n    22→    *Use VISION to examine* the spatial layout of Lot 1 and Lot 2, their orientation between Valley View Road and Clawson Road, and the location of drainage easements. \n    23→    *READ the block content for* precise boundary bearings/dimensions, curve data details, historical recording information, and standard legal declarations.\n    24→\n    25→*   **Blocks 7, 18: text_block — Project Title**\n    26→    Identifies the current project name, site address, and the specific title of this sheet.\n    27→    *READ the block content for* the project address (3759 Valley View Road) and sheet title (\"Plat\").\n    28→\n    29→*   **Blocks 9, 11: table/text_block — Project Details**\n    30→    Contains the project tracking details.\n    31→    *READ the block content for* the Project No. (RJ3 70496) and the initials for Design (CS, JR), CAD (JW, CS), and Review (JKR).\n    32→\n    33→*   **Blocks 2, 13: text_block — Sheet Indexing**\n    34→    *READ the block content for* the sheet number (Sheet 2 of 39) and tracking number (SP-2025-0126C).\n    35→\n    36→*   **Blocks 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    37→    Contains standard engineering firm contact info (Bleyl Engineering), office locations, the empty revision schedule, original layout size (24x36), and CAD file path plots. These can generally be skipped during technical review unless confirming the engineer of record's contact information.\n    38→\n    39→---\n    40→\n    41→- `block-1.md` — drawing: The main content area showing the plat for the Mecey Subdivision. This includes the legal descriptio\n    42→\n    43→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    44→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f19fb8e-f97f-429a-bf6c-feca3089c5bd","uuid":"3689dfbe-b138-4cf3-b539-d5df50206c49","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-02/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 2 — Plat\n\nSubdivision plat map showing legal boundaries, easements, and signatures.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet displays the historical recorded plat for the Mecey Subdivision (Travis County, 1975), which serves as the base property boundary framework for the current Valley View Townhomes project. Its primary purpose is to establish the legal boundary dimensions, right-of-way dedications, existing easements, and historical surveyor certifications for Lots 1 and 2 between Valley View Road and Clawson Road.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   **Property Recording:** Book 73, Page 76 (Filed December 11, 1975, at 2:10 PM)\n*   **Original Survey Date:** August 20, 1975\n*   **Base Property Reference:** Lot A, Trico Subdivision (Book 69, Page 14) / Volume 4916, Page 2385\n*   **Scale:** 1\" = 100 Feet\n*   **Curve Data:** Delta (Δ) = 31°25', Tangent (T) = 20.84', Radius (R) = 74.10', Long Chord (L.C.) = 39.12', Arc = 40.63'\n*   **Surveyor:** Forrest B. Scott, Reg. Public Surveyor #399 (Austin, Texas)\n*   **Current Project Address:** 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, Texas 78704\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Block 1: drawing — Mecey Subdivision Plat**\n    The main drawing area containing the historical plat map, legal descriptions, owner's dedication, surveyor's certification, and approval signatures. \n    *Use VISION to examine* the spatial layout of Lot 1 and Lot 2, their orientation between Valley View Road and Clawson Road, and the location of drainage easements. \n    *READ the block content for* precise boundary bearings/dimensions, curve data details, historical recording information, and standard legal declarations.\n\n*   **Blocks 7, 18: text_block — Project Title**\n    Identifies the current project name, site address, and the specific title of this sheet.\n    *READ the block content for* the project address (3759 Valley View Road) and sheet title (\"Plat\").\n\n*   **Blocks 9, 11: table/text_block — Project Details**\n    Contains the project tracking details.\n    *READ the block content for* the Project No. (RJ3 70496) and the initials for Design (CS, JR), CAD (JW, CS), and Review (JKR).\n\n*   **Blocks 2, 13: text_block — Sheet Indexing**\n    *READ the block content for* the sheet number (Sheet 2 of 39) and tracking number (SP-2025-0126C).\n\n*   **Blocks 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    Contains standard engineering firm contact info (Bleyl Engineering), office locations, the empty revision schedule, original layout size (24x36), and CAD file path plots. These can generally be skipped during technical review unless confirming the engineer of record's contact information.\n\n---\n\n- `block-1.md` — drawing: The main content area showing the plat for the Mecey Subdivision. This includes the legal descriptio\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951757624,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"3.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P1UQGtij2innjhkYdS9vCk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 8\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Notes\n     6→1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     7→2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     8→3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n     9→4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n    11→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eedca44c-cd40-46be-bd59-597db07f0864","uuid":"34431b2b-f972-4929-899c-1a9f8d3480a0","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-8.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 8\n\n## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n\nElectric Notes\n1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n","numLines":11,"startLine":1,"totalLines":11}}}
