{"level":30,"time":1776951757758,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XuMf3HNPyATdzTNWo9rgdn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 9 — Site Plan\n     2→\n     3→Detailed site plan layout with associated building and parking data.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet contains the primary Site Plan for the Valley View Townhomes project (Sheet 9 of 39). It illustrates the overall site layout, including the arrangement of eight multi-family residential buildings, internal driveways, parking, a detention pond, pedestrian walkways, and retaining walls, while providing comprehensive tabular data to verify compliance with zoning, area, coverage, height, and parking requirements.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   **Site Area:** 94,192 sf (2.162 acres)\n    11→*   **Zoning:** MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density\n    12→*   **Total Units:** 39 units (16 Two Bedroom, 23 Three Bedroom) across 8 buildings\n    13→*   **Maximum Building Height:** 40' allowed; 36' - 1\" is the highest proposed (Building 3)\n    14→*   **Impervious Cover:** 59% proposed (55,502 sf) vs 60% allowable\n    15→*   **Building Coverage:** 34% proposed (32,296 sf) vs 50% allowable\n    16→*   **Gross Floor Area:** 83,986 sf\n    17→*   **Vehicle Parking:** 88 total spaces provided (10 surface, 78 garage)\n    18→*   **Bicycle Parking:** 5 required; 41 provided (39 long term, 2 short term)\n    19→*   **Accessibility Slopes:** Max parking slope 1:50; Max cross-slope 1:50; Max ramp slope 1:12\n    20→*   **Benchmarks (NAVD 88):** BM #1 = 678.52'; BM #2 = 681.08'\n    21→\n    22→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    23→\n    24→*   **Block 1: drawing — Main Site Plan**\n    25→    A comprehensive layout drawing of the property. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout of Buildings 1-8, the routing of internal driveways and concrete/decomposed granite walkways, the location of the biofiltration/detention pond in the northeast corner, and the placement of retaining walls around the site perimeter.\n    26→\n    27→*   **Block 16: table — Site Data Table**\n    28→    A summary table of overall site metrics. READ the block content for exact values verifying zoning compliance, including site area, existing vs. proposed impervious cover percentages, allowable vs. proposed building coverage, and gross floor area.\n    29→\n    30→*   **Block 14: table — Building Data Table**\n    31→    A breakdown of metrics per building. READ the block content for individual building types (4-, 5-, or 6-plexes), gross floor areas, building footprints, bedroom configurations, and total unit counts (39 total, maximum 3 stories).\n    32→\n    33→*   **Block 15: table — Building Height Table**\n    34→    A summary of building heights for compliance checking. READ the block content for the average MSL, physical building heights (ranging from 33'-4.75\" to 36'-1\"), and verification that all buildings fall under the 40' allowable height limit.\n    35→\n    36→*   **Block 17: table — Site Area Calculation (MF-2)**\n    37→    A calculation of required area based on unit types. READ the block content to verify that the 94,192 sf provided site area satisfies the 93,600 sf required for the 39 2+ bedroom units.\n    38→\n    39→*   **Block 8: notes — Parking Data**\n    40→    A tabular summary of parking requirements. READ the block content for the breakdown of vehicle parking (surface vs. garage) and the exact counts for short-term and long-term bicycle parking spaces.\n    41→\n    42→*   **Block 10: notes — Site Plan Release Notes**\n    43→    A list of administrative conditions for the site plan. READ the block content for compliance requirements regarding standard building codes, signage, utility providers, demolition, and right-of-way excavation permits.\n    44→\n    45→*   **Block 4: notes — Accessibility Notes**\n    46→    Design constraints for ADA compliance. READ the block content for specific, mandated slope tolerances for accessible parking spaces, cross-slopes, and ramps (e.g., max ramp slope of 1:12).\n    47→\n    48→*   **Block 2: drawing — Lighting Note & Figure 34**\n    49→    Requirements for exterior lighting. READ for the requirement that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded per Subchapter E 2.5. Use VISION to review the four acceptable fixture profiles illustrated in Figure 34.\n    50→\n    51→*   **Block 6: notes — Fence Note**\n    52→    Tree protection requirements. READ the block content for instructions mandating hand-digging of post holes within Critical Root Zones (CRZ) and the required application of 3-inch hardwood mulch.\n    53→\n    54→*   **Block 9: notes — Trash Collection Notes**\n    55→    Rules for waste management. READ the block content for curbside unattended container restrictions and necessary enforcement signage requirements.\n    56→\n    57→*   **Blocks 3, 5, 7, 11, 12, 13: various — Administrative & Boilerplate Data**\n    58→    Standard site plan identifiers and administrative data. READ for the legal description (Block 3), the specific benchmark elevations (Block 5), and general utility liability notes (Block 7). Use VISION to locate the graphic scale showing 1\" = 20' (Block 11), the Texas 811 call-before-you-dig logo (Block 12), and the engineer's seal dated 2/27/26 (Block 13).\n    59→\n    60→---\n    61→\n    62→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    63→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f19fb8e-f97f-429a-bf6c-feca3089c5bd","uuid":"244da703-c910-4ef0-8b7b-19842d51d10b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-09/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 9 — Site Plan\n\nDetailed site plan layout with associated building and parking data.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet contains the primary Site Plan for the Valley View Townhomes project (Sheet 9 of 39). It illustrates the overall site layout, including the arrangement of eight multi-family residential buildings, internal driveways, parking, a detention pond, pedestrian walkways, and retaining walls, while providing comprehensive tabular data to verify compliance with zoning, area, coverage, height, and parking requirements.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   **Site Area:** 94,192 sf (2.162 acres)\n*   **Zoning:** MF-2/Multi-Family Low Density\n*   **Total Units:** 39 units (16 Two Bedroom, 23 Three Bedroom) across 8 buildings\n*   **Maximum Building Height:** 40' allowed; 36' - 1\" is the highest proposed (Building 3)\n*   **Impervious Cover:** 59% proposed (55,502 sf) vs 60% allowable\n*   **Building Coverage:** 34% proposed (32,296 sf) vs 50% allowable\n*   **Gross Floor Area:** 83,986 sf\n*   **Vehicle Parking:** 88 total spaces provided (10 surface, 78 garage)\n*   **Bicycle Parking:** 5 required; 41 provided (39 long term, 2 short term)\n*   **Accessibility Slopes:** Max parking slope 1:50; Max cross-slope 1:50; Max ramp slope 1:12\n*   **Benchmarks (NAVD 88):** BM #1 = 678.52'; BM #2 = 681.08'\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n*   **Block 1: drawing — Main Site Plan**\n    A comprehensive layout drawing of the property. Use VISION to examine the spatial layout of Buildings 1-8, the routing of internal driveways and concrete/decomposed granite walkways, the location of the biofiltration/detention pond in the northeast corner, and the placement of retaining walls around the site perimeter.\n\n*   **Block 16: table — Site Data Table**\n    A summary table of overall site metrics. READ the block content for exact values verifying zoning compliance, including site area, existing vs. proposed impervious cover percentages, allowable vs. proposed building coverage, and gross floor area.\n\n*   **Block 14: table — Building Data Table**\n    A breakdown of metrics per building. READ the block content for individual building types (4-, 5-, or 6-plexes), gross floor areas, building footprints, bedroom configurations, and total unit counts (39 total, maximum 3 stories).\n\n*   **Block 15: table — Building Height Table**\n    A summary of building heights for compliance checking. READ the block content for the average MSL, physical building heights (ranging from 33'-4.75\" to 36'-1\"), and verification that all buildings fall under the 40' allowable height limit.\n\n*   **Block 17: table — Site Area Calculation (MF-2)**\n    A calculation of required area based on unit types. READ the block content to verify that the 94,192 sf provided site area satisfies the 93,600 sf required for the 39 2+ bedroom units.\n\n*   **Block 8: notes — Parking Data**\n    A tabular summary of parking requirements. READ the block content for the breakdown of vehicle parking (surface vs. garage) and the exact counts for short-term and long-term bicycle parking spaces.\n\n*   **Block 10: notes — Site Plan Release Notes**\n    A list of administrative conditions for the site plan. READ the block content for compliance requirements regarding standard building codes, signage, utility providers, demolition, and right-of-way excavation permits.\n\n*   **Block 4: notes — Accessibility Notes**\n    Design constraints for ADA compliance. READ the block content for specific, mandated slope tolerances for accessible parking spaces, cross-slopes, and ramps (e.g., max ramp slope of 1:12).\n\n*   **Block 2: drawing — Lighting Note & Figure 34**\n    Requirements for exterior lighting. READ for the requirement that all exterior lighting must be full cut-off and fully shielded per Subchapter E 2.5. Use VISION to review the four acceptable fixture profiles illustrated in Figure 34.\n\n*   **Block 6: notes — Fence Note**\n    Tree protection requirements. READ the block content for instructions mandating hand-digging of post holes within Critical Root Zones (CRZ) and the required application of 3-inch hardwood mulch.\n\n*   **Block 9: notes — Trash Collection Notes**\n    Rules for waste management. READ the block content for curbside unattended container restrictions and necessary enforcement signage requirements.\n\n*   **Blocks 3, 5, 7, 11, 12, 13: various — Administrative & Boilerplate Data**\n    Standard site plan identifiers and administrative data. READ for the legal description (Block 3), the specific benchmark elevations (Block 5), and general utility liability notes (Block 7). Use VISION to locate the graphic scale showing 1\" = 20' (Block 11), the Texas 811 call-before-you-dig logo (Block 12), and the engineer's seal dated 2/27/26 (Block 13).\n\n---\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":63,"startLine":1,"totalLines":63}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951757771,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTbCnS8a6r22vq6TeVVifb","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 16 — Biofiltration-Detention Pond Profiles\n     2→\n     3→Biofiltration pond profiles, outfall details, and comprehensive maintenance requirements.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet provides cross-sectional profiles and outfall construction details for a biofiltration-detention pond at the Valley View Townhomes project. It also contains extensive textual specifications detailing the routine maintenance, major maintenance, and construction requirements for Stormwater Control Measures (SCMs).\n     8→\n     9→**Key values**:\n    10→*   **Water Surface Elevations (WSEL)**: 100-yr (676.75'), 25-yr (676.57'), 10-yr (676.45'), 2-yr (676.25')\n    11→*   **Biofiltration Media Elevation**: 672.00'\n    12→*   **Retaining Wall Top Elevations**: 681.00', 677.25', and 677.50' (outfall)\n    13→*   **Outfall Weir**: 4.5' wide at elevation 675.65'\n    14→*   **Storm Pipe**: 5.00 linear feet of 24\" HDPE laid at a 1.35% slope\n    15→*   **Rock Flow Spreader**: 6\"-12\" (H) x 3' (W) using 5-8\" diameter rock\n    16→*   **Rock Rip-Rap**: 5-8\" diameter rock\n    17→*   **Drawdown Time Limits (Maintenance)**: Maximum 96 hours for water quality volume; 24 hours for detention ponds\n    18→\n    19→**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n    20→\n    21→*   **Block 1: drawing — Pond Section 'A-A'**\n    22→    Cross-sectional profile detailing the pond's left and right concrete retaining walls, pipe inlet, rock flow spreader, rock rip-rap, and biofiltration media elevations.\n    23→    *Navigation*: Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, slope grades, and assembly of the pond floor features relative to the structural walls. \n    24→\n    25→*   **Block 2: drawing — Pond Section 'B-B'**\n    26→    Cross-sectional profile showing the transition from existing ground to proposed surfaces, including an existing retaining wall designated for removal.\n    27→    *Navigation*: Use VISION to understand the cut/fill relationships and the exact location of the existing retaining wall to be removed.\n    28→\n    29→*   **Block 3: drawing — Pond Outfall Detail**\n    30→    Detailed cross-section of the outfall structure featuring the concrete retaining wall and the 4.5' wide weir.\n    31→    *Navigation*: Use VISION for the structural layout and READ for the exact weir elevation (675.65') relative to the projected storm event water surface elevations.\n    32→\n    33→*   **Block 8: text_block — Maintenance and Construction Requirements**\n    34→    Comprehensive specifications for SCMs (Stormwater Control Measures), including warranty terms, tree preservation, retaining wall water-tightness, drawdown time requirements, sediment removal criteria, and a seasonal schedule for biofiltration routine maintenance.\n    35→    *Navigation*: READ the block content carefully for specific maintenance triggers (e.g., sediment exceeding 6 inches) and the required schedule for inspecting and managing vegetation.\n    36→\n    37→*   **Blocks 4, 5, 6, 7: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    38→    Project title block (Valley View Townhomes, Sheet 16 of 39), engineering firm details (Bleyl Engineering), engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), and standard liability disclaimers regarding utility locations.\n    39→    *Navigation*: READ for sheet identification, revision dates, and project administration details.\n    40→\n    41→---\n    42→\n    43→- `block-8.md` — text_block: Text detailing the maintenance and construction requirements for stormwater control measures (SCMs),\n    44→\n    45→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24aa3565-6e12-4421-aab9-b461d3b546f4","uuid":"54369d4b-79f7-4fa2-b1cd-5b3ecbaf03db","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-16/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 16 — Biofiltration-Detention Pond Profiles\n\nBiofiltration pond profiles, outfall details, and comprehensive maintenance requirements.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet provides cross-sectional profiles and outfall construction details for a biofiltration-detention pond at the Valley View Townhomes project. It also contains extensive textual specifications detailing the routine maintenance, major maintenance, and construction requirements for Stormwater Control Measures (SCMs).\n\n**Key values**:\n*   **Water Surface Elevations (WSEL)**: 100-yr (676.75'), 25-yr (676.57'), 10-yr (676.45'), 2-yr (676.25')\n*   **Biofiltration Media Elevation**: 672.00'\n*   **Retaining Wall Top Elevations**: 681.00', 677.25', and 677.50' (outfall)\n*   **Outfall Weir**: 4.5' wide at elevation 675.65'\n*   **Storm Pipe**: 5.00 linear feet of 24\" HDPE laid at a 1.35% slope\n*   **Rock Flow Spreader**: 6\"-12\" (H) x 3' (W) using 5-8\" diameter rock\n*   **Rock Rip-Rap**: 5-8\" diameter rock\n*   **Drawdown Time Limits (Maintenance)**: Maximum 96 hours for water quality volume; 24 hours for detention ponds\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet**:\n\n*   **Block 1: drawing — Pond Section 'A-A'**\n    Cross-sectional profile detailing the pond's left and right concrete retaining walls, pipe inlet, rock flow spreader, rock rip-rap, and biofiltration media elevations.\n    *Navigation*: Use VISION to examine the spatial layout, slope grades, and assembly of the pond floor features relative to the structural walls. \n\n*   **Block 2: drawing — Pond Section 'B-B'**\n    Cross-sectional profile showing the transition from existing ground to proposed surfaces, including an existing retaining wall designated for removal.\n    *Navigation*: Use VISION to understand the cut/fill relationships and the exact location of the existing retaining wall to be removed.\n\n*   **Block 3: drawing — Pond Outfall Detail**\n    Detailed cross-section of the outfall structure featuring the concrete retaining wall and the 4.5' wide weir.\n    *Navigation*: Use VISION for the structural layout and READ for the exact weir elevation (675.65') relative to the projected storm event water surface elevations.\n\n*   **Block 8: text_block — Maintenance and Construction Requirements**\n    Comprehensive specifications for SCMs (Stormwater Control Measures), including warranty terms, tree preservation, retaining wall water-tightness, drawdown time requirements, sediment removal criteria, and a seasonal schedule for biofiltration routine maintenance.\n    *Navigation*: READ the block content carefully for specific maintenance triggers (e.g., sediment exceeding 6 inches) and the required schedule for inspecting and managing vegetation.\n\n*   **Blocks 4, 5, 6, 7: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    Project title block (Valley View Townhomes, Sheet 16 of 39), engineering firm details (Bleyl Engineering), engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), and standard liability disclaimers regarding utility locations.\n    *Navigation*: READ for sheet identification, revision dates, and project administration details.\n\n---\n\n- `block-8.md` — text_block: Text detailing the maintenance and construction requirements for stormwater control measures (SCMs),\n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951757772,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"3.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KiUsWNZM8eJ2gXkFXfCsMu","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 10: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n     4→\n     5→Site Plan Release Notes\n     6→1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n     7→2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n     8→3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n     9→4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n    10→5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n    11→6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n    12→8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n    13→9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n    14→10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n    15→\n    16→## Block 11: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n    17→\n    18→Benchmarks\n    19→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    20→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    21→\n    22→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    23→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    24→\n    25→## Block 12: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n    26→\n    27→Americans with Disabilities Act\n    28→The City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n    29→\n    30→## Block 13: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n    31→\n    32→Fire Department\n    33→1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n    34→2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n    35→3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n    36→4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n    37→5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n    38→6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n    39→7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n    40→\n    41→## Block 17: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n    42→\n    43→Legal Description\n    44→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"60bcd99d-409f-4cd6-a909-b056cb4b2c38","uuid":"fcc915ee-fb43-4649-97f0-5966c25d8f63","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 10: text_block — List of 10 site plan release notes outlining requirements and conditions for the approved site plan, including building codes, relocation costs, demolition permits, and utility service providers.\n\nSite Plan Release Notes\n1. All improvements shall be made in accordance with the released site plan. Any additional improvements will require a site plan amendment and approval from the Development Services Department.\n2. Approval of this site plan does not include Building Code and Fire Code approval; or building, demolition, or relocation permits approval. A City demolition or relocation permit can only be issued once the historic review process is completed.\n3. All signs must comply with the requirements of the Land Development Code.(Chapter 25-10)\n4. The owner is responsible for all costs of relocation of, or damage to, utilities.\n5. Additional electric easements may be required at a later date.\n6. A Site Development Permit must be issued prior to an application for building permit for non-consolidated or Land Use Commission approved site plans.\n8. All existing structures shown to be removed will require a demolition permit from the City of Austin Development Services Department.\n9. For construction within the right-of-way, a ROW excavation permit is required.\n10. Water and wastewater service will be provided by the City of Austin (or identify the service provider if other than the City of Austin).\n\n## Block 11: text_block — Locations and elevations for two benchmarks used in the project.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 12: text_block — Notice stating the applicant's responsibility for determining ADA compliance.\n\nAmericans with Disabilities Act\nThe City of Austin has reviewed this plans for compliance with City development regulations only. The applicant, property owner, and occupant of the premises are responsible for determining whether the plan complies with all other laws, regulations , and restrictions which may be applicable to the property and its use.\n\n## Block 13: text_block — List of 7 requirements from the Austin Fire Department regarding pavement, hydrants, installation timing, paving standards, dumpster placement, fire lanes, and vertical clearances.\n\nFire Department\n1. The Austin Fire Department requires asphalt or concrete pavement prior to construction as an \"all-weather driving surface.\"\n2. Hydrants must be installed with the center of the four-inch opening at least 18 inches above finished grade. The four-inch opening must face the driveway or street with three- to six-foot setbacks from the curbline(s). No obstruction is allowed within three feet of any hydrant and the four-inch opening must be totally unobstructed from the street.\n3. TIMING OF INSTALLATION: When fire protection facilities are installed by the developer, such facilities shall include all surface access roads which shall be installed and made serviceable prior to and during the time of construction. Where alternative methods of protection, as approved by the Fire Chief, are provided, the above may be modified or waived.\n4. All pervious/decorative paving shall be engineered and installed for 80,000 lb. live-vehicle loads. Any pervious/decorative paving within 100 feet of any building must be approved by the Fire Department.\n5. Commercial dumpsters and containers with an individual capacity of 1.5 cubic yards or greater shall not be stored or placed within ten feet of openings, combustible walls, or combustible eave lines.\n6. Fire lanes designated on site plan shall be registered with City of Austin Fire Marshal's office and inspected for final approval.\n7. Vertical clearance required for fire apparatus is 14 feet for full width of access drive.\n\n## Block 17: text_block — Legal description of the property for the site plan.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
