{"level":30,"time":1776951768218,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PqbLSjjGfXLtMHpgLdwucN","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 21 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: diagram — A graphic scale bar showing distances of 0, 20, and 40 feet, along with the text 'Scale 1\" = 20''. A north arrow is also present above the scale bar.\n     4→\n     5→[Graphic scale bar showing distances 0, 20, 40]\n     6→Scale 1\" = 20'\n     7→[North arrow pointing upwards and slightly to the right]\n     8→\n     9→## Block 3: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n    10→\n    11→A detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n    12→\n    13→## Block 4: notes — A block listing two benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2) with descriptions indicating they are mag nails set in asphalt, along with their respective elevations in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n    14→\n    15→**Benchmarks**\n    16→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    17→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    18→\n    19→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    20→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    21→\n    22→## Block 5: notes — A block containing a 'Tree List' reference stating 'Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.' Below it is a 'Note' regarding a Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule that requires trenching over 5 feet deep to be inspected by a geologist.\n    23→\n    24→**Tree List**\n    25→Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n    26→\n    27→**Note**\n    28→This project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n    29→\n    30→## Block 6: notes — This block contains 'Utility Notes' detailing contractor responsibilities regarding existing utilities and specific requirements for private domestic water and wastewater branches. Below the notes are disclaimers about the application not verifying all data and the reliance on record information for existing utilities.\n    31→\n    32→**Utility Notes**\n    33→1. Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\n    34→2. Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\n    35→3. All Private Domestic Water Branches to the buildings are to be 2\" HDPE service tube per Detail 520-AW-01B\n    36→4. All Private Wastewater Branches to the building are to be 6\" PVC service connections per Detail 520-AW-01C\n    37→\n    38→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    39→\n    40→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    41→\n    42→## Block 7: notes — A small block containing text that references another sheet for profile information.\n    43→\n    44→See Sht. 19\n    45→for Profile\n    46→\n    47→## Block 11: text_block — A block providing the legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision.\n    48→\n    49→**Legal Description**\n    50→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    51→\n    52→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8937ea6-a85f-452c-ba14-5b8849922a4d","uuid":"3885f08d-91b9-43ac-974b-658c82c7ce1f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-21/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 21 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: diagram — A graphic scale bar showing distances of 0, 20, and 40 feet, along with the text 'Scale 1\" = 20''. A north arrow is also present above the scale bar.\n\n[Graphic scale bar showing distances 0, 20, 40]\nScale 1\" = 20'\n[North arrow pointing upwards and slightly to the right]\n\n## Block 3: drawing — This block contains a detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n\nA detailed site plan showing multiple buildings (Bldg. 1 to Bldg. 8) and their respective units, with FFE (Finished Floor Elevation) and square footage information. The plan includes electrical elements such as transformers, pull boxes, underground electric lines, and electrical meters. Additionally, the plan shows topography lines, property boundaries, and various easements like drainage, public utility, and wastewater. Information on adjoining properties is provided, detailing acreage, zoning, and use.\n\n## Block 4: notes — A block listing two benchmarks (B.M. #1 and B.M. #2) with descriptions indicating they are mag nails set in asphalt, along with their respective elevations in NAVD 88 (GEOID 18).\n\n**Benchmarks**\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 5: notes — A block containing a 'Tree List' reference stating 'Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.' Below it is a 'Note' regarding a Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule that requires trenching over 5 feet deep to be inspected by a geologist.\n\n**Tree List**\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n**Note**\nThis project is subject to the Void and Water Flow Mitigation Rule (COA ECM 1.12.0 and COA Item No. 658S of the SSM) provision that all trenching greater than 5 feet deep must be inspected by a geologist (Texas P.G.) or a geologist's representative.\n\n## Block 6: notes — This block contains 'Utility Notes' detailing contractor responsibilities regarding existing utilities and specific requirements for private domestic water and wastewater branches. Below the notes are disclaimers about the application not verifying all data and the reliance on record information for existing utilities.\n\n**Utility Notes**\n1. Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\n2. Contractor shall verify location of existing line, point of connection and notify engineer of any conflicts/discrepancies prior to installation of improvements.\n3. All Private Domestic Water Branches to the buildings are to be 2\" HDPE service tube per Detail 520-AW-01B\n4. All Private Wastewater Branches to the building are to be 6\" PVC service connections per Detail 520-AW-01C\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 7: notes — A small block containing text that references another sheet for profile information.\n\nSee Sht. 19\nfor Profile\n\n## Block 11: text_block — A block providing the legal description of the property, identifying it as Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision.\n\n**Legal Description**\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n","numLines":52,"startLine":1,"totalLines":52}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776951768230,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":5,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014ZpYPfvzQy5iKe3CCjC5Ey","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 15 — Biofiltration-Detention Pond Plan & Calculations\n     2→\n     3→Biofiltration and detention pond plan, details, and engineering calculations.\n     4→\n     5→## Reading Guide\n     6→\n     7→This sheet provides the detailed site plan, schematic cross-sections, and comprehensive engineering calculations for a partial biofiltration and onsite detention pond associated with Unit 501. Its primary purpose is to outline the spatial layout, stage-storage volumes, flow rates, and material specifications required to ensure proper stormwater management and water quality control for the development.\n     8→\n     9→**Key values:**\n    10→*   Drainage Area to Control: 1.48 ac (86.09% Impervious Cover, 1.27 ac)\n    11→*   Water Quality Volume (WQV): 6,237 cf required, 6,388 cf provided\n    12→*   Water Quality Elevation Provided: 675.65 ft msl\n    13→*   Top of Biofiltration Media Elevation: 672.00 ft\n    14→*   Maximum Ponding Depth above Sand Bed: 3.65 ft\n    15→*   Filtration Pond Area Provided: 831 sf\n    16→*   Peak Flow Rates (Q100): 18.83 cfs (Entrance), 15.60 cfs (Outfall)\n    17→*   D50 Rock Rip-Rap: 0.25 in (Entrance), 0.39 in (Outfall)\n    18→*   Required Orifice Diameter: 1.39 in (Area: 0.011 sq. ft.)\n    19→\n    20→**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n    21→\n    22→**Block 3: drawing — Unit 501 Site Plan Drawing**\n    23→*   Contains the core layout of the biofiltration-detention pond, retaining walls, rip-rap placement, and routing of drainage infrastructure.\n    24→*   Use VISION to examine the spatial layout and relationships between pond elements. READ for specific spot elevations (e.g., Top of Biofiltration Media at 672.00) and pipe specifications (e.g., 24\" HDPE @ 1.35%).\n    25→\n    26→**Block 2: table — Appendix R-6 Partial Biofiltration Pond Calculations**\n    27→*   Contains the primary water quality control and biological element calculations for the pond system.\n    28→*   READ the block content for critical drainage area parameters, water quality volume verifications, and exact minimum planting requirements.\n    29→\n    30→**Block 1: diagram — Biofiltration Bed Underdrain Details**\n    31→*   Contains cross-sectional diagrams of the biofiltration underdrain pipe and medium bed profile.\n    32→*   Use VISION to examine the layering and spatial arrangement of the filter system. READ for specific material depths (e.g., 18\" min sand, 3\"-6\" gravel) and pipe perforation layout specifications.\n    33→\n    34→**Block 15: text_block — Partial Sedimentation/Biofiltration Pond Schematic**\n    35→*   Contains a cross-sectional illustration showing the general flow from the flow spreader through the sedimentation chamber to the filtration basin.\n    36→*   Use VISION to examine the conceptual layout and components of the dual-chamber pond system.\n    37→\n    38→**Block 6: table — Water Quality Pond Water Surface Elevations**\n    39→*   Contains a stage-storage table showing depths, areas, and cumulative storage for the entire water quality pond.\n    40→*   READ the block content for exact storage volumes at given elevations and to verify the required vs. provided WQV elevations (675.57 vs 675.65).\n    41→\n    42→**Block 11: table — Onsite Detention Pond Tables**\n    43→*   Contains stage vs. storage capacities and the outflow summary for 2-year, 10-year, 25-year, and 100-year storm events.\n    44→*   READ the block content for peak outflow rates (up to 15.60 cfs) at specific event elevations.\n    45→\n    46→**Block 5: table — Discharge Summary**\n    47→*   Contains existing and proposed stormwater flow rates (cfs) across various analysis points based on Atlas 14 data.\n    48→*   READ the block content to compare pre- and post-development flow rates for different storm intervals.\n    49→\n    50→**Blocks 7 & 10: table — Sedimentation and Filtration Pond Stage-Storage**\n    51→*   Contains individual stage, area, and storage breakdowns for the sedimentation pond and the filtration pond.\n    52→*   READ the block content for specific area and volume metrics at 1-foot increments between 672.00 and 677.50 ft msl.\n    53→\n    54→**Block 12: table — Entrance and Outfall Sizing Calculations**\n    55→*   Contains velocity and D50 rock rip-rap sizing calculations for the 100-year storm event.\n    56→*   READ the block content to verify entrance (1.39 fps) and exit (1.73 fps) velocities and required rip-rap diameters.\n    57→\n    58→**Block 13: table — Falling Head Orifice Calculator**\n    59→*   Contains the variables and calculated results for sizing the detention pond orifice.\n    60→*   READ the block content for the orifice sizing parameters, specifically the final required diameter of 1.39 inches, and the governing equation.\n    61→\n    62→**Blocks 8 & 9: table — Material Specifications (Filter Fabric & Biofiltration Medium)**\n    63→*   Contains the required physical characteristics, composition percentages, and testing methods for the high flow filter fabric and biofiltration soil medium.\n    64→*   READ the block content for strict material acceptance criteria (e.g., 70-90% sand + coarse fragments, 3.0 oz/sy minimum fabric weight).\n    65→\n    66→**Blocks 4, 14, 16: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n    67→*   Contains the Engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), standard release of application disclaimers, utility verification (Texas 811) notes, and an ECM compliance note.\n    68→*   READ briefly to confirm professional engineer certification and standard municipal compliance language.\n    69→\n    70→---\n    71→\n    72→- `block-1.md` — diagram: This section provides diagrams and notes for biofiltration bed construction, focusing on underdrain \n    73→\n    74→All other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n    75→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15b83afc-0aa4-4376-b427-846989125e55","uuid":"2d1c31b8-407c-4515-96b1-ec6e7d919c12","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-15/guide.md","content":"# Sheet 15 — Biofiltration-Detention Pond Plan & Calculations\n\nBiofiltration and detention pond plan, details, and engineering calculations.\n\n## Reading Guide\n\nThis sheet provides the detailed site plan, schematic cross-sections, and comprehensive engineering calculations for a partial biofiltration and onsite detention pond associated with Unit 501. Its primary purpose is to outline the spatial layout, stage-storage volumes, flow rates, and material specifications required to ensure proper stormwater management and water quality control for the development.\n\n**Key values:**\n*   Drainage Area to Control: 1.48 ac (86.09% Impervious Cover, 1.27 ac)\n*   Water Quality Volume (WQV): 6,237 cf required, 6,388 cf provided\n*   Water Quality Elevation Provided: 675.65 ft msl\n*   Top of Biofiltration Media Elevation: 672.00 ft\n*   Maximum Ponding Depth above Sand Bed: 3.65 ft\n*   Filtration Pond Area Provided: 831 sf\n*   Peak Flow Rates (Q100): 18.83 cfs (Entrance), 15.60 cfs (Outfall)\n*   D50 Rock Rip-Rap: 0.25 in (Entrance), 0.39 in (Outfall)\n*   Required Orifice Diameter: 1.39 in (Area: 0.011 sq. ft.)\n\n**Content blocks on this sheet:**\n\n**Block 3: drawing — Unit 501 Site Plan Drawing**\n*   Contains the core layout of the biofiltration-detention pond, retaining walls, rip-rap placement, and routing of drainage infrastructure.\n*   Use VISION to examine the spatial layout and relationships between pond elements. READ for specific spot elevations (e.g., Top of Biofiltration Media at 672.00) and pipe specifications (e.g., 24\" HDPE @ 1.35%).\n\n**Block 2: table — Appendix R-6 Partial Biofiltration Pond Calculations**\n*   Contains the primary water quality control and biological element calculations for the pond system.\n*   READ the block content for critical drainage area parameters, water quality volume verifications, and exact minimum planting requirements.\n\n**Block 1: diagram — Biofiltration Bed Underdrain Details**\n*   Contains cross-sectional diagrams of the biofiltration underdrain pipe and medium bed profile.\n*   Use VISION to examine the layering and spatial arrangement of the filter system. READ for specific material depths (e.g., 18\" min sand, 3\"-6\" gravel) and pipe perforation layout specifications.\n\n**Block 15: text_block — Partial Sedimentation/Biofiltration Pond Schematic**\n*   Contains a cross-sectional illustration showing the general flow from the flow spreader through the sedimentation chamber to the filtration basin.\n*   Use VISION to examine the conceptual layout and components of the dual-chamber pond system.\n\n**Block 6: table — Water Quality Pond Water Surface Elevations**\n*   Contains a stage-storage table showing depths, areas, and cumulative storage for the entire water quality pond.\n*   READ the block content for exact storage volumes at given elevations and to verify the required vs. provided WQV elevations (675.57 vs 675.65).\n\n**Block 11: table — Onsite Detention Pond Tables**\n*   Contains stage vs. storage capacities and the outflow summary for 2-year, 10-year, 25-year, and 100-year storm events.\n*   READ the block content for peak outflow rates (up to 15.60 cfs) at specific event elevations.\n\n**Block 5: table — Discharge Summary**\n*   Contains existing and proposed stormwater flow rates (cfs) across various analysis points based on Atlas 14 data.\n*   READ the block content to compare pre- and post-development flow rates for different storm intervals.\n\n**Blocks 7 & 10: table — Sedimentation and Filtration Pond Stage-Storage**\n*   Contains individual stage, area, and storage breakdowns for the sedimentation pond and the filtration pond.\n*   READ the block content for specific area and volume metrics at 1-foot increments between 672.00 and 677.50 ft msl.\n\n**Block 12: table — Entrance and Outfall Sizing Calculations**\n*   Contains velocity and D50 rock rip-rap sizing calculations for the 100-year storm event.\n*   READ the block content to verify entrance (1.39 fps) and exit (1.73 fps) velocities and required rip-rap diameters.\n\n**Block 13: table — Falling Head Orifice Calculator**\n*   Contains the variables and calculated results for sizing the detention pond orifice.\n*   READ the block content for the orifice sizing parameters, specifically the final required diameter of 1.39 inches, and the governing equation.\n\n**Blocks 8 & 9: table — Material Specifications (Filter Fabric & Biofiltration Medium)**\n*   Contains the required physical characteristics, composition percentages, and testing methods for the high flow filter fabric and biofiltration soil medium.\n*   READ the block content for strict material acceptance criteria (e.g., 70-90% sand + coarse fragments, 3.0 oz/sy minimum fabric weight).\n\n**Blocks 4, 14, 16: various — Boilerplate & Administrative**\n*   Contains the Engineer's seal (Jason K. Rodgers), standard release of application disclaimers, utility verification (Texas 811) notes, and an ECM compliance note.\n*   READ briefly to confirm professional engineer certification and standard municipal compliance language.\n\n---\n\n- `block-1.md` — diagram: This section provides diagrams and notes for biofiltration bed construction, focusing on underdrain \n\nAll other block detail in `./blocks.md`\n","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":75}}}
