nces required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n> 5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\"\n\n### 3. Additional Safety & Utility Mentions\n*   **General Construction Notes (Item 2):** Requires coordinating with the state utility dig line prior to commencement: \n    > \"Contractor shall call Texas 811 (811 or 1-800-344-8377) for utility locations prior to any work in City easements or street R.O.W.\"\n*   **Bottom Center Logo:** A prominent logo for **\"Texas 811\"** is displayed with the text: \n    > \"Call Before You Dig!!\"\n\n### Summary of Key Quantitative Clearances/Restrictions Noted:\n*   **25 Feet:** Minimum clearance from the base of transmission structures/poles for planting trees (Note 1), digging/grading (Notes 2 & 15), staging areas/dumpsters/aerial equipment (Note 7), and sprinklers (Note 14).\n*   **20 Feet:** OSHA requirement for clearance from energized transmission lines during construction (Note 3); minimum distance for fire hydrants from any transmission structure (Note 13).\n*   **10 Feet:** Distance at which barricades must be erected from electric transmission structures during construction (Note 4); radius of responsibility for initial tree pruning/removal around the centerline of proposed overhead electric facilities (Electric Note 3).\n*   **46,000 lbs:** The structural support capacity required for roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement (Note 12).","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1422,"output