 electric lines.\n    23→\n    24→## Block 3: notes — Two disclaimer statements regarding the verification of data, utility locations, and the responsibilities of the applicant, engineer, and contractor.\n    25→\n    26→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    27→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n    28→\n    29→## Block 4: notes — This is a legend explaining the symbols used on the site plan to represent different types of trees and their status (e.g., to remain, to be removed, protected, heritage).\n    30→\n    31→Tree Legend\n    32→[circle with '3268' and 'o' inside] Trees to Remain\n    33→[circle with '3267H' and 'o' inside] Heritage Trees\n    34→[circle with '3266P' and 'o' inside] Protected Trees\n    35→[circle with '3269H' and 'x' inside] Heritage Trees to be Removed\n    36→[circle with '3265' and 'x' inside] Trees to be Removed\n    37→\n    38→## Block 5: notes — A list of two benchmarks with their descriptions and elevations.\n    39→\n    40→Benchmarks\n    41→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    42→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    43→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    44→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    45→\n    46→## Block 6: notes — A list of notes detailing the requirements for protecting trees during demolition, including fencing, trunk armor, and pruning procedures.\n    47→\n    48→Tree Protection Notes\n    49→- Tree protection fencing is required for all trees within the limits of destruction on site BEFORE demolition occurs. Where fencing cannot be placed to protect the extent of the CRZ with natural ground cover, provide an 8\" layer of organic hardwood mulch outside of the fencing.\n    50→- Trunk armor to be added to avoid mechanical impacts. Add the following note to the Existing Conditions/Demolition Plan: strapping 2x4 or thicker lumber (to match height of building) securely around tree trunk, buttress roots, and root flare, is required if fencing cannot go around the entire Half CRZ.\n    51→- Where demolishing existing structures, removing utilities, and/or removing flatwork within the CRZs of trees 8\" or greater in diameter, please add call-outs specifying the use of only hand-tools.\n    52→- Add the following note to the Existing Conditions/Demolition Plan: If pruning is necessary during demolition, it should take place prior to the start of the demolition process. It must be performed by a qualified arborist and no more than 25% is permitted.\n    53→Reference: ECM 3.6.1\n    54→\n    55→## Block 7: notes — The legal description of the property.\n    56→\n    57→Legal Description\n    58→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    59→\n    60→## Block 9: notes — A list of ten notes detailing the requirements and procedures for site demolition, including tree protection, utility abandonment, and material removal.\n    61→\n    62→Site Demolition Notes\n    63→1. Install all tree protection fencing as per sheet 5.\n    64→2. All demolition within the full critical root zone of preserved trees to be performed with hand tools only.\n    65→3. A pre-construction meeting with the Environmental Inspector is required prior to any site disturbance.\n    66→4. Contractor is responsible for locating and abandoning existing private water and wastewater lines. All abandoned piping is to be plugged.\n    67→5. Existing trees on the site are to remain unless noted otherwise.\n    68→6. Contractor is to remove all demolished materials from site, and to repair or replace any materials or items designated to remain.\n    69→7. Demolition work should be completed prior to any commencement of work on the proposed building.\n    70→8. Contractor to remove gas & water meters and return meters to respective utility companies.\n    71→9. Contractor to use \"Sidewalk Closed\" signs during sidewalk closures.\n    72→10. Notify contracting officer and arborist before commencement of work which may jeopardize the well-being of any trees to remain.\n    73→\n    74→## Block 10: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for the complete tree list.\n    75→\n    76→Tree List\n    77→Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n    78→\n    79→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24aa3565-6e12-4421-aab9-b461d3b546f4","uuid":"67917715-8c0e-4af6-af4f-b8ce397f622b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/wnavey/code/controlroom/conductor/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-08/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 8 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — A detail drawing of a water intersection, including existing and proposed valves, lines, and a fire hydrant.\n\nThe detail drawing shows a water intersection with the following key elements:\n- A 6\" DI Water Line.\n- An existing valve and tee to be removed.\n- A 6\" gate valve to be added.\n- An existing 6\" DI water line to be removed.\n- An existing fire hydrant to be removed and replaced with a new fire hydrant.\n- Various dimensions and elevations.\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This is a detailed site plan for a townhome development, showing existing conditions, utilities, and items to be removed or preserved. It includes callouts for various demolition activities such as removing concrete sidewalks, curbs, gutters, retaining walls, and utility structures.\n\nThe site plan includes the following key elements:\n- A large area of asphalt and concrete paving to be demolished, indicated by dashed lines and callouts such as \"Demo All Concrete Sidewalks\" and \"Demo Asphalt.\"\n- Existing utilities including water lines, wastewater lines, gas meters, and electric lines.\n- Trees to remain or be removed, indicated by specific symbols.\n- Retaining walls to be removed or preserved, with callouts such as \"Demo 79 Lf Concrete Retaining Wall\" and \"Existing Retaining Wall To Remain.\"\n- Various easements including drainage, electric, and sidewalk easements.\n- Neighboring properties and zoning information.\n- Notes instructing coordination with the City of Austin for the removal of power poles and electric lines.\n\n## Block 3: notes — Two disclaimer statements regarding the verification of data, utility locations, and the responsibilities of the applicant, engineer, and contractor.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning construction.\n\n## Block 4: notes — This is a legend explaining the symbols used on the site plan to represent different types of trees and their status (e.g., to remain, to be removed, protected, heritage).\n\nTree Legend\n[circle with '3268' and 'o' inside] Trees to Remain\n[circle with '3267H' and 'o' inside] Heritage Trees\n[circle with '3266P' and 'o' inside] Protected Trees\n[circle with '3269H' and 'x' inside] Heritage Trees to be Removed\n[circle with '3265' and 'x' inside] Trees to be Removed\n\n## Block 5: notes — A list of two benchmarks with their descriptions and elevations.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 6: notes — A list of notes detailing the requirements for protecting trees during demolition, including fencing, trunk armor, and pruning procedures.\n\nTree Protection Notes\n- Tree protection fencing is required for all trees within the limits of destruction on site BEFORE demolition occurs. Where fencing cannot be placed to protect the extent of the CRZ with natural ground cover, provide an 8\" layer of organic hardwood mulch outside of the fencing.\n- Trunk armor to be added to avoid mechanical impacts. Add the following note to the Existing Conditions/Demolition Plan: strapping 2x4 or thicker lumber (to match height of building) securely around tree trunk, buttress roots, and root flare, is required if fencing cannot go around the entire Half CRZ.\n- Where demolishing existing structures, removing utilities, and/or removing flatwork within the CRZs of trees 8\" or greater in diameter, please add call-outs specifying the use of only hand-tools.\n- Add the following note to the Existing Conditions/Demolition Plan: If pruning is necessary during demolition, it should take place prior to the start of the demolition process. It must be performed by a qualified arborist and no more than 25% is permitted.\nReference: ECM 3.6.1\n\n## Block 7: notes — The legal description of the property.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 9: notes — A list of ten notes detailing the requirements and procedures for site demolition, including tree protection, utility abandonment, and material removal.\n\nSite Demolition Notes\n1. Install all tree protection fencing as per sheet 5.\n2. All demolition within the full critical root zone of preserved trees to be performed with hand tools only.\n3. A pre-construction meeting with the Environmental Inspector is required prior to any site disturbance.\n4. Contractor is responsible for locating and abandoning existing private water and wastewater lines. All abandoned piping is to be plugged.\n5. Existing trees on the site are to remain unless noted otherwise.\n6. Contractor is to remove all demolished materials from site, and to repair or replace any materials or items designated to remain.\n7. Demolition work should be completed prior to any commencement of work on the proposed building.\n8. Contractor to remove gas & water meters and return meters to respective utility companies.\n9. Contractor to use \"Sidewalk Closed\" signs during sidewalk closures.\n10. Notify contracting officer and arborist before commencement of work which may jeopardize the well-being of any trees to remain.\n\n## Block 10: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for the complete tree list.\n\nTree List\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n","numLines":79,"startLine":1,"totalLines":79}}}
