INING WALLS AND MAINTENANCE ACCESS\n*   **Retaining Walls:** Yes. The perimeter of both the sedimentation and biofiltration basins is enclosed by walls explicitly labeled multiple times as `\"Concrete Structural Retaining Wall w/ Fall Protection Railing (By Others)\"`.\n*   **Maintenance Paths or Ramps:** Yes, located on the west (left) side of the pond structure.\n    *   `\"8' Wide Concrete Maintenance Path\"` approaches from the south.\n    *   Access is controlled by a `\"Pond Maintenance Gate 2 4' Swing Gates\"`.\n    *   Inside the gate is an `\"8' Wide Maintenance Ramp 4:1 Max Slope\"` leading down to an `\"11'\"` `\"Ramp Landing\"` inside the sedimentation basin.\n*   **Visible Clearances:** The inflow pipe (`\"63.84 ft 24\" HDPE\"`) passes underneath the maintenance path area before penetrating the retaining wall. The retaining walls form the hard structural boundary between the internal pond utilities and the external maintenance paths.\n\n### 5. CONFLICTS OR CLEARANCE ISSUES\nBased strictly on the 2D visual layout provided on Sheet 15:\n*   There are no apparent conflicts annotated between utilities and pond structures. The underdrain utilities are specifically designed to fit within the retaining wall boundaries of the pond.\n*   The 24\" HDPE inflow utility passes directly beneath the maintenance access path area, which is a standard routing practice and does not visually indicate a conflict on this plan sheet.\n*   There are no explicit notes or \"clouded\" revision areas on this specific sheet indicating that existing external utilities had to be relocated or rerouted due to the placement of this pond.\n\n***\n\n### APPENDIX: Exact Table Transcriptions\nPer your instructions to reproduce tables exactly as they appear, character-by-character:\n\n**APPENDIX R-6 PARTIAL BIOFILTRATION POND CALCULATIONS**\n**DRAINAGE AREA DATA:**\n| | | |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Drainage Area to Control | 1.48 | ac |\n| Drainage Area Impervious Cover | 86.09 | % 1.27 ac