{"level":30,"time":1776952161743,"pid":35263,"hostname":"Williams-MacBook-Pro.local","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01EDMvbpaYRiPak9rWVxLMSp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Now I have a clear picture. Looking at Block 20 (Electric Transmission Notes), I can see the 16 notes include requirements for:\n- Pre-construction safety meeting 48 hours before (note 3)\n- Barricades 10 feet from transmission structures (note 4)\n- Warning signs under overhead facilities (note 6)\n- Staging areas not within 20 feet of transmission (note 7)\n\nHOWEVER, Block 20 also states in note 7 \"For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\"\n\nThis is slightly different from the grouping's UCM 1.14.9 requirement which says \"25 feet\" not \"20 feet.\"\n\nWait - let me recheck. Looking back at the earlier vision output from Sheet 3, it said:\n\"7. No ponds, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 25 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\"\n\nBut now Block 20 shows:\n\"7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\"\n\nThat's a discrepancy between what the vision tool reported and what Block 20 actually says. Let me go with what's in Block 20.md since that's the actual text.\n\nNow, let me check if there's any field verification language. Let me look for notes about contractor verification of utilities. I saw in Sheet 21 that the utility notes mention \"Contractor shall verify horizontal and vertical location of existing utilities prior to construction.\"\n\nLet me now compile my findings:\n\n## APPLICABILITY\n\nFrom facts.md:\n- Valley View Road is a LOCAL STREET (not a CTC or Urban Roadway)\n\n\n- The site sits within West Bouldin Creek Watershed and includes a biofiltration/detention pond. The plan sheets show OHE lines running along the west and south boundaries with power poles, but no transmission easement is depicted on the plat or site plan—only 5' Electric Easements and P.U.E. markings. No transmission structures appear on the site itself.\n\nSheet 3 contains 16 detailed Electric Transmission Notes, yet no corresponding easement or structures are graphically shown anywhere. This creates a significant discrepancy: either the transmission facilities exist but are missing from the drawings, or these notes shouldn't be included in the plans at all. Given the prominence and specificity of the notes, the transmission infrastructure likely exists but hasn't been properly depicted.\n\nFor the checklist evaluation, EL-1.1 doesn't apply since Valley View Road is classified as a local street, not a Core Transit Corridor or Urban Roadway, making the overhead utility zone requirement inapplicable.\n\nEL-1.2 through EL-1.4 present clearance verification issues. The overhead electric line visible on site boundaries requires a 7.5-ft horizontal clearance from distribution conductors and a 15-ft radial clearance from primary/neutral conductors, but neither clearance is dimensioned or documented on the electrical plan. The fire demand meter vault mentioned on the Utility Tap Plan may be positioned near overhead lines, yet no labeled vertical clearance dimension or AE approval letter appears in the submittals.\n\nEL-1.5 and EL-1.6 cannot be verified due to missing dimensional documentation. Clearance dimensions should be anchored to the conductor rather than pole centerline, and service entrance facilities must maintain 10-ft clearance from AE poles—Sheet 21 shows transformer pads and meters without any clearance callouts. EL-1.7 addresses service drop clearances over truck traffic, requiring an 18-ft minimum height, but this dimension is also not documented on the plans.\n\nFor EL-1.8, no Texas Gas Service lines appear on the site plan, making this requirement inapplicable. EL-1.9 involves underground utility crossings needing 12-inch clearance at perpendicular intersections, and while multiple underground systems are proposed (water, wastewater, electrical, storm drain), Sheet 20 shows the utility layout without clear separation distances marked.\n\nEL-1.10 addresses non-AE utilities sharing trenches with electrical conduit, which may apply given the adjacent utility routing, but no written agreements documenting these configurations are evident. EL-1.11 requires AULCC approval for utilities within 24 inches of existing ROW facilities, and utilities are being extended into the public right-of-way to connect with existing infrastructure.\n\nFor EL-1.12, the water tap crossing shown on Sheet 20 doesn't specify whether trenchless or open-cut methods will be used. EL-1.13 requires encasement where utilities cross beneath roads and structures—the utility tap passes under Valley View Road, yet no encasement details are provided.\n\nEL-1.14 and EL-1.15 both involve clearance requirements: wastewater cleanouts need 5-foot separation from utility poles (Sheet 26 shows the details but lacks horizontal clearance dimensions), and storm drain manholes similarly require 5-foot clearance from AE overhead electric poles (Sheet 12 shows the layout but no clearance dimensions are documented).\n\nEL-1.16 addresses non-electrical utilities running parallel within electrical or PUE easements. While the plat and site plan identify electric easements, the routing of water, wastewater, and storm utilities relative to these easement boundaries isn't clearly shown in the available sheets.\n\nEL-1.17 concerns electric utility poles positioned within water easements or near large transmission structures. Sheet 3 references transmission structures, but none appear on the site plans; the power poles shown aren't documented as being within water easements, and it's unclear whether transmission structures actually exist on the property or where they'd be located.\n\nEL-1.18 requires demolition plans to include specific transmission line safety measures—a pre-construction meeting at least 48 hours prior, 10-foot barricades from transmission structures, warning signage under overhead facilities, and restrictions on materials in the transmission easement. Sheet 8's demolition notes lack all of these required safety provisions, though this may not apply if transmission facilities don't actually exist on site.\n\nEL-1.19 calls for construction plans showing a 25-foot-wide access corridor with 16-foot vertical clearance along the transmission right-of-way. No such corridor is depicted or dimensioned on the available sheets, despite Sheet 3 referencing a transmission easement or ROW.\n\nEL-1.20 mandates a caution note in demolition plans regarding at-grade utility appurtenances. Sheet 8 demolition notes omit this specific requirement for contractors to exercise extreme caution near such utilities.\n\nEL-1.21 requires field verification of utility separations and clearances. Sheet 21 Block 6 includes a contractor verification requirement for existing utility locations prior to construction, though the language doesn't fully match the standard's specificity regarding \"all utility separations and clearances during construction and prior to final acceptance.\"\n\nFor EL-1.22, the site contains a biofiltration-detention pond with combined storage exceeding 5,000 cubic feet, potentially triggering the 14-foot minimum vertical clearance requirement for SCM staging areas. However, no staging area clearances appear on the examined sheets, requiring further investigation to determine if a staging area is mandated and whether clearances are documented. Similarly, EL-1.23 addresses maintenance access drive clearances—Sheet 15 or 16 should contain these details, but I need to verify if the 14-foot minimum is actually shown on the pond maintenance access specifications.\n\nFor EL-1.24, overhead electrical lines run along the property boundaries and construction access is planned, yet no vertical clearance dimensions are documented for access drives passing beneath these lines, making this requirement not verifiable as currently presented. EL-1.25 involves potential conflicts between proposed staging or temporary spoils areas and utility alignments or transmission easements; since no construction or staging plan details have been reviewed yet, I cannot assess whether spoils placement overlaps with utility corridors. The transmission requirements are noted but facilities aren't clearly delineated on the plans examined so far.\n\nFor EL-1.26, overhead electrical utilities exist on the site but no clearance envelope boundaries, 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground zones, 15-foot radial zones, or transmission clearance envelopes appear on any sheet—this is a clear failure. EL-1.27 also fails because no horizontal dashed height limit lines delineating clearance envelope boundaries are shown on the site plan or utility plan sheets. EL-1.28 applies given the sloped terrain present, yet no sloped or stepped height limit lines appear on the grading plan.\n\nNow I need to organize these findings into fail and not-verifiable categories for the final summary.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2519,"cache_read_input_tokens":68747,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2519},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a849ead6-fbef-4e89-ab5d-465127c74861","uuid":"7a96141a-70b9-41e4-89c9-6e40c953a656"}
